WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:07.849
The fabric of our world is littered with doorways
if you know where to look for them. Tears,

00:00:07.849 --> 00:00:14.230
portals, anomalies, all leading to places
and planes beyond human imagining and understanding,

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:21.470
and SCP-2317, otherwise known as A Door to
Another World, certainly fits that description.

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:26.420
Contained and kept at all times under the
watch of armed guards, SCP-2317 appears to

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:31.599
be a simple and unsuspecting wooden door in
its frame. It hardly looks like it requires

00:00:31.599 --> 00:00:37.070
such extreme round-the-clock security or needs
a strange, secretive ritual that the Foundation

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:43.570
performs, presumably to keep the door closed.
But, of course, sometimes the most interesting

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:48.940
thing about a closed doorway isn’t where
it leads, it’s what it keeps out.

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:54.800
Even by the Foundation’s already-high standards,
the requirements and regulations for personnel

00:00:54.800 --> 00:01:01.750
who are assigned to SCP-2317 seem oddly specific.
Psychological testing is standard practice

00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:06.570
for to work for the Foundation, but an additional
hurdle that anyone has to clear before even

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:11.280
getting to glimpse at this unassuming wooden
door is having a score of at least seventy-two

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:16.770
on the Milgram Obedience Examination. It is
also mandatory that personnel assigned to

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:22.909
maintaining it are both unmarried, with no
children or next-of-kin, as well as an unwavering,

00:01:22.909 --> 00:01:29.560
unquestioning loyalty to the Foundation, pure
devotion to its code and objectives. These

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:37.770
may seem like strange requirements, after
all, SCP-2317 is just a door…isn’t it?

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:41.799
Perhaps there’s a reason that the Foundation
keeps so much of the information about SCP-2317

00:01:41.799 --> 00:01:47.990
buried deep under layers upon layers of security,
with only the Overseer Council privy to the

00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:54.939
full details of its strange nature. Knowledge,
as they say, is power - but maybe knowing

00:01:54.939 --> 00:02:02.009
too much about whatever is behind that door
can prove deadly. Still, if SCP-2317 is a

00:02:02.009 --> 00:02:07.200
Door to Another World, an alternate dimension
or parallel reality, it must be safe enough

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:12.650
to visit. After all, the Foundation has been
sending personnel in there on a regular basis.

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:19.140
Daily, in fact. According to the O5 Council,
this is done as part of a procedure to maintain

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:25.940
Active Containment of…something lurking
beyond that old wooden doorframe. But what

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:29.750
could possibly warrant such constant maintenance
and surveillance?

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:35.160
In accordance with the Foundation’s guidelines,
all staff are required to rotate out of observing

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:41.130
SCP-2317 after every two months and spend
the following third month in full psychological

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:45.730
counselling before they are permitted to return
to the containment unit housing the Door to

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:51.430
Another World. It was after one of these month-long
periods of evaluation that a Foundation guard

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:56.090
was informed that his security clearance had
been raised to Level 3 and that he’d been

00:02:56.090 --> 00:03:02.640
selected for the duty of carrying out 220-Calabasas.
He knew the name instantly; this was the title

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:08.040
given to the daily containment procedure that
absolutely had to be carried out.

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:11.820
The guard didn’t question these orders,
after all, he’d been selected precisely

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:16.810
because of his loyalty to the Foundation.
He did make one request to his commanding

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:20.950
officer, however: he wanted to know what had
happened to the last guard that had performed

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:25.710
the procedure.
“Didn’t make it out of psychological evaluation,”

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:30.130
the officer replied.
Not letting this affect his dedication, the

00:03:30.130 --> 00:03:36.120
guard was told to prepare for Procedure 220-Calabasas.
Along with a fellow member of Foundation security

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:41.910
personnel, the guard was instructed to gather
everything on a strange list. The first was

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:47.900
a pre-selected member of Class-D Personnel,
specifically a convicted murderer. Class-D

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:52.910
refers to ‘Disposable Class’ personnel,
expendable individuals recruited by the Foundation

00:03:52.910 --> 00:03:59.470
for the sole purpose of testing SCPs.
Class-Ds were usually prison inmates repurposed

00:03:59.470 --> 00:04:06.490
for SCP testing, and the one chosen for 220-Calabasas
was no exception, serving multiple life sentences

00:04:06.490 --> 00:04:10.940
for murders, or at least that was what the
guard had been told. A Foundation personnel

00:04:10.940 --> 00:04:15.280
member instructed him to refer to the Class-D
solely as the ‘assistant’ from that point

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:18.579
on.
Next, the guard collected a live chicken,

00:04:18.579 --> 00:04:24.729
an obsidian-edged knife, a silver aspergillum
and aspersorium, to be filled with 500ccs

00:04:24.729 --> 00:04:30.590
of Holy Water that had been blessed by a priest
of the Abrahamic faith, and finally a one-kiloton

00:04:30.590 --> 00:04:36.100
nuclear device, which, according to instructions,
was to only be detonated in the unlikely event

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:42.370
of a catastrophic containment failure. In
other words, the last resort.

00:04:42.370 --> 00:04:46.681
After following his instructions to the letter
and without question, the guard and his colleague

00:04:46.681 --> 00:04:51.009
were briefed. The Foundation personnel member
informed them that he’d be joining and leading

00:04:51.009 --> 00:04:55.979
them in the procedure. The staff member also
specified that henceforth he be referred to

00:04:55.979 --> 00:05:01.169
as the ‘celebrant’ until the completion
of 220-Calabasas. The guard was acutely aware

00:05:01.169 --> 00:05:06.379
of how specific these instructions were, but
trusted in the Foundation, knowing that if

00:05:06.379 --> 00:05:10.710
they wanted this procedure performed a certain
way, then it was in everyones’ best interest

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:15.430
to carry out the orders to the letter. But
what the celebrant then went on to explain,

00:05:15.430 --> 00:05:23.849
raised far more questions about SCP-2317 and
the nature of Procedure 220-Calabasas. The

00:05:23.849 --> 00:05:28.289
Class-D joining them, wasn’t actually a
Class-D.

00:05:28.289 --> 00:05:33.469
The assistant, as they were now referred to,
was in reality another Foundation staff member,

00:05:33.469 --> 00:05:40.069
with a Level 4 security clearance, specifically
tailored to SCP-2317. Every member of staff

00:05:40.069 --> 00:05:46.370
entering through SCP-2317 and taking an active
role in 220-Calabasas needed to be informed

00:05:46.370 --> 00:05:51.760
that this assistant was not to be harmed or
treated as a member of Disposable Class. Fighting

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:57.140
back the nagging question of why the Foundation
would employ this subterfuge, the guard, along

00:05:57.140 --> 00:06:02.009
with his fellow security officer, the celebrant
and assistant, prepared for their departure

00:06:02.009 --> 00:06:07.370
through the Door to Another World at solar
noon, when the sun was highest over SCP-2317.

00:06:07.370 --> 00:06:15.340
Solar noon, chickens and holy water, this
all seemed like an oddly-occult combination

00:06:15.340 --> 00:06:19.270
for the Foundation.
As they entered the old wooden door, beyond

00:06:19.270 --> 00:06:25.159
lay a barren salt plane, stretching out for
kilometers in every direction. This alternate

00:06:25.159 --> 00:06:31.650
dimension, according to the briefing, was
designated SCP-2317-Prime. The guard immediately

00:06:31.650 --> 00:06:36.479
noticed a ring of seven pillars directly ahead
of the group as they entered, each of them

00:06:36.479 --> 00:06:42.969
bearing intricately-detailed engravings unlike
anything from any era of ancient history.

00:06:42.969 --> 00:06:48.400
Procedure 220-Calabasas was carried out quickly,
but carefully, the guard watching as the celebrant

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:53.569
and assistant were careful not to miss a step.
First, the celebrant scattered Holy Water

00:06:53.569 --> 00:06:58.539
into the center of the pillars with the aspergillum
and aspersorium, looking down at his feet

00:06:58.539 --> 00:07:04.319
and keeping a steady pace as he stepped counter-clockwise
around them. The guard watched intently as

00:07:04.319 --> 00:07:09.449
the celebrant completed his circuit around
the pillars and turned to the assistant, anointing

00:07:09.449 --> 00:07:15.580
his head with Holy Water.
“Seven Seals, Seven Rings, Seven Thrones

00:07:15.580 --> 00:07:21.189
for the Scarlet King,” he said aloud.
The assistant, with the obsidian blade in

00:07:21.189 --> 00:07:26.120
his hand, took the chicken and dispatched
it in sacrifice, letting its blood mix with

00:07:26.120 --> 00:07:30.960
the Holy Water. He then repeated the celebrant’s
circuit in the opposite direction, before

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:36.919
stepping into the center of the stone pillars.
“Blood for the Old Gods, Water for the New

00:07:36.919 --> 00:07:41.550
King,” the assistant recited, pouring the
remaining mix of blood and Holy Water over

00:07:41.550 --> 00:07:45.569
a patch of salt in the middle of the seven
pillars.

00:07:45.569 --> 00:07:50.180
Even though he knew it wasn’t his place
to question the Foundation, as the 220-Calabasas

00:07:50.180 --> 00:07:56.219
procedure took place, the guard couldn’t
help but wonder what all this was for. It

00:07:56.219 --> 00:08:02.650
seemed so…ritualistic, like something deeply
religious or even magical. He’d never bought

00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:07.840
into all that occult mumbo-jumbo, even while
working for the Foundation, but he had learned

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:14.059
not to question anything, even the strangest
and most inexplicable of sights.

00:08:14.059 --> 00:08:20.889
Little did he know that beneath his feet,
was an ancient and unknowable horror. A beast,

00:08:20.889 --> 00:08:26.059
chained and lying in wait. Contained in a
chamber directly underneath the pillars, sat

00:08:26.059 --> 00:08:31.839
an impossibly-large creature. Humanoid and
obese, its body covered entirely in scales

00:08:31.839 --> 00:08:37.070
thicker than armour plating. Branch-like horns
protruded from its jawless head, pointing

00:08:37.070 --> 00:08:43.680
up to chains that hung from the seven pillars
above, each one hooked into the entity’s

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:49.490
back. All but one of the chains was broken,
a final, withering shackle keeping the Devourer

00:08:49.490 --> 00:08:56.680
of Worlds in its underground prison.
Ever since 1894 BCE, when Erikeshan mystics

00:08:56.680 --> 00:09:02.440
imprisoned it, the Devourer has been waiting
patiently for its inevitable freedom. It knows,

00:09:02.440 --> 00:09:06.850
as well as the Foundation, that nothing can
be done to prevent the final chain from one

00:09:06.850 --> 00:09:13.220
day breaking. Even Procedure 220-Calabasas
won’t keep the creature contained, it’s

00:09:13.220 --> 00:09:19.320
nothing more than a smokescreen, an act, designed
to create an illusion of active containment

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:28.410
and maintain Foundation morale until a permanent
solution can be devised to keep SCP-2317 imprisoned.

00:09:28.410 --> 00:09:33.310
Of course, if the guard had known this, it
would have also explained the need for a one-kiloton

00:09:33.310 --> 00:09:39.510
nuclear device as part of this staged ritual.
Procedure 220-Calabasas has all the components

00:09:39.510 --> 00:09:45.310
to trick everyone below the O5 Council, emulating
religious and occult rituals, the increased

00:09:45.310 --> 00:09:50.399
level of security surrounding the procedure
and its purpose, and telling staff that any

00:09:50.399 --> 00:09:56.800
failure to correctly, and completely, perform
the 220-Calabasas procedure will result in

00:09:56.800 --> 00:10:03.450
an XK-Class End of the World Scenario. All
these elements work together to conceal the

00:10:03.450 --> 00:10:10.399
truth: that one day, the Devourer will escape
and lay waste to our dimension.

00:10:10.399 --> 00:10:16.450
Knowledge is power, and maybe knowing too
much truly is deadly. Perhaps if the guard

00:10:16.450 --> 00:10:21.279
had learned any of this, he’d have understood
why his predecessor never made it out of psychological

00:10:21.279 --> 00:10:26.589
evaluation. Maybe if he had questioned the
purpose of Procedure 220-Calabasas, he'd have

00:10:26.589 --> 00:10:34.519
learned the true nature of SCP-2317, and what
that doorway kept out. But he was loyal to

00:10:34.519 --> 00:10:39.959
the Foundation, through and through. As the
team finished performing 220-Calabasas and

00:10:39.959 --> 00:10:44.600
returned though the wooden door, the guard
took one last glance over his shoulder at

00:10:44.600 --> 00:10:52.810
the vast salt plane. The entire dimension
was calm, silent, but not peaceful: it was

00:10:52.810 --> 00:10:57.940
patient. The entity had waited centuries for
its time, and now all it would take was the

00:10:57.940 --> 00:11:02.870
breaking of the seventh and final chain. One
day.

00:11:02.870 --> 00:11:08.230
The door was closed behind the guard as he,
the celebrant, the assistant and his fellow

00:11:08.230 --> 00:11:14.800
security officer stepped back through, their
work done and, as far as they knew, preventing

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:20.089
catastrophe for another day.
Only the Foundation higher-ups, the Overseer

00:11:20.089 --> 00:11:27.980
Council, are aware of the true danger posed
by SCP-2317 and its sole inhabitant. Current

00:11:27.980 --> 00:11:32.449
predictions are that at some point within
the next thirty years, the Devourer of Worlds

00:11:32.449 --> 00:11:37.959
will be freed. Any and all attempts to repair
or recreate the chains holding it in place

00:11:37.959 --> 00:11:44.250
have so far failed. As such, the O5 Council
has elected to continue providing Foundation

00:11:44.250 --> 00:11:51.300
personnel with the ignorant hope that Procedure
220-Calabasas is an effective strategy for

00:11:51.300 --> 00:11:55.060
containment.
As we’ve said, sometimes the most interesting

00:11:55.060 --> 00:12:00.400
thing about a closed door isn’t where it
leads, it’s what it keeps out. In the case

00:12:00.400 --> 00:12:07.329
of SCP-2317, the unassuming wooden door holds
at bay an ancient creature of untold power,

00:12:07.329 --> 00:12:14.080
that will one day break free and wreak havoc
in our dimension. Nothing the Foundation does

00:12:14.080 --> 00:12:19.860
can prevent it, or keep it contained behind
the Door to Another World, and only the Overseer

00:12:19.860 --> 00:12:26.899
Council knows that any and all efforts to
do so are futile. With all that in mind, we

00:12:26.899 --> 00:12:33.639
can only hope that the doorway of SCP-2317
stays closed, at least for a little while

00:12:33.639 --> 00:12:39.670
longer.
A veteran worker of the SCP Foundation sits

00:12:39.670 --> 00:12:44.470
at his terminal, performing one of the most
critical tasks in the entire organization

00:12:44.470 --> 00:12:51.230
- creating a file for an as yet undescribed
SCP. But there’s something terribly wrong.

00:12:51.230 --> 00:12:57.740
His eyes are glazed over, his mouth hangs
open - is this a zombie or a trained foundation

00:12:57.740 --> 00:13:00.360
researcher? What is going on?

00:13:00.360 --> 00:13:04.880
Like any large international organization,
it takes more than just the exciting, action

00:13:04.880 --> 00:13:10.779
filled jobs to keep the wheels turning at
the SCP Foundation. Sure, the head researchers,

00:13:10.779 --> 00:13:15.500
guards, mobile task force soldiers, and members
of the O5 Command get all the praise, but

00:13:15.500 --> 00:13:21.300
a legion of number crunchers, cleaners, and
paper pushers are equally important. One such

00:13:21.300 --> 00:13:26.399
person was archivist Walter Bainbridge, who
had been tasked with digitizing some of the

00:13:26.399 --> 00:13:29.180
older records that the Foundation had on file.

00:13:29.180 --> 00:13:36.779
It was when he was innocently recording the
details on SCP - 050 through 060 that he first

00:13:36.779 --> 00:13:44.199
came under the strange and startling effects
of SCP - 055. But, the most peculiar part,

00:13:44.199 --> 00:13:50.730
as with all incidences of SCP - 055’s anomalous
effects taking hold, is that Walter had no

00:13:50.730 --> 00:13:53.519
idea any of it was happening.

00:13:53.519 --> 00:14:00.050
In his new digitized filing system, he first
took note of SCP - 053. Euclid Class, also

00:14:00.050 --> 00:14:05.379
known as the Young Girl, this anomaly was
a seemingly normal human female child who

00:14:05.379 --> 00:14:10.819
provoked homicidal insanity in those directly
exposed to her. Then SCP - 054. Safe Class,

00:14:10.819 --> 00:14:18.810
a non-aggressive humanoid female made entirely
of, as well as biologically and chemically

00:14:18.810 --> 00:14:26.410
identical to regular spring water. Next, SCP
- 056. Euclid Class, a being that changes

00:14:26.410 --> 00:14:33.829
form to suit its environment, but only when
all observers lose focus on it. And then SCP

00:14:33.829 --> 00:14:38.990
- 057. Safe Class, an underground chamber
that crushes the humans who walk within.

00:14:38.990 --> 00:14:43.760
It was at this point that Walter received
a concerned message from one of his superiors

00:14:43.760 --> 00:14:49.910
at Site-19, Mr. Kovach. The message praised
the thorough digitization of the other anomalies’

00:14:49.910 --> 00:14:56.389
records, but was confused about why Walter
had left out any mention of SCP - 055. Immediately,

00:14:56.389 --> 00:15:02.889
Walter was embarrassed. How could he have
forgotten SCP - 055? That iconic anomaly known

00:15:02.889 --> 00:15:07.920
for...Well, he couldn’t quite say, off the
top of his head, but he’d be sure to look

00:15:07.920 --> 00:15:12.480
into it. A quick trip to the Site 19 Archives
showed him that there was actually quite a

00:15:12.480 --> 00:15:18.610
hefty file on the nature of SCP - 055, which
must have been the result of a huge number

00:15:18.610 --> 00:15:19.630
of studies.

00:15:19.630 --> 00:15:24.240
What struck him as strange was that all the
files were filled out in pen rather than being

00:15:24.240 --> 00:15:28.889
typed up, like a traditional file. The majority
of these notes were written in short-hand,

00:15:28.889 --> 00:15:34.180
too, as though they were frantically taken
during the tests themselves on extremely short

00:15:34.180 --> 00:15:37.760
notice. There weren’t even any redactions.

00:15:37.760 --> 00:15:42.180
Walter made a mental note of what he had seen,
put the file back in its proper place, and

00:15:42.180 --> 00:15:47.149
headed back to his computer terminal. However,
after writing in an almost trancelike state,

00:15:47.149 --> 00:15:53.529
he looked back on his work to see that he
had written an entry on SCP - 058, a giant,

00:15:53.529 --> 00:15:59.190
evil bovine heart with insect legs and a scorpion
stinger… Strange, he thought.

00:15:59.190 --> 00:16:04.779
That’s when Walter got a call from Mr. Kovach
on his Foundation-issue phone, and he didn’t

00:16:04.779 --> 00:16:10.920
sound happy. He’d given Walter direct instructions
to go back and digitize the files on 055,

00:16:10.920 --> 00:16:16.170
and instead, he’d been working on 058. What
was the meaning of this? Walter was typically

00:16:16.170 --> 00:16:20.759
an extremely loyal and diligent employee,
but the verbal barrage from his supervisor

00:16:20.759 --> 00:16:25.839
had him considering talking back, just this
once, and hoping it didn’t get him demoted

00:16:25.839 --> 00:16:30.480
to D-Class and thrown into 682’s acid bath
for play time.

00:16:30.480 --> 00:16:34.930
Walter gulped, picked up some courage, and
interrupted Mr. Kovach’s rant to ask if

00:16:34.930 --> 00:16:41.840
he had any idea what SCP - 055 actually was.
The line went silent for a moment, then the

00:16:41.840 --> 00:16:46.439
supervisor spoke again, this time with less
confidence.

00:16:46.439 --> 00:16:53.260
“Of course I can tell you about SCP - 055.
It’s a classic, one of the first hundred.

00:16:53.260 --> 00:17:02.120
How could you forget its...Or, um, you know,
its...I think it’s the one with the...”

00:17:02.120 --> 00:17:07.230
Another long pause, as Mr. Kovach seemed to
search for the words, but instead just trailed

00:17:07.230 --> 00:17:12.900
off into silence. Knowing that some of the
anomalies on file were dangerous memetic hazards,

00:17:12.900 --> 00:17:16.699
Walter was worried for a moment that he may
have accidentally killed his boss by getting

00:17:16.699 --> 00:17:23.690
him to think too hard about SCP - 055. He
asked if Mr. Kovach was okay, and finally

00:17:23.690 --> 00:17:24.690
got a reply.

00:17:24.690 --> 00:17:29.340
“I’m sorry, I seem to have zoned out for
a second there. What were we talking about,

00:17:29.340 --> 00:17:30.340
again?”

00:17:30.340 --> 00:17:34.970
But this time, it was Walter who couldn't
answer. He had no idea at all what the two

00:17:34.970 --> 00:17:40.309
of them were discussing just moments ago.
He felt disoriented and kind of sick, like

00:17:40.309 --> 00:17:45.230
they’d taken some low-level amnestics. Mr.
Kovach told Walter to get back to his filing

00:17:45.230 --> 00:17:49.240
duties and they’d speak later. Walter then
checked the messages he’d received from

00:17:49.240 --> 00:17:56.200
Mr. Kovach earlier, and there it was, plain
as day: “You missed 055. Go back and digitize

00:17:56.200 --> 00:17:59.040
that before proceeding. - Mr. K.”

00:17:59.040 --> 00:18:05.270
But Walter had never even heard of an SCP
- 055, if such an anomaly even existed! What

00:18:05.270 --> 00:18:06.780
was going on here?

00:18:06.780 --> 00:18:11.650
In that moment, Walter realized he was dealing
with something much stranger than just a standard

00:18:11.650 --> 00:18:18.270
digitization job. After all, how could he
properly complete his duties if SCP - 055

00:18:18.270 --> 00:18:23.390
seemed to be impossible to speak, write, or
even think about, unless you were directly

00:18:23.390 --> 00:18:28.640
observing it at that moment? Walter had to
know, and ask around the entirety of Site

00:18:28.640 --> 00:18:32.640
19 to find the answers if he had to.

00:18:32.640 --> 00:18:37.279
Sadly for Walter, he was about to embark on
a much more challenging task than he could

00:18:37.279 --> 00:18:42.640
have ever imagined. To paraphrase a supposed
quote from Socrates, “All I know is that

00:18:42.640 --> 00:18:48.099
I know nothing.” And that’s also about
the extent of the knowledge we have on SCP

00:18:48.099 --> 00:18:54.679
- 055, also known as The Anti-Meme and the
Self-Keeping Secret. What does it look like?

00:18:54.679 --> 00:18:59.750
When and how was it obtained by the Foundation?
What are its anomalous abilities? Is this

00:18:59.750 --> 00:19:01.150
thing dangerous?

00:19:01.150 --> 00:19:06.880
We may never know, because the only anomalous
ability of SCP - 055 that we’re aware of

00:19:06.880 --> 00:19:12.270
is the fact that nobody is capable of retaining
any information about it. It’s crucial to

00:19:12.270 --> 00:19:20.010
note that, whatever 055 is, it isn’t invisible,
or indescribable. Foundation personnel are

00:19:20.010 --> 00:19:24.590
perfectly capable of entering its containment
chamber and observing it without incident,

00:19:24.590 --> 00:19:30.051
but mere minutes after leaving the chamber,
any memories of the particulars of 055 seem

00:19:30.051 --> 00:19:35.790
to spontaneously erase themselves. Hence,
the Self-Keeping Secret.

00:19:35.790 --> 00:19:39.880
But this didn’t deter Walter. Perhaps his
greatest advantage was that he didn’t know

00:19:39.880 --> 00:19:45.190
enough about the thing he was investigating
to know how futile his mission was. He wanted

00:19:45.190 --> 00:19:50.230
to know the unknowable, and a pesky issue
like “impossibility” wouldn’t stop him.

00:19:50.230 --> 00:19:55.810
He’d get to whoever he needed to at Site
19 to get the answers he needed. Of course

00:19:55.810 --> 00:20:00.860
most people had no knowledge of the mysterious
anomaly. The common response he got back from

00:20:00.860 --> 00:20:05.020
his colleagues was, “055? Do we even have
an 055?”

00:20:05.020 --> 00:20:10.170
While the realization of sudden memory loss
- or the realization of 055’s existence

00:20:10.170 --> 00:20:15.880
- has been known to cause momentary stress,
there are no known long term physical or mental

00:20:15.880 --> 00:20:21.700
effects from 055’s anomalous abilities.
It’s a fleeting idea in its purest form

00:20:21.700 --> 00:20:27.780
- like forgetting why you walked into a room.
055 could be the most harmless object on the

00:20:27.780 --> 00:20:34.250
Foundation’s roster, or the most deadly.
Either way, we just don’t know.

00:20:34.250 --> 00:20:40.830
At times, Walter worried he was going insane.
055, and everything related to it, was gaslighting

00:20:40.830 --> 00:20:47.690
him. Was 055 even real? The one thing that
proved to him that 055 must have existed is

00:20:47.690 --> 00:20:53.480
that its containment chamber existed. According
to the official records kept by the Foundation

00:20:53.480 --> 00:20:59.730
on the Site 19 Containment Facilities, 055
is kept in a “five by five by two point

00:20:59.730 --> 00:21:05.750
five meter square room constructed of fifty-foot-thick
cement, with a Faraday cage surrounding the

00:21:05.750 --> 00:21:07.380
cement walls.”

00:21:07.380 --> 00:21:12.360
The report continues that, “Access is via
a heavy containment door measuring two by

00:21:12.360 --> 00:21:18.070
two point five meters constructed on bearings
to ensure door closes and locks automatically

00:21:18.070 --> 00:21:24.429
unless held open deliberately.” 055’s
cell is one of the few to have no posted security

00:21:24.429 --> 00:21:28.980
guards, and any personnel working on other
SCPs in the area are ordered to remain at

00:21:28.980 --> 00:21:34.970
least fifty feet from the geometric center
of 055’s cell, where the anomaly itself

00:21:34.970 --> 00:21:39.640
is kept. When he tried to explore further
why the cell was constructed in this manner,

00:21:39.640 --> 00:21:44.270
he found that - surprise, surprise - nobody
knew.

00:21:44.270 --> 00:21:49.990
055 was an anomaly whose containment requirements
were so mysterious that it automatically netted

00:21:49.990 --> 00:21:55.960
itself a Keter Class designation. After all,
how can you properly contain something you

00:21:55.960 --> 00:22:00.159
can’t even hope to comprehend? There were
plenty of rumors about the true nature of

00:22:00.159 --> 00:22:07.240
055. Some of the more conspiratorial minds
at Site 19 theorized that 055 was actually

00:22:07.240 --> 00:22:13.419
an autonomous or remotely controlled spy inserted
into the site to observe Foundation operations

00:22:13.419 --> 00:22:16.300
- or even humanity as a whole.

00:22:16.300 --> 00:22:20.700
If you’re on the more paranoid end of the
psychological spectrum, this theory makes

00:22:20.700 --> 00:22:25.460
total sense. An anomaly that’s physically
impossible to remember, even when writings

00:22:25.460 --> 00:22:31.350
and pictures on the subject exist, would be
a perfect spy. However, this was all ultimately

00:22:31.350 --> 00:22:36.690
little more than speculation. Walter was barely
any further along than when he started.

00:22:36.690 --> 00:22:42.040
There were multiple points in his investigation
where Walter seriously considered giving up,

00:22:42.040 --> 00:22:45.350
until, finally, he had a major breakthrough.

00:22:45.350 --> 00:22:50.830
Dr. Bartholomew Hughes and Dr. John Marachek
were two scientists that had performed extensive

00:22:50.830 --> 00:22:56.620
research into 055, and who - Walter hoped
- might have the answers he sought about the

00:22:56.620 --> 00:23:01.399
Self-Keeping Secret. These scientists were
the first to discover the “Anti-Memetic”

00:23:01.399 --> 00:23:06.789
nature of 055, performing numerous tests on
D-Class personnel to see if it was possible

00:23:06.789 --> 00:23:12.600
to create feasible written records, sketches,
or any other records or impressions that could

00:23:12.600 --> 00:23:15.159
bypass its anomalous effects.

00:23:15.159 --> 00:23:21.480
The disorienting, memory-ruining effects of
055 also extend to any materials concerning

00:23:21.480 --> 00:23:27.880
055. It seemed to be a truly uncrackable code,
but Dr. Hughes may have finally found some

00:23:27.880 --> 00:23:29.620
cracks in the armor.

00:23:29.620 --> 00:23:35.920
For starters, the fact we’re able to remember
that 055 is Anti-Memetic is an ironic exception

00:23:35.920 --> 00:23:42.070
to its Anti-Memetic qualities. This revelation
also inspired another realization from Dr.

00:23:42.070 --> 00:23:48.260
Hughes: Would it be possible to discover more
about 055 from the process of deduction rather

00:23:48.260 --> 00:23:54.580
than the typical induction? In other words,
could they possibly learn about 055 by figuring

00:23:54.580 --> 00:23:58.580
out all the things it isn’t rather than
what it is?

00:23:58.580 --> 00:24:03.760
Dr. Marachek designed an experiment with Dr.
Hughes to explore this theory. They designed

00:24:03.760 --> 00:24:10.090
the experiment around a simple question: Is
055 not spherical? In designing the question

00:24:10.090 --> 00:24:16.210
to specifically find out what 055 isn’t,
they hoped to subvert the anomaly’s Anti-Memetic

00:24:16.210 --> 00:24:17.210
Powers.

00:24:17.210 --> 00:24:22.130
Walter was fascinated by this potential method
of getting answers. Marachek and Hughes found

00:24:22.130 --> 00:24:27.730
that, while the questioning process for those
exposed was often arduous and frustrating,

00:24:27.730 --> 00:24:33.270
they could now definitely say that 055 is
not a sphere.

00:24:33.270 --> 00:24:38.910
It is theoretically possible to discover the
true nature of 055 by an almost endless barrage

00:24:38.910 --> 00:24:44.269
of deductive questions, though whether command
would authorize the resources for such extensive

00:24:44.269 --> 00:24:47.190
testing is still an open question.

00:24:47.190 --> 00:24:53.380
Walter, in his desperation, begged Marachek
and Hughes for clearance to view 055 himself.

00:24:53.380 --> 00:24:57.870
The curiosity had become too great during
his search to just walk away with the single

00:24:57.870 --> 00:25:04.120
fact that 055 wasn’t spherical. He needed
to see this thing. And after several weeks

00:25:04.120 --> 00:25:10.220
of filling out forms and cutting red tape,
his wish was finally granted. Walter Bainbridge

00:25:10.220 --> 00:25:18.130
was allowed a private audience with SCP - 055,
the subject of his months-long obsession.

00:25:18.130 --> 00:25:22.710
Outsiders observed that Walter spent just
over an hour in the containment chamber, taking

00:25:22.710 --> 00:25:28.300
photos, drawing sketches, writing down notes,
recording audio logs, and reciting memory

00:25:28.300 --> 00:25:33.159
mnemonics. He was pulling out every stop to
counteract the anti-memetic effects of the

00:25:33.159 --> 00:25:38.659
Self-Keeping Secret. He was adamant that he
would not be defeated by his non-spherical

00:25:38.659 --> 00:25:42.190
nemesis, not after all this time and effort.

00:25:42.190 --> 00:25:46.830
Once his time in the 055 containment chamber
was over, he retired back to his office to

00:25:46.830 --> 00:25:52.270
finally digitize his exhaustive findings,
so that his supervisor, Mr. Kovach, would

00:25:52.270 --> 00:25:58.340
finally get off his back. Walter smiled, took
a deep breath, and began to type.

00:25:58.340 --> 00:26:04.480
“SCP - 059. Keter Class. This anomaly is
a radioactive mineral that emits a unique

00:26:04.480 --> 00:26:09.269
radiation known as Delta Radiation. Exposure
to this radiation has caused strange fungal

00:26:09.269 --> 00:26:14.990
growths on the infected...” Wait, what was
this supposed to be about again? Oh well,

00:26:14.990 --> 00:26:19.700
it couldn’t have been that important.
The esteemed doctor Thomas Morstead entered

00:26:19.700 --> 00:26:21.289
the cell of the anomaly.

00:26:21.289 --> 00:26:26.820
He’d been warned and even chastised by his
colleagues, but who in the foundation could

00:26:26.820 --> 00:26:27.820
tell him what to do?

00:26:27.820 --> 00:26:34.549
He was the best at what he did, maybe the
greatest in the whole history of the foundation.

00:26:34.549 --> 00:26:41.260
As he entered the room, SCP-049 bid him welcome,
cordial as always, so polite in fact that

00:26:41.260 --> 00:26:44.200
you’d never guess you were talking to a
killer.

00:26:44.200 --> 00:26:48.419
Doctor Morstead knew the truth of what he
was dealing with, but he also believed he

00:26:48.419 --> 00:26:53.690
could get through to 049, calm him, exorcise
the devil from him.

00:26:53.690 --> 00:26:59.280
It was the meeting of two great minds, one
of them human, one of them part-human, part...

00:26:59.280 --> 00:27:01.179
something that has never been clear.

00:27:01.179 --> 00:27:07.340
It was to be a battle of wits, and like so
many great battles, this one would turn into

00:27:07.340 --> 00:27:08.870
a massacre.

00:27:08.870 --> 00:27:11.910
Before we get to that fateful meeting, there
are some things you should know about the

00:27:11.910 --> 00:27:13.299
anomaly known as SCP-049.

00:27:13.299 --> 00:27:19.280
If you saw him in the street the first thing
you’d think of is “plague”, because

00:27:19.280 --> 00:27:24.809
049 always looked the same - a man dressed
in black robes with a plague doctor’s mask.

00:27:24.809 --> 00:27:28.370
But this wasn’t a costume that could be
taken off.

00:27:28.370 --> 00:27:30.179
In fact it wasn’t a costume at all.

00:27:30.179 --> 00:27:36.380
It was him… the robes had grown out of him
like an exoskeleton, that horrible mask with

00:27:36.380 --> 00:27:42.549
the pointed nose wasn’t covering his face,
it was his face, a kind of shell that had

00:27:42.549 --> 00:27:44.250
seemingly sprouted from bone.

00:27:44.250 --> 00:27:46.750
The first reports came during World War Two.

00:27:46.750 --> 00:27:51.590
In a picturesque town in the south of France
called Montauban, people had begun going missing.

00:27:51.590 --> 00:27:55.654
Children disappeared from their beds in the
middle of the night and weren’t seen again;

00:27:55.654 --> 00:27:58.480
adults went to the market and never returned.

00:27:58.480 --> 00:28:03.279
Local authorities searched high and low; they
scoured nearby woods and dragged the rivers,

00:28:03.279 --> 00:28:04.860
but nothing was found.

00:28:04.860 --> 00:28:08.639
Because what was happening wasn’t criminal,
there was no clue they could stumble upon

00:28:08.639 --> 00:28:11.179
or eye witness who would break the case.

00:28:11.179 --> 00:28:15.529
No, this was something else, something that
the townsfolk could never understand.

00:28:15.529 --> 00:28:20.799
Word spread, and that’s when a search and
discovery team was sent from The Foundation.

00:28:20.799 --> 00:28:26.100
It was a cold, dark night in January of 1941
when the team found what they were looking

00:28:26.100 --> 00:28:27.100
for.

00:28:27.100 --> 00:28:31.080
They walked through the open door of a small
house located not too far from the grand Château

00:28:31.080 --> 00:28:34.830
de Richelieu, to find a masked man sitting
next to an open fire.

00:28:34.830 --> 00:28:36.149
And he wasn’t alone.

00:28:36.149 --> 00:28:38.830
The floor around him looked like it was moving.

00:28:38.830 --> 00:28:43.590
Upon closer inspection the team saw that the
floor was covered with writhing, grasping

00:28:43.590 --> 00:28:44.590
bodies.

00:28:44.590 --> 00:28:47.200
Its patients as it called them.

00:28:47.200 --> 00:28:52.149
“Bienvenue chez moi,” said the thing,
“Welcome to my home.”

00:28:52.149 --> 00:28:56.559
Those so-called patients crawled towards the
team, intent it seemed to cause harm.

00:28:56.559 --> 00:29:03.220
The hostiles, now known as SCP-049-2s, were
deemed dangerous and had to be eliminated.

00:29:03.220 --> 00:29:07.809
A sight, it seemed, that didn’t bother 049
in the slightest.

00:29:07.809 --> 00:29:12.070
It just sat there, occasionally looking up
from writing notes in a leather-bound book

00:29:12.070 --> 00:29:14.390
as his patients were gunned down.

00:29:14.390 --> 00:29:20.260
Once the carnage ended it simply closed its
book, stood up, and allowed itself to be escorted

00:29:20.260 --> 00:29:21.260
away.

00:29:21.260 --> 00:29:25.600
And that’s the story of how 049 ended up
at the facility, becoming a guest of sorts

00:29:25.600 --> 00:29:31.549
staying in a Standard Secure Humanoid Containment
Cell, Research Sector-02, Site-19.

00:29:31.549 --> 00:29:35.779
The few that came into contact with 049, remarked
that it was a pleasure for them.

00:29:35.779 --> 00:29:40.389
With its impeccable manners, vast knowledge
of medicine and human anatomy, sharp tongue

00:29:40.389 --> 00:29:41.980
and stinging wit.

00:29:41.980 --> 00:29:46.450
They almost became spellbound listening to
it, caught in the throes of its charms until,

00:29:46.450 --> 00:29:50.049
with the simple touch of its hand, it would
drain the life from them.

00:29:50.049 --> 00:29:53.880
That’s why SCP-049 was classified as a Euclid.

00:29:53.880 --> 00:29:56.919
That’s why armed guards were always stationed
outside its cell.

00:29:56.919 --> 00:29:59.980
It’s why doctors took great precautions
when in its presence.

00:29:59.980 --> 00:30:03.200
And it’s why Doctor Morstead should have
known better.

00:30:03.200 --> 00:30:08.470
Remember, when 049 was discovered in France
it willingly went with the team, like it was

00:30:08.470 --> 00:30:12.100
happy it had been found; as if it had planned
its own capture.

00:30:12.100 --> 00:30:16.120
When it arrived at the facility it didn’t
act like it was contained against its will,

00:30:16.120 --> 00:30:18.520
it was like it was returning home.

00:30:18.520 --> 00:30:22.460
Initial findings as to the biology of 049
were that it didn’t require any sustenance

00:30:22.460 --> 00:30:24.260
at all, not even water.

00:30:24.260 --> 00:30:26.870
It seemed content to be left alone with its
notebooks.

00:30:26.870 --> 00:30:30.790
It did not object when it was asked if it
could share some of its notes and gladly handed

00:30:30.790 --> 00:30:34.700
over its journals, but upon examination it
was discovered that they were written in a

00:30:34.700 --> 00:30:40.120
language that no linguist or cryptologist
has so far been able to translate.

00:30:40.120 --> 00:30:45.820
It’s apparent that 049 derives much satisfaction
from seeing so-called experts struggle over

00:30:45.820 --> 00:30:46.820
its text.

00:30:46.820 --> 00:30:52.309
Unable to read those notes, a long line of
doctors visited 049 in its cell, each fascinated

00:30:52.309 --> 00:30:53.309
by what they beheld.

00:30:53.309 --> 00:30:55.710
It was learned that it has traveled the globe.

00:30:55.710 --> 00:31:00.830
It speaks many languages, but prefers to speak
what it calls “le langage de l'amour”

00:31:00.830 --> 00:31:01.830
- French.

00:31:01.830 --> 00:31:05.500
It asked for only one thing, warm-blooded
animals.

00:31:05.500 --> 00:31:10.549
The facility agreed to supply 049 with various
kinds, including rabbits, cattle, and even

00:31:10.549 --> 00:31:12.389
an ape on one occasion.

00:31:12.389 --> 00:31:16.570
Just like with humans, it could kill the animals
with a mere touch of its hand, sucking the

00:31:16.570 --> 00:31:17.870
life right out of them.

00:31:17.870 --> 00:31:20.740
But that wasn’t even the most incredible
part.

00:31:20.740 --> 00:31:24.480
Soon those animals would rise again, as if
reanimated by 049.

00:31:24.480 --> 00:31:29.299
They would become, for all intents and purposes,
the living dead.

00:31:29.299 --> 00:31:31.700
And they were hostile.

00:31:31.700 --> 00:31:35.020
After several unfortunate incidents, they
were then taken from the cell the moment they

00:31:35.020 --> 00:31:37.980
arose and disposed of in the incinerator.

00:31:37.980 --> 00:31:42.620
This was not to the liking of 049, who would
claim it had “cured” the animals.

00:31:42.620 --> 00:31:47.250
For it, the world was sick…it saw plague
and pestilence everywhere and the meaning

00:31:47.250 --> 00:31:50.549
of its existence was to rid the world of disease.

00:31:50.549 --> 00:31:55.570
Humans, it said contained a virus, and had
to be cleansed.

00:31:55.570 --> 00:32:00.549
In the first days after arriving at the facility,
049 didn’t seem to pose a threat to humans.

00:32:00.549 --> 00:32:02.780
It was quite friendly in fact.

00:32:02.780 --> 00:32:07.139
It seemed aware of the fear it caused in staff
and would often go out its way to make them

00:32:07.139 --> 00:32:09.190
feel comfortable and safe.

00:32:09.190 --> 00:32:13.419
This was a ruse, of course, or a “canard”
as 049 liked to say.

00:32:13.419 --> 00:32:15.830
It had no intention to help humans.

00:32:15.830 --> 00:32:18.980
No, it had come for humans.

00:32:18.980 --> 00:32:22.659
It wasn’t trapped…it had set a trap.

00:32:22.659 --> 00:32:27.460
One of the first people to truly upset 049
was Dr. Raymond Hamm, a well-respected physician

00:32:27.460 --> 00:32:31.250
that had twice been a contender for the Nobel
Prize for his more mainstream work.

00:32:31.250 --> 00:32:36.120
What had confused Dr. Hamm the most was not
049’s clothes-like exoskeleton, or even

00:32:36.120 --> 00:32:39.539
his ability to reanimate the dead, but the
bag that it used.

00:32:39.539 --> 00:32:45.110
049 was somehow able to pull a seemingly endless
supply of surgical tools from that bag.

00:32:45.110 --> 00:32:48.409
Sometimes it would even pull out objects that
were somehow larger than the bag itself.

00:32:48.409 --> 00:32:52.860
It was as if the bag connected to somewhere
else, and that’s what Dr. Hamm wanted to

00:32:52.860 --> 00:32:54.720
talk about on that fateful day.

00:32:54.720 --> 00:32:59.820
With 049 on one side of the cell and Dr. Hamm
on the other, he asked, “How is it that

00:32:59.820 --> 00:33:04.299
you can produce a great quantity of tools
from that bag…I have observed you, and it

00:33:04.299 --> 00:33:06.409
seems to me, that you are doing the impossible.”

00:33:06.409 --> 00:33:11.010
“Dear doctor,” replied 049, “The Scourge!

00:33:11.010 --> 00:33:15.009
The Great Dying, cannot be fought with a handful
of toys.

00:33:15.009 --> 00:33:20.289
My bag is merely the product of my imagination,
it gives me what I require.

00:33:20.289 --> 00:33:25.480
You dear sir, it seems, are limited by your
imagination.”

00:33:25.480 --> 00:33:28.400
It stopped for a second or two and stared
at Dr. Hamm.

00:33:28.400 --> 00:33:33.029
“I detect you are unwell,” it said, in
a voice not as amiable as before.

00:33:33.029 --> 00:33:35.580
“It’s just a cold,” said the doctor.

00:33:35.580 --> 00:33:37.669
“Ah, just a cold?

00:33:37.669 --> 00:33:42.049
If you had seen what I have seen you would
not utter such insulting words.”

00:33:42.049 --> 00:33:47.210
Dr. Hamm pulled out some papers from a briefcase
and approached 049, holding them close enough

00:33:47.210 --> 00:33:48.730
so it could read them.

00:33:48.730 --> 00:33:52.269
“You see,” said Doctor Hamm pointing to
the results on the paper, “Those animals

00:33:52.269 --> 00:33:57.659
you say you cured, they were not diseased…they
were perfectly healthy before they died…and

00:33:57.659 --> 00:34:01.130
your so-called cure, it turned them into something
quite terrible.

00:34:01.130 --> 00:34:05.789
We found that if they were left alone, they
began to eat each other, and then themselves.”

00:34:05.789 --> 00:34:11.300
049 did not respond and after a brief pause
said only, “A good day to you doctor, please

00:34:11.300 --> 00:34:12.800
close the door on your way out.

00:34:12.800 --> 00:34:14.700
You should get some rest.”

00:34:14.700 --> 00:34:19.659
Hamm refused to go, and instead turned the
conversation to this real interest, the bag,

00:34:19.659 --> 00:34:23.610
demanding that 049 let him see inside of it.

00:34:23.610 --> 00:34:25.290
“Very well, doctor.”

00:34:25.290 --> 00:34:27.530
049 said, “in private.”

00:34:27.530 --> 00:34:32.060
049 began to pull a series of long metal poles
out of its bag followed by a rolled up curtain

00:34:32.060 --> 00:34:35.970
that it hung between them, creating a kind
of medical tent around Doctor Hamm.

00:34:35.970 --> 00:34:39.909
It seemed to stare for just a moment into
the observation camera outside of its cell

00:34:39.909 --> 00:34:41.970
before whipping the curtains shut.

00:34:41.970 --> 00:34:46.849
Doctor Hamm was discovered three hours later,
crawling around the floor of 049’s cell,

00:34:46.849 --> 00:34:49.000
now another mindless undead.

00:34:49.000 --> 00:34:53.720
When he was retrieved by security, 049 didn’t
even look up from his notebook.

00:34:53.720 --> 00:34:58.339
Doctor Hamm didn’t get the incinerator treatment,
but he did receive a fatal dose of drugs.

00:34:58.339 --> 00:34:59.349
A mercy.

00:34:59.349 --> 00:35:04.380
A removal team was sent to 049’s cell, but
it said there was no need for special extraction

00:35:04.380 --> 00:35:05.380
techniques.

00:35:05.380 --> 00:35:07.580
It would go willingly, wherever they wanted
it to go.

00:35:07.580 --> 00:35:10.620
It was not, it said, an enemy of the people.

00:35:10.620 --> 00:35:15.000
“The Hippocratic Oath forbids me to hurt
a human being,” it said while walking to

00:35:15.000 --> 00:35:16.290
the interrogation center.

00:35:16.290 --> 00:35:21.220
“My only desire is to offer you my services
and expertise.”

00:35:21.220 --> 00:35:25.390
The floors and walls of the interrogation
center room were painted a bright white.

00:35:25.390 --> 00:35:30.300
Even the table was white, which contrasted
with 049, a mass of black, sitting in the

00:35:30.300 --> 00:35:31.901
middle of the room.

00:35:31.901 --> 00:35:36.440
During interrogation it refused to admit or
even accept that it had killed Dr. Hamm.

00:35:36.440 --> 00:35:40.599
“I cured him, I removed the pestilence from
his body,” it said.

00:35:40.599 --> 00:35:45.290
It was later asked if it regretted its actions,
to which it replied, “Well, good sir, one

00:35:45.290 --> 00:35:50.440
always regrets the loss of a colleague for
any reason, but I stand by my actions.

00:35:50.440 --> 00:35:53.940
The pestilence must be abated before it’s
too late.”

00:35:53.940 --> 00:35:57.420
Every two weeks from that point 049 was given
animals.

00:35:57.420 --> 00:36:01.910
The scientists at the facility observed it
time and again, touching the animals, killing

00:36:01.910 --> 00:36:05.930
them, before producing a saw or scalpel and
opening them up.

00:36:05.930 --> 00:36:08.200
Organs would be carefully removed with perfect
precision.

00:36:08.200 --> 00:36:12.849
It was astounding to even trained surgeons
just how talented 049 was.

00:36:12.849 --> 00:36:18.770
“I require a close relative of yours,”
said 049 one day to a young doctor, who expressed

00:36:18.770 --> 00:36:21.300
shock that it was asking for one of the do
ctor’s family members.

00:36:21.300 --> 00:36:26.770
“I mean a great ape,” said 049, “not
your dear aunt.”

00:36:26.770 --> 00:36:30.940
There were several instances of 049 displaying
a crude sense of humor.

00:36:30.940 --> 00:36:34.800
Staff would almost forget that the thing they
were talking to wasn’t human… almost.

00:36:34.800 --> 00:36:39.849
And it was Doctor Thomas Morstead that had
supplied the great apes, orangutans in fact,

00:36:39.849 --> 00:36:45.089
that had been rescued from the rainforests
of Borneo only to be taken to 049’s cell.

00:36:45.089 --> 00:36:47.450
Then one day something changed.

00:36:47.450 --> 00:36:52.119
049 told Doctor Morstead that its work was
done, that it had accomplished what it had

00:36:52.119 --> 00:36:55.660
wanted to do, and could someone remove the
cured animal from its cell.

00:36:55.660 --> 00:37:00.390
“I think you’ll find that it’s quite
the work of art.

00:37:00.390 --> 00:37:02.160
A triumph,” 049 said through the intercom.

00:37:02.160 --> 00:37:06.319
When the removal team entered the cell they
found the orangutan, or what was left of it.

00:37:06.319 --> 00:37:10.119
It was lying in the corner of the cell, the
top of its skull had been removed leaving

00:37:10.119 --> 00:37:11.500
its brain exposed.

00:37:11.500 --> 00:37:16.980
On its face was an expression of relaxation
and from its mouth it issued very soft squeaks,

00:37:16.980 --> 00:37:18.880
like that of an infant.

00:37:18.880 --> 00:37:25.190
049 said, “Tell Doctor Morstead that it’s
rage mechanism no longer exists, I’ve removed

00:37:25.190 --> 00:37:29.460
the amygdala and made some changes to the
hypothalamus and limbic system.

00:37:29.460 --> 00:37:32.450
It is cured and quite harmless.”

00:37:32.450 --> 00:37:37.020
The next day Doctor Morstead announced that
he wanted to visit 049’s cell himself, after

00:37:37.020 --> 00:37:42.000
which he heard a chorus of disapproval from
his colleagues, all telling him that 049 was

00:37:42.000 --> 00:37:43.450
now too dangerous.

00:37:43.450 --> 00:37:47.859
“Dr. Hamm was sick,” replied Morstead,
“and 049 has assured us that he would never

00:37:47.859 --> 00:37:49.210
take another human life.

00:37:49.210 --> 00:37:52.720
He’s never lied to us and I’m going to
take him at his word.”

00:37:52.720 --> 00:37:56.670
It appeared that 049 had created the perfect
specimen, so what was next?

00:37:56.670 --> 00:37:59.410
Dr. Morstead had to know.

00:37:59.410 --> 00:38:04.880
“Everyone is sick,” 049 told Doctor Morstead
after the two had talked for a couple of minutes.

00:38:04.880 --> 00:38:07.020
“The great pandemic has started.

00:38:07.020 --> 00:38:13.020
Fear not doctor, I have a cure, no longer
will you humans spread your disease.”

00:38:13.020 --> 00:38:17.329
“I’m afraid you are wrong,” replied
the doctor, “This pandemic you speak of

00:38:17.329 --> 00:38:18.650
does not exist.

00:38:18.650 --> 00:38:20.250
We can happily live with our pathogens.

00:38:20.250 --> 00:38:22.270
We have done so for millennia.”

00:38:22.270 --> 00:38:25.230
Doctor Morstead became angry that he couldn’t
get through to 049.

00:38:25.230 --> 00:38:27.650
“I’m afraid you are suffering from paranoia.

00:38:27.650 --> 00:38:32.869
It is you who need to be cured”
“You have no idea,” said 049, standing

00:38:32.869 --> 00:38:33.869
up.

00:38:33.869 --> 00:38:37.470
“What are you doing?” shouted Morstead,
“you promised you wouldn’t hurt a human

00:38:37.470 --> 00:38:38.470
again!”

00:38:38.470 --> 00:38:44.660
“I’m not hurting you, I’m healing you”
049 said and leapt across the room in a flash,

00:38:44.660 --> 00:38:46.370
placing a hand on the doctor’s head.

00:38:46.370 --> 00:38:48.180
Morstead slumped to the ground.

00:38:48.180 --> 00:38:52.060
They were being watched in the observation
room and this had gone too far.

00:38:52.060 --> 00:38:55.340
He had to be moved to the containment cells,
permanently.

00:38:55.340 --> 00:38:59.200
Mobile Task Force Epsilon-11 was right on
the scene and burst through the door.

00:38:59.200 --> 00:39:05.820
“No imagination,” 049 said to himself,
“those humans have no imagination at all.”

00:39:05.820 --> 00:39:10.200
It began walking towards the task force who
opened fire on the anomaly, but the bullets

00:39:10.200 --> 00:39:13.540
bounced off its black coat and mask.

00:39:13.540 --> 00:39:18.460
SCP-049 calmly touched each of the members
of the task force one by one draining the

00:39:18.460 --> 00:39:19.711
life from them.

00:39:19.711 --> 00:39:25.790
The last one standing stopped firing and attempted
to run but again 049 leapt across the room,

00:39:25.790 --> 00:39:30.369
black cape billowing out behind him, and gently
touched the man causing him to drop to the

00:39:30.369 --> 00:39:31.369
floor.

00:39:31.369 --> 00:39:35.440
049 stepped over the bodies of the fallen
team and walked out of the confinement cell.

00:39:35.440 --> 00:39:39.620
The full details of what happened next are
available only to The O5 Council, what are

00:39:39.620 --> 00:39:41.119
sometimes called The Overseers.

00:39:41.119 --> 00:39:45.780
The redacted report that is available reads:
Standard Secure Humanoid Containment Cell,

00:39:45.780 --> 00:39:52.269
Research Sector-02, Site-19 – subject: SCP-049
Date of breach: REDACTED.

00:39:52.269 --> 00:39:57.410
Euclid Class SCP-049 breached cell and subsequently
gained access to adjoining rooms and nearby

00:39:57.410 --> 00:39:58.410
buildings.

00:39:58.410 --> 00:40:01.619
Breach lasted approximately three days and
five hours.

00:40:01.619 --> 00:40:06.109
Total Casualties: REDACTED with REDACTED number
of survivors requiring incineration therapy

00:40:06.109 --> 00:40:10.819
Course of action: Department of Science - Alchemy
Division, suggested injecting anti-transmogrify

00:40:10.819 --> 00:40:15.460
disinfectant into Class D former prisoners
who were transported to site and allowed to

00:40:15.460 --> 00:40:17.490
come into contact with SCP-049.

00:40:17.490 --> 00:40:21.900
SCP-049 failed to reanimate injected prisoners
and “cure” them.

00:40:21.900 --> 00:40:25.800
SCP-049 acknowledged this failure and surrendered
to Mobile Task Force Alpha-1.

00:40:25.800 --> 00:40:35.400
SCP-049 then requested to be contained.
Corpses littered the facility, around twenty

00:40:35.400 --> 00:40:36.400
in total.

00:40:36.400 --> 00:40:41.260
Their massacre had unfolded in a matter of
seconds despite their advanced armor and heavy

00:40:41.260 --> 00:40:42.260
weaponry.

00:40:42.260 --> 00:40:46.930
Their killer, wielding an ancient sword he’d
somehow pulled from thin air, looked upon

00:40:46.930 --> 00:40:48.280
them with disdain.

00:40:48.280 --> 00:40:50.579
Weak, unworthy opponents.

00:40:50.579 --> 00:40:55.740
He breathed heavily - His torso was covered
with bullet wounds and many had punctured

00:40:55.740 --> 00:40:56.740
his lungs.

00:40:56.740 --> 00:41:01.060
It was enough to kill a normal man ten times
over, but not him.

00:41:01.060 --> 00:41:02.880
He was the ultimate warrior.

00:41:02.880 --> 00:41:04.680
The perfect killing machine.

00:41:04.680 --> 00:41:09.020
He would need to seek out an enemy more fitting
for his legendary combat skills.

00:41:09.020 --> 00:41:13.570
He fled from the chamber into a connecting
hallway - where a giant, metal door slammed

00:41:13.570 --> 00:41:14.579
shut behind him.

00:41:14.579 --> 00:41:17.560
At the other end of the hall another door
did the same.

00:41:17.560 --> 00:41:22.109
He laughed at the thought of someone thinking
he could be trapped so easily and charged

00:41:22.109 --> 00:41:23.109
towards it.

00:41:23.109 --> 00:41:26.900
But then, large valves began to open all around
him.

00:41:26.900 --> 00:41:30.970
Torrents of freezing seawater began pouring
in, filling up the chamber and submerging

00:41:30.970 --> 00:41:33.290
him before he could reach the door.

00:41:33.290 --> 00:41:38.750
The warrior fought for breath, and he held
it far longer than any mortal man could - especially

00:41:38.750 --> 00:41:43.119
considering the state of his lungs, but water
always wins.

00:41:43.119 --> 00:41:48.020
He soon gave his last gasp, and floated, lifeless,
in the hallway.

00:41:48.020 --> 00:41:52.820
And soon after his body turned to dust and
disappeared in the briny water.

00:41:52.820 --> 00:41:54.000
But he would be back.

00:41:54.000 --> 00:41:56.170
He’d always be back.

00:41:56.170 --> 00:41:58.089
His brother was a different story.

00:41:58.089 --> 00:42:03.480
Tall and handsome, with a strange Sumerian
rune tattooed on his forehead, he was practically

00:42:03.480 --> 00:42:08.230
a pacifist and spent most of his days reading,
conversing with the staff, and wandering the

00:42:08.230 --> 00:42:10.490
facility of his own accord.

00:42:10.490 --> 00:42:14.410
Upon hearing of the latest incident at the
facility, he gave a sigh.

00:42:14.410 --> 00:42:17.329
He knew, on some level, that all of this was
his fault.

00:42:17.329 --> 00:42:20.030
It’d been centuries, millennia even.

00:42:20.030 --> 00:42:23.140
But was it too late to make things right?

00:42:23.140 --> 00:42:26.400
Six days later, the two are standing face
to face.

00:42:26.400 --> 00:42:30.809
This meeting had been a long time coming,
but it was always destined to end in blood

00:42:30.809 --> 00:42:31.869
and pain.

00:42:31.869 --> 00:42:37.210
The man with the runic tattoo was unarmed
- the only thing he carried was his regrets.

00:42:37.210 --> 00:42:41.490
The warrior, eyes full of burning fury, drew
his sword.

00:42:41.490 --> 00:42:44.740
And not long after, a head hit the floor.

00:42:44.740 --> 00:42:46.970
Who are these two strange men?

00:42:46.970 --> 00:42:49.450
And what is the significance of this fatal
meeting?

00:42:49.450 --> 00:42:52.390
In our own universe, these two may never meet
again.

00:42:52.390 --> 00:42:56.329
But that doesn’t mean there isn’t a universe
out there where they will.

00:42:56.329 --> 00:42:59.980
And it is that universe where today’s tale
springs from.

00:42:59.980 --> 00:43:04.900
The story between these two began a long,
long time ago, when they were both set on

00:43:04.900 --> 00:43:06.030
a course of destruction.

00:43:06.030 --> 00:43:11.730
But to learn how these two finally met once
again, we only need to go back a few days.

00:43:11.730 --> 00:43:16.140
While they seem more human than a lot of the
beings under the SCP Foundation’s watchful

00:43:16.140 --> 00:43:21.890
eyes, there’s no doubt that these two are
anomalous - they’re known as SCP - 073 and

00:43:21.890 --> 00:43:27.520
SCP - 076, but better known as Cain and Able.

00:43:27.520 --> 00:43:31.510
From the name alone, most people with a basic
knowledge of the bible could tell that these

00:43:31.510 --> 00:43:36.329
two have a connection, but the particulars
were largely a mystery to the Foundation.

00:43:36.329 --> 00:43:39.559
They wanted to know how much of the story
was actually true.

00:43:39.559 --> 00:43:45.920
SCP Researchers approached SCP - 073 - aka,
Cain - and asked if he’d be interested in

00:43:45.920 --> 00:43:48.710
reuniting with his brother one last time.

00:43:48.710 --> 00:43:55.220
It took Cain three days to answer: Yes, but
on one condition - that he was the only person

00:43:55.220 --> 00:43:56.980
who could abort the mission.

00:43:56.980 --> 00:44:01.309
The Foundation mulled over this requirement
for three days before the O5 Council finally

00:44:01.309 --> 00:44:04.500
made a decision - Cross-testing approved.

00:44:04.500 --> 00:44:10.670
These two brothers, separated by millennia,
would finally meet again - and the SCP Foundation

00:44:10.670 --> 00:44:13.480
would get to watch what happened when they
did.

00:44:13.480 --> 00:44:17.971
Of course, for safety reasons, the cross-test
would have to take place in 076’s containment

00:44:17.971 --> 00:44:21.950
facility - a bedrock chamber two hundred meters
beneath the sea.

00:44:21.950 --> 00:44:26.440
That would be the closest thing to “safe”
this fateful meeting could ever be.

00:44:26.440 --> 00:44:31.500
Though safe was really a relative term when
it came to SCP - 076.

00:44:31.500 --> 00:44:36.630
His escape attempts were as frequent and unpredictable
as they were deadly, and everyone who’d

00:44:36.630 --> 00:44:39.240
ever worked with him knew exactly why.

00:44:39.240 --> 00:44:44.569
To the untrained eye, it would seem strange
to create such a complex facility for a three-meter-squared

00:44:44.569 --> 00:44:48.160
stone cube, known as SCP - 076 - 01.

00:44:48.160 --> 00:44:51.120
But the cube isn’t what’s being locked
up here.

00:44:51.120 --> 00:44:58.300
The real danger comes from the corpse stored
inside - known as SCP - 076 - 02, but better

00:44:58.300 --> 00:44:59.820
known as Able.

00:44:59.820 --> 00:45:05.010
He’s a lean, olive-skinned man who appears
to be in his late twenties, but his most distinctive

00:45:05.010 --> 00:45:09.660
physical feature is the fact he’s covered
in an elaborate network of arcane and occult

00:45:09.660 --> 00:45:15.080
tattoos, largely of scowling, demonic faces
- similar to those found on members of the

00:45:15.080 --> 00:45:16.080
Yakuza.

00:45:16.080 --> 00:45:20.260
Whenever Able’s corpse reanimates, as it
does at random intervals, everyone in his

00:45:20.260 --> 00:45:22.290
vicinity is in terrible danger.

00:45:22.290 --> 00:45:26.750
He’s capable of pulling bladed weapons out
of miniature dimensional rifts that appear

00:45:26.750 --> 00:45:30.800
around him, though he does this so quickly
that it seems as though the weapons are simply

00:45:30.800 --> 00:45:33.180
materializing in his hands.

00:45:33.180 --> 00:45:37.119
Despite the increasingly complex efforts to
contain him, Able has been able to breach

00:45:37.119 --> 00:45:42.690
containment and go on brutal rampages multiple
times, killing scores of Foundation personnel

00:45:42.690 --> 00:45:44.349
in the process.

00:45:44.349 --> 00:45:47.849
Able possesses superhuman strength, speed,
and durability.

00:45:47.849 --> 00:45:52.650
During prior containment breaches, he was
able to shrug off rounds from .50 caliber

00:45:52.650 --> 00:45:53.650
machine guns.

00:45:53.650 --> 00:45:55.730
He’s torn through reinforced steel doors.

00:45:55.730 --> 00:46:00.369
He’s even swatted bullets from both handguns
and assault rifles out of the air with a length

00:46:00.369 --> 00:46:01.930
of steel rebar.

00:46:01.930 --> 00:46:07.000
Only killing him can truly end one of these
rampages - and the Foundation has had to go

00:46:07.000 --> 00:46:10.760
to terrifying lengths in order to achieve
this during past containment breaches.

00:46:10.760 --> 00:46:15.760
He’s been drowned, asphyxiated, crushed,
burned to death with a thermite grenade directly

00:46:15.760 --> 00:46:21.339
inserted into his chest cavity, and even disintegrated
by the activation of the facility’s on-site

00:46:21.339 --> 00:46:22.880
nuclear warhead.

00:46:22.880 --> 00:46:28.810
This may seem like overkill, but if Able ever
made his way into a major population center,

00:46:28.810 --> 00:46:34.230
the fatalities would be… well, of biblical
proportions.

00:46:34.230 --> 00:46:39.349
Even death can’t keep him down for long
- his body will rapidly decompose, and reappear

00:46:39.349 --> 00:46:42.730
intact within SCP - 076 - 01.

00:46:42.730 --> 00:46:47.680
There’s no telling when he’ll reawaken,
and his periods of inactivity can range from

00:46:47.680 --> 00:46:49.740
hours to years.

00:46:49.740 --> 00:46:54.700
Because of this, he must be constantly observed
by guards highly proficient in close combat,

00:46:54.700 --> 00:46:59.520
always ready to go toe to toe with perhaps
the most dangerous melee combatant in history.

00:46:59.520 --> 00:47:04.130
He’s such a proficient killer that there
was once a project aimed at weaponizing Able,

00:47:04.130 --> 00:47:07.730
but this turned out to be a disastrous failure
for the Foundation.

00:47:07.730 --> 00:47:11.460
When they ran out of missions to give him,
Able’s thirst for blood caused him to turn

00:47:11.460 --> 00:47:16.430
his blade on his allies with no remorse, hacking
them up with the same ruthless abandon he

00:47:16.430 --> 00:47:18.829
gave his assigned enemies.

00:47:18.829 --> 00:47:22.170
Interestingly, Able does seem to have a twisted
sense of honour.

00:47:22.170 --> 00:47:26.440
He has a respect for combatants capable of
providing him with a real challenge - and

00:47:26.440 --> 00:47:30.950
has even expressed concern for guards he considers
to be respectable adversaries.

00:47:30.950 --> 00:47:32.920
But don’t be fooled.

00:47:32.920 --> 00:47:39.910
There is no reasoning with Able - either you
kill him, or he kills you, and trust us, he’s

00:47:39.910 --> 00:47:41.510
much better at it than you are.

00:47:41.510 --> 00:47:46.190
While he’s often overshadowed by world-ending
anomalies or more talkative mass killers like

00:47:46.190 --> 00:47:51.980
SCP - 682, Able is one of the most deadly
creatures known to the Foundation.

00:47:51.980 --> 00:47:56.059
Able’s counterpart, 073, is an entirely
different story.

00:47:56.059 --> 00:48:01.109
Despite his namesake being the first biblical
murderer, this Cain is a far more enigmatic

00:48:01.109 --> 00:48:02.109
figure.

00:48:02.109 --> 00:48:06.140
A tall man in his early thirties appearing
to be of Middle Eastern descent, he has two

00:48:06.140 --> 00:48:10.950
very notable physical characteristics - The
untranslated Sumerian symbol tattooed onto

00:48:10.950 --> 00:48:16.089
his forehead, and the fact that several of
his body parts - namely, his arms, legs, spinal

00:48:16.089 --> 00:48:21.640
cord, and shoulder blades - have been replaced
by mysterious prosthetics of unknown origin.

00:48:21.640 --> 00:48:27.140
Unlike Able, Cain is polite, non-confrontational,
articulate, and communicative.

00:48:27.140 --> 00:48:31.710
He’s even allowed to wander the facility
he’s housed in freely, though with certain

00:48:31.710 --> 00:48:33.190
regulations.

00:48:33.190 --> 00:48:37.599
Namely that Cain is never permitted to come
into contact with plant life or uncovered

00:48:37.599 --> 00:48:38.599
ground.

00:48:38.599 --> 00:48:43.280
This is because, in spite of the fact Cain
doesn’t appear to harbor any malicious intent,

00:48:43.280 --> 00:48:48.950
direct contact with him causes plants to wither
and die, and the ground to become infertile.

00:48:48.950 --> 00:48:53.260
If Cain was ever introduced into a natural
environment again, he could unwittingly cause

00:48:53.260 --> 00:48:56.570
an ecological catastrophe with his mere presence.

00:48:56.570 --> 00:49:01.490
In that sense, he can be almost as dangerous
to large populations as Able.

00:49:01.490 --> 00:49:06.490
However, Cain’s danger to flora is only
one of his several anomalous qualities.

00:49:06.490 --> 00:49:10.900
Another is extraordinary mental capabilities,
and a near-perfect memory.

00:49:10.900 --> 00:49:15.920
His memory and ability to recall is so good,
that some Foundation personnel have suggested

00:49:15.920 --> 00:49:20.940
using Cain’s mind as a kind of backup server
for the Foundation’s collective knowledge.

00:49:20.940 --> 00:49:25.720
But perhaps his most well-known anomalous
trait is his ability to act as a kind of immortal

00:49:25.720 --> 00:49:27.230
human voodoo doll.

00:49:27.230 --> 00:49:32.670
That’s right: Any damage inflicted on Cain
causes no permanent injury to him, and is

00:49:32.670 --> 00:49:34.819
reflected back on the perpetrator.

00:49:34.819 --> 00:49:39.690
While Cain has remarked on still being able
to feel the pain of these assaults, he’s

00:49:39.690 --> 00:49:41.170
functionally immortal.

00:49:41.170 --> 00:49:45.920
This damage-reflecting ability has even made
performing certain kinds of tests impossible

00:49:45.920 --> 00:49:50.309
- as doctors attempting to draw blood from
Cain have found that their sample was actually

00:49:50.309 --> 00:49:54.480
their own blood, and their skin exhibited
the telltale bruising and puncture marks of

00:49:54.480 --> 00:49:55.599
blood testing.

00:49:55.599 --> 00:50:00.550
But, of most interest to the Foundation right
now was the fact that Cain has shown prior

00:50:00.550 --> 00:50:01.960
knowledge of Able.

00:50:01.960 --> 00:50:04.150
However, he was cagey when questioned.

00:50:04.150 --> 00:50:08.980
He refused to add any additional information,
and commented that it would be best if he

00:50:08.980 --> 00:50:11.440
and Able were never brought into contact.

00:50:11.440 --> 00:50:14.740
But what does “never” really mean when
you live forever?

00:50:14.740 --> 00:50:19.120
Cain finally did agree to the test, and while
nobody knows what caused Cain’s change of

00:50:19.120 --> 00:50:23.839
heart, the Foundation knew better than to
push him and potentially get the entire mission

00:50:23.839 --> 00:50:24.839
scrapped.

00:50:24.839 --> 00:50:29.300
Cain was taken from his facility, and transported
to the access point of Able’s underwater

00:50:29.300 --> 00:50:30.300
prison.

00:50:30.300 --> 00:50:34.770
The experiment’s design was simple: Cain
would occupy a temporary residence in Able’s

00:50:34.770 --> 00:50:39.040
containment facility until he next reanimated,
at which point, the interaction between them

00:50:39.040 --> 00:50:41.140
would be closely observed.

00:50:41.140 --> 00:50:45.640
Perhaps it would take weeks, months, or even
years before Able once again awoke and left

00:50:45.640 --> 00:50:49.260
his cube, but when he did, Cain would be there.

00:50:49.260 --> 00:50:54.150
Thankfully, as Cain whispered soothing words
into Able’s tomb, the resurrection took

00:50:54.150 --> 00:50:55.550
only minutes.

00:50:55.550 --> 00:50:59.270
If you were expecting a heartwarming reunion,
think again.

00:50:59.270 --> 00:51:03.940
Cain tried to apologize, but Able immediately
struck him with a decapitating blow which

00:51:03.940 --> 00:51:09.569
- due to Cain's damage reflecting properties
- caused him to sever his own head instead.

00:51:09.569 --> 00:51:14.339
Cain remained at Able’s side as he regenerated,
and as soon as Able did he immediately tried

00:51:14.339 --> 00:51:15.990
killing Cain again.

00:51:15.990 --> 00:51:19.160
It went on like this again and again and again.

00:51:19.160 --> 00:51:25.800
It was an arduous process, and eventually,
even Able began to tire of it.

00:51:25.800 --> 00:51:28.599
The duo fell into a tearful embrace.

00:51:28.599 --> 00:51:31.540
Able asked his brother why he killed him,
so long ago.

00:51:31.540 --> 00:51:36.630
And Cain apologized, saying it was the actions
of a younger and more foolish man.

00:51:36.630 --> 00:51:41.930
In that moment, the two finally reconciled,
and having fulfilled their bargain with their

00:51:41.930 --> 00:51:44.880
creator, both finally crumbled into dust.

00:51:44.880 --> 00:51:47.059
Peace at last.

00:51:47.059 --> 00:51:51.980
As we said, this didn’t happen to the Cain
and Able of our universe, but when universes

00:51:51.980 --> 00:51:57.390
are more numerous than grains of sand in an
endless desert, such a thing has indeed happened

00:51:57.390 --> 00:52:01.359
somewhere out there - perhaps in a kinder
universe than our own.

00:52:01.359 --> 00:52:06.730
We can only hope that our Cain and Able may
someday get to meet and find the same resolution,

00:52:06.730 --> 00:52:11.210
but until then, these brothers will remain
one of the most dangerous and fascinating

00:52:11.210 --> 00:52:16.359
duos in the Foundation’s catalogue.

00:52:16.359 --> 00:52:18.940
Joseph and Frank were two life-long Squatchers.

00:52:18.940 --> 00:52:23.980
No, that isn’t an insult – that’s a
self-given title for Bigfoot enthusiasts who

00:52:23.980 --> 00:52:28.540
are willing to head out into the woods and
search for the legendary Sasquatch first-hand.

00:52:28.540 --> 00:52:32.940
While most Squatchers will go their whole
lives without ever encountering one, Joseph

00:52:32.940 --> 00:52:37.010
and Frank were about to get lucky – they
just didn’t know what kind of luck.

00:52:37.010 --> 00:52:40.690
During a journey through the forests of the
Pacific Northwest, Frank spotted something

00:52:40.690 --> 00:52:46.200
moving in the distance: A huge, ape-like creature
with greyish fur and human-like movements.

00:52:46.200 --> 00:52:51.040
Frank thought he was finally laying eyes on
the mighty Bigfoot after decades of searching.

00:52:51.040 --> 00:52:54.369
What he didn’t know was that he’d just
made a deadly mistake.

00:52:54.369 --> 00:52:57.900
He had looked directly at SCP – 1000.

00:52:57.900 --> 00:53:00.760
And there would be terrible consequences.

00:53:00.760 --> 00:53:04.750
Frank was excited – he’d just achieved
the life goal of any Squatcher.

00:53:04.750 --> 00:53:08.760
He tapped Joseph on the shoulder and directed
him to look in the direction of the creature.

00:53:08.760 --> 00:53:11.880
Joseph followed Frank’s direction and stared
into the distance.

00:53:11.880 --> 00:53:16.230
When his eyes finally came into focus on the
ape-like beast... he froze.

00:53:16.230 --> 00:53:18.119
His brain just short-circuited.

00:53:18.119 --> 00:53:22.530
One second, he was about to encounter the
Holy Grail of his hobby, and the next, he

00:53:22.530 --> 00:53:24.490
was literally brain dead.

00:53:24.490 --> 00:53:26.170
Joseph collapsed.

00:53:26.170 --> 00:53:29.980
In the distance, the ape-like creature disappeared
back into the woods.

00:53:29.980 --> 00:53:34.930
Not that Frank even noticed – he was too
busy trying to wake Joseph but it was no use.

00:53:34.930 --> 00:53:38.579
Joseph was gone, and Frank had no idea why.

00:53:38.579 --> 00:53:41.109
The headlines read, “BIGFOOT KILLED MY FRIEND.”

00:53:41.109 --> 00:53:43.890
Most people either ignored it or laughed it
off.

00:53:43.890 --> 00:53:47.670
Just a couple of cranks goofing off in a forest
and one of them had dropped dead.

00:53:47.670 --> 00:53:48.710
Who cares?

00:53:48.710 --> 00:53:52.910
Well, one organization cared – The SCP Foundation.

00:53:52.910 --> 00:53:58.400
Mobile Task Force Zeta-1000, the Foundation’s
specialized SCP – 1000 detail, were alerted

00:53:58.400 --> 00:53:59.520
to the reports.

00:53:59.520 --> 00:54:03.349
They sprang into action, tracking down and
detaining Frank for questioning.

00:54:03.349 --> 00:54:07.740
They process a million loony bigfoot lovers
every year, and usually find nothing, but

00:54:07.740 --> 00:54:11.260
the death of Frank’s friend made it all
too clear – they hadn’t encountered a

00:54:11.260 --> 00:54:15.770
bigfoot but a real, genuine example of SCP
– 1000.

00:54:15.770 --> 00:54:22.400
SCP – 1000 rarely ranks among the scariest
or most dangerous SCPs, but underestimating

00:54:22.400 --> 00:54:27.430
the creature is a terrible mistake because
just looking at it gives you a 2% chance of

00:54:27.430 --> 00:54:29.400
dropping dead on the spot.

00:54:29.400 --> 00:54:32.599
Frank, despite losing his friend, was one
of the lucky ones.

00:54:32.599 --> 00:54:37.171
The Foundation debriefed him before administering
amnestics, and making sure that he’d never

00:54:37.171 --> 00:54:41.760
venture back to those mysterious forests in
the Pacific Northwest again.

00:54:41.760 --> 00:54:46.440
Director Jones, the site director charged
with the management of SCP – 1000 populations,

00:54:46.440 --> 00:54:50.309
was given the information on this latest case
of a SCP – 1000-related fatality.

00:54:50.309 --> 00:54:53.700
It was a story he’d heard many times before.

00:54:53.700 --> 00:54:56.630
For Director Jones, they all seemed to bleed
into one another.

00:54:56.630 --> 00:54:59.849
So, what exactly is SCP – 1000?

00:54:59.849 --> 00:55:02.640
And how did it leave poor Joseph dead in the
woods?

00:55:02.640 --> 00:55:06.830
SCP – 1000 is a whole species of large,
hominid ape-like creatures.

00:55:06.830 --> 00:55:10.770
They’re largely nocturnal, but sightings
of the creature during the day aren’t unheard

00:55:10.770 --> 00:55:11.770
of.

00:55:11.770 --> 00:55:15.960
They’re omnivorous, mostly seeming to consume
plants and insects and their fur is usually

00:55:15.960 --> 00:55:19.960
grey, brown, black, red, or occasionally white.

00:55:19.960 --> 00:55:25.990
The creatures have large eyes capable of impressive
vision nestled underneath a pronounced, Neanderthal-like

00:55:25.990 --> 00:55:26.990
brow.

00:55:26.990 --> 00:55:30.839
Another defining feature is the ridge of bone
on the forehead, much like that of a gorilla,

00:55:30.839 --> 00:55:33.190
that is present in both sexes.

00:55:33.190 --> 00:55:37.380
According to Foundation studies, the creatures
exhibit a level of intelligence on par with

00:55:37.380 --> 00:55:41.319
that of the common chimpanzee, but nowhere
near that of us humans.

00:55:41.319 --> 00:55:44.630
What they lack in intelligence, though, they
more than make up for in size.

00:55:44.630 --> 00:55:49.510
The adults can be as large as ten feet tall,
and weigh up to 600 pounds.

00:55:49.510 --> 00:55:54.540
Despite their great size and impressive strength,
the creatures are neither aggressive nor territorial.

00:55:54.540 --> 00:55:58.910
In fact, they seem to instinctively avoid
humans, mostly residing deep in the forests

00:55:58.910 --> 00:56:02.079
of the American Pacific Northwest and in the
Himalayan Alps.

00:56:02.079 --> 00:56:06.530
There have been sightings of SCP – 1000
on every continent, though the Foundation

00:56:06.530 --> 00:56:11.900
has taken pains to exterminate all SCP – 1000
populations situated near human population

00:56:11.900 --> 00:56:17.420
centres to prevent a potential disaster, considering
the 2% chance of instantaneous death upon

00:56:17.420 --> 00:56:19.200
visual contact.

00:56:19.200 --> 00:56:23.130
That brings us to our second question: What
is it that makes these seemingly harmless

00:56:23.130 --> 00:56:24.670
creatures so dangerous?

00:56:24.670 --> 00:56:29.970
Sadly, for both these unfortunate creatures
and us humans, the danger is beyond the control

00:56:29.970 --> 00:56:32.630
of SCP – 1000.

00:56:32.630 --> 00:56:38.070
According to Foundation research, SCP – 1000
likely evolved alongside us homo sapiens,

00:56:38.070 --> 00:56:41.670
until a tragedy occurred between ten and fifteen-thousand
years ago.

00:56:41.670 --> 00:56:46.520
A mysterious extinction event eliminated the
vast majority of their species, leaving only

00:56:46.520 --> 00:56:49.549
one to five percent alive in the aftermath.

00:56:49.549 --> 00:56:50.549
What happened?

00:56:50.549 --> 00:56:54.920
It’s believed that around this time, SCP
– 1000 contracted what the Foundation refers

00:56:54.920 --> 00:56:57.450
to as an “anomalous pseudo-disease.”

00:56:57.450 --> 00:57:04.160
Meet SCP-1000-f1, a disease that is passed
along at the genetic level, and is so durable

00:57:04.160 --> 00:57:06.980
that it persists in the species to this day.

00:57:06.980 --> 00:57:11.220
The tiny fraction of the population that are
immune to its effects manage to survive, but

00:57:11.220 --> 00:57:14.309
the majority who aren’t immune die shortly
after birth.

00:57:14.309 --> 00:57:17.760
This is why the overall population remains
relatively low to this day.

00:57:17.760 --> 00:57:22.620
It’s a disease that only appears to affect
hominids – including humans, chimpanzees,

00:57:22.620 --> 00:57:26.369
bonobos, and non-immune instances of SCP-1000.

00:57:26.369 --> 00:57:31.220
Any hominid that lays eyes on a carrier of
the disease has a two percent chance of experiencing

00:57:31.220 --> 00:57:33.089
immediate brain death.

00:57:33.089 --> 00:57:37.170
While a two percent chance of instant death
may not seem all that threatening, at least

00:57:37.170 --> 00:57:42.170
when compared to some other nightmare inducing
SCPs, the truly scary part is that the percentage

00:57:42.170 --> 00:57:43.530
is cumulative.

00:57:43.530 --> 00:57:49.049
In other words, the longer one observes a
carrier of SCP-1000-f1, the higher that percentage

00:57:49.049 --> 00:57:54.160
rises and the greater your chance of experiencing
an abrupt death.

00:57:54.160 --> 00:57:59.490
According to Foundation studies, the percentage
rises by around one percent every twenty minutes.

00:57:59.490 --> 00:58:04.450
And the percentage also varies between specimens,
with some exhibiting a terrifyingly high death

00:58:04.450 --> 00:58:07.200
chance of 90% upon viewing.

00:58:07.200 --> 00:58:11.579
This death chance continues to occur in dead
specimens if they carried the anomalous pseudo-virus

00:58:11.579 --> 00:58:15.780
while alive – though thankfully the risk
doesn’t appear to apply to small fur or

00:58:15.780 --> 00:58:17.079
tissue samples.

00:58:17.079 --> 00:58:22.170
The Foundation’s true concern actually goes
far beyond SCP – 1000 themselves.

00:58:22.170 --> 00:58:27.900
Because of the species’ close relation to
homo sapiens, there’s a worry that SCP-1000-f1

00:58:27.900 --> 00:58:32.859
could transmit to humans, causing our own
species to meet a similar fate.

00:58:32.859 --> 00:58:39.049
If humans did indeed become carriers of SCP-1000-f1,
it’s extremely likely that humanity would

00:58:39.049 --> 00:58:44.359
undergo an unprecedented extinction event,
with billions across the globe dropping dead

00:58:44.359 --> 00:58:46.730
as brain death sets in en masse.

00:58:46.730 --> 00:58:51.380
While full extermination of the entire species
has been deemed unlikely, the existential

00:58:51.380 --> 00:58:58.280
threat they pose to humanity more than justifies
the occasional culling of SCP – 1000 populations.

00:58:58.280 --> 00:58:59.780
That was a lot to take in, right?

00:58:59.780 --> 00:59:03.930
First, the creature we thought was Bigfoot
was actually a new species of SCP out in the

00:59:03.930 --> 00:59:04.930
wild.

00:59:04.930 --> 00:59:08.980
And second, these creatures could end human
life as we know it if they made it into a

00:59:08.980 --> 00:59:10.350
population center.

00:59:10.350 --> 00:59:14.320
But what you’re about to hear next – a
dark secret only available to people with

00:59:14.320 --> 00:59:20.530
Level 3 SCP Foundation clearance – is the
most shocking SCP - 1000 fact of all...

00:59:20.530 --> 00:59:21.559
Are you ready?

00:59:21.559 --> 00:59:26.569
The true secret of SCP - 1000 is that what
you’ve just heard is a lie.

00:59:26.569 --> 00:59:31.630
There is no anomalous pseudo-disease, and
SCP – 1000 poses no pathogenic threat to

00:59:31.630 --> 00:59:33.099
humanity whatsoever.

00:59:33.099 --> 00:59:35.400
Who would spread such a thing?

00:59:35.400 --> 00:59:37.790
The SCP Foundation, of course.

00:59:37.790 --> 00:59:43.170
Strictly speaking, the Foundation has disseminated
two direct lies about the nature of SCP – 1000.

00:59:43.170 --> 00:59:47.800
The first is that of the disease, which does
not exist, nor has it ever existed.

00:59:47.800 --> 00:59:52.109
The second lie is about the creature’s intelligence
level – they’re far smarter than the average

00:59:52.109 --> 00:59:53.109
chimp.

00:59:53.109 --> 00:59:56.280
In fact, they’re every bit as intelligent
as human beings.

00:59:56.280 --> 01:00:00.640
These were all lies formulated by Director
Jones and the Foundation, as was the very

01:00:00.640 --> 01:00:03.000
existence of the Bigfoot myth.

01:00:03.000 --> 01:00:06.940
The Foundation has been spreading information
that makes the very concept of the Sasquatch

01:00:06.940 --> 01:00:13.230
out to be a joke for decades, all to discredit
and further push the very concept of SCP – 1000

01:00:13.230 --> 01:00:14.740
into the shadows.

01:00:14.740 --> 01:00:15.900
But…why?

01:00:15.900 --> 01:00:19.799
The Foundation is no stranger to coming up
with cover stories, but why would they put

01:00:19.799 --> 01:00:24.089
intentional lies into their own files to anyone
below a Level 3 clearance?

01:00:24.089 --> 01:00:29.339
Well, that all comes down to the horrifying
truth behind the origins of today’s SCP

01:00:29.339 --> 01:00:30.779
– 1000 population.

01:00:30.779 --> 01:00:34.730
The creatures were first brought to the attention
of the Foundation by outcast members of the

01:00:34.730 --> 01:00:40.000
Serpent’s Hand, an organization dedicated
to defying the Foundation’s activities.

01:00:40.000 --> 01:00:44.799
These members, known as the Children of the
Sun, told them the secret history of SCP – 1000.

01:00:44.799 --> 01:00:49.390
While at first, Foundation personnel like
Director Jones didn’t want to believe what

01:00:49.390 --> 01:00:53.849
they were hearing, they soon came to terms
with the horrifying truth.

01:00:53.849 --> 01:00:58.730
Humans and SCP – 1000 did evolve alongside
each other, with humans occupying the day

01:00:58.730 --> 01:01:00.920
and SCP – 1000 the night.

01:01:00.920 --> 01:01:06.990
However, while humans were still basic hunter-gatherers,
SCP – 1000 were undergoing vast intellectual

01:01:06.990 --> 01:01:08.819
and societal development.

01:01:08.819 --> 01:01:14.630
They were able to create tools, weapons, agriculture,
stable settlements, domesticated animals,

01:01:14.630 --> 01:01:16.880
and eventually even fully developed cities.

01:01:16.880 --> 01:01:22.069
It was like nothing the world had ever seen,
and wouldn’t see for thousands of years

01:01:22.069 --> 01:01:23.069
to come.

01:01:23.069 --> 01:01:27.140
Their numbers swelled into the tens of billions,
as they created culture and technology hitherto

01:01:27.140 --> 01:01:31.280
unimagined – including weapons of devastating
power.

01:01:31.280 --> 01:01:35.191
Meanwhile, humanity was pushed to the brink
of destruction by their competitor’s rapid

01:01:35.191 --> 01:01:37.789
and seemingly unstoppable growth.

01:01:37.789 --> 01:01:42.040
It looked as though the human species had
lost the evolutionary arms race, and would

01:01:42.040 --> 01:01:46.980
have to bow out, but according to the Children
of the Sun, a trickster forest god smiled

01:01:46.980 --> 01:01:52.600
upon humanity, and gave them the power to
use SCP – 1000’s weapons and technology

01:01:52.600 --> 01:01:53.830
against them.

01:01:53.830 --> 01:01:59.570
70% of SCP – 1000’s population were wiped
out in a single horrific day, known to the

01:01:59.570 --> 01:02:04.430
Children of the Sun as “The Day of the Flowers”
– as every flower supposedly bloomed that

01:02:04.430 --> 01:02:07.020
day during the massacre.

01:02:07.020 --> 01:02:11.859
Humanity destroyed the entire civilization,
and with the same technology they stole from

01:02:11.859 --> 01:02:16.529
these unfortunate creatures, the vengeful
humans drove the apes mad.

01:02:16.529 --> 01:02:20.809
Their higher consciousnesses were blocked
out, reducing them back into the states of

01:02:20.809 --> 01:02:22.369
mere animals.

01:02:22.369 --> 01:02:26.420
Once the massacre was done and everything
that was built had been destroyed, we – the

01:02:26.420 --> 01:02:31.930
human race – used the SCP – 1000 weapons
to wipe any memory of the atrocity from our

01:02:31.930 --> 01:02:33.299
own minds.

01:02:33.299 --> 01:02:38.109
The advanced civilization of SCP – 1000
had been wiped from history.

01:02:38.109 --> 01:02:41.539
Humans returned to their plodding path of
evolution, none the wiser.

01:02:41.539 --> 01:02:46.910
For thousands of years, all the way up until
today, this time remained a mystery to us.

01:02:46.910 --> 01:02:49.789
So again, why did the Foundation lie to us?

01:02:49.789 --> 01:02:53.170
What did they have to gain, by convincing
us all that it was dangerous to even look

01:02:53.170 --> 01:02:54.170
at these creatures?

01:02:54.170 --> 01:02:58.289
Well, as the frequency of sightings and the
attempts at communication increased, people

01:02:58.289 --> 01:03:03.349
like Director Jones became aware of a frightening
possibility: What if the pendulum was swinging

01:03:03.349 --> 01:03:04.589
back?

01:03:04.589 --> 01:03:09.380
What if the apes were regaining their lost
intelligence, and worse, still harboured feelings

01:03:09.380 --> 01:03:14.280
of revenge for what we did to their species
thousands of years ago?

01:03:14.280 --> 01:03:18.650
Even the mere possibility that they could
do to us what we once did to them is a chance

01:03:18.650 --> 01:03:24.080
that the Foundation simply cannot take, and
thus, limiting contact between humans and

01:03:24.080 --> 01:03:28.069
SCP - 1000 at all costs is an absolute must.

01:03:28.069 --> 01:03:33.109
However, in spite of the Foundation’s fears,
one intercepted message from the apes suggests

01:03:33.109 --> 01:03:35.660
that their paranoia may be misplaced.

01:03:35.660 --> 01:03:40.020
This message, translated from an attempt at
communicating with Foundation personnel, reads

01:03:40.020 --> 01:03:43.140
simply as follows:
“we forgive you;

01:03:43.140 --> 01:03:47.170
given choice for now, not forever;
let us back in.”

01:03:47.170 --> 01:03:51.230
It’s enough to make you wonder what species
the Foundation should really be keeping tabs

01:03:51.230 --> 01:03:52.230
on here.

01:03:52.230 --> 01:03:55.990
After all, when it comes to meting out violence
and death, humanity has a track record to

01:03:55.990 --> 01:03:59.819
rival the worst creatures in the Foundation
containment cells.

01:03:59.819 --> 01:04:08.460
And few examples illustrate that better than
the tragic case of SCP – 1000.

01:04:08.460 --> 01:04:12.470
Ask yourself a simple question: is the world
around you real?

01:04:12.470 --> 01:04:16.270
It seems like the answer should be pretty
obvious, of course the world as you know it

01:04:16.270 --> 01:04:19.420
is real, how else would you be able to interact
with it?

01:04:19.420 --> 01:04:24.619
Perhaps a better question would be this: how
much do you trust that the world you know

01:04:24.619 --> 01:04:25.960
is real?

01:04:25.960 --> 01:04:31.220
How much do you believe that reality, as you
experience it, is constant and cannot be changed

01:04:31.220 --> 01:04:32.890
by external forces?

01:04:32.890 --> 01:04:39.500
Now imagine, if you can, an entity to which
the very fabric of reality is like clay, able

01:04:39.500 --> 01:04:41.980
to be shaped and moulded at will.

01:04:41.980 --> 01:04:47.880
Then imagine that this entity, this malicious,
twisted, and unknowable horror, has taken

01:04:47.880 --> 01:04:51.250
a special interest in you.

01:04:51.250 --> 01:04:55.440
If you can picture such a scenario, then you
may as well be standing in the shoes of James

01:04:55.440 --> 01:05:01.150
Talloran, a Foundation Researcher assigned
to SCP-3999.

01:05:01.150 --> 01:05:05.710
Talloran could only have imagined what the
Foundation kept contained in an airtight cell

01:05:05.710 --> 01:05:10.471
all the way at the bottom of a kilometer-long
shaft; and never expected that it was where

01:05:10.471 --> 01:05:13.580
his lifeless body would be found.

01:05:13.580 --> 01:05:19.940
Despite having the entity contained, The Foundation’s
only knowledge of SCP-3999 came from a text

01:05:19.940 --> 01:05:24.140
document discovered on Talloran’s phone
after his demise.

01:05:24.140 --> 01:05:28.410
Even their own records indicated no such creature
ever existed.

01:05:28.410 --> 01:05:29.410
Why?

01:05:29.410 --> 01:05:33.180
What could possibly shape reality in such
a way that it could hide its own existence

01:05:33.180 --> 01:05:37.230
from the Foundation and where was it now?

01:05:37.230 --> 01:05:41.660
The document on Talloran’s phone yielded
some evidence, seeming to be a long, rambling

01:05:41.660 --> 01:05:47.470
and confused account of some sort of battle
between a reality-shaping entity and the ill-fated

01:05:47.470 --> 01:05:49.109
researcher sent to examine it.

01:05:49.109 --> 01:05:55.580
Unlike many of the Foundation’s documents,
the entry for SCP-3999 is riddled with contradictions

01:05:55.580 --> 01:06:01.089
and strikethroughs, a confusing mess of information
that makes piecing together the mystery of

01:06:01.089 --> 01:06:03.470
Talloran’s fate all the more difficult.

01:06:03.470 --> 01:06:11.040
Beneath the strange formatting and uncertainty
surrounding SCP-3999, lies a tale of one man’s

01:06:11.040 --> 01:06:17.079
torture for the amusement of an impossibly
powerful, and eternally sadistic creature.

01:06:17.079 --> 01:06:23.630
Based on the information available, it appears
that SCP-3999 was fixated on Talloran.

01:06:23.630 --> 01:06:28.369
Upon his arrival to the entity’s cell, the
researcher had no idea how long he would spend

01:06:28.369 --> 01:06:33.720
in the company of SCP-3999, or that he would
never emerge alive.

01:06:33.720 --> 01:06:38.470
Many SCPs contained by the Foundation require
their own, specially made prisons in order

01:06:38.470 --> 01:06:44.000
to hold them, and maintain the safety of Foundation
staff as well as the outside world.

01:06:44.000 --> 01:06:49.190
But imagine if an SCP could turn the tables,
bending the world around a single member of

01:06:49.190 --> 01:06:54.619
the Foundation, using all of reality as their
own personalized torture chamber.

01:06:54.619 --> 01:06:59.150
The list of horrors endured by Talloran is
both exhaustive and incomplete, given the

01:06:59.150 --> 01:07:01.720
length of time he spent with the creature.

01:07:01.720 --> 01:07:08.010
Shortly after James Talloran was assigned
to Site 118, where SCP-3999 was contained,

01:07:08.010 --> 01:07:12.619
the entity was somehow able to breach containment,
and began to twist the world around it in

01:07:12.619 --> 01:07:18.770
the name of a singular, cruel objective: the
torture of James Talloran.

01:07:18.770 --> 01:07:23.799
The creature, while having no physical description,
possessed the capability to cause either a

01:07:23.799 --> 01:07:30.079
CK-Class reality-restructuring event or a
ZK-Class end-of-reality event.

01:07:30.079 --> 01:07:35.740
SCP-3999 was somehow able to both destroy
reality itself, while simultaneously rebuilding

01:07:35.740 --> 01:07:39.680
it around its prisoner, Researcher Talloran.

01:07:39.680 --> 01:07:46.460
Every creature, person and machination that
SCP-3999 created was done so with the express

01:07:46.460 --> 01:07:52.240
intention of making Talloran suffer horribly,
over the course of millions of years, according

01:07:52.240 --> 01:07:54.900
to the researcher’s own writings.

01:07:54.900 --> 01:08:00.220
The exact details of how SCP-3999 tortured
Talloran are littered throughout the document

01:08:00.220 --> 01:08:04.859
he was found with, but it is apparent that
the entity was able to use its reality-altering

01:08:04.859 --> 01:08:11.670
abilities to continually revive the man after
killing him as a way to prolong his pain.

01:08:11.670 --> 01:08:18.000
SCP-3999 seemed to be able to use its powers
however it pleased, but focused all its attention

01:08:18.000 --> 01:08:21.540
and actions on causing misery for one man.

01:08:21.540 --> 01:08:25.160
It simply enjoyed inflicting suffering.

01:08:25.160 --> 01:08:29.620
One of the examples of the atrocities James
Talloran suffered at the mercy of a cold,

01:08:29.620 --> 01:08:33.720
remorseless creature, was witnessing the deaths
of others.

01:08:33.720 --> 01:08:39.350
Although they were constructed and controlled
by SCP-3999, Talloran saw trained Foundation

01:08:39.350 --> 01:08:42.560
security personnel drag three people into
the cell.

01:08:42.560 --> 01:08:46.500
He recognized them instantly as his own family.

01:08:46.500 --> 01:08:51.880
Talloran was made to watch as his mother,
father and sister were executed with a single

01:08:51.880 --> 01:08:56.830
gunshot to the head by members of the same
organization that he was a part of.

01:08:56.830 --> 01:09:00.470
All of Talloran’s professional colleagues
were also killed in front of him, followed

01:09:00.470 --> 01:09:05.370
by anyone and everyone that had ever come
into contact with James Talloran – including

01:09:05.370 --> 01:09:07.330
the O5 Council.

01:09:07.330 --> 01:09:12.220
He was made to watch as they were systematically
murdered one by one, all for the enjoyment

01:09:12.220 --> 01:09:15.250
of SCP-3999.

01:09:15.250 --> 01:09:18.721
At some point during the creature’s torture
of Talloran, there appears to have been some

01:09:18.721 --> 01:09:22.241
attempt to contain SCP-3999 within another
SCP.

01:09:22.241 --> 01:09:29.870
SCP-2432, a room within a hotel now owned
by the Foundation that appears to compel its

01:09:29.870 --> 01:09:34.040
guests to write exaggerated positive reviews
after staying there.

01:09:34.040 --> 01:09:41.220
When introduced to SCP-2432, SCP-3999’s
ability to alter reality seemed to have the

01:09:41.220 --> 01:09:45.100
effect of creating a dimensional anomaly within
the hotel.

01:09:45.100 --> 01:09:51.320
A crawl space opened up in SCP-2432, leading
to an almost-identical room.

01:09:51.320 --> 01:09:57.820
The front door of this new room, designated
SCP-2432-1, led to an alternate dimension

01:09:57.820 --> 01:10:02.510
that resembled the hotel housing SCP-2432.

01:10:02.510 --> 01:10:08.820
The rooms of this new version of the SCP-2432
hotel were a seemingly infinite, endless loop

01:10:08.820 --> 01:10:10.540
of rooms.

01:10:10.540 --> 01:10:15.060
This dimension also played host to a number
of strange creatures, as uncovered by Foundation

01:10:15.060 --> 01:10:17.350
research teams that ventured there.

01:10:17.350 --> 01:10:23.890
Most notably, a creature resembling a male
homo sapien calling itself ‘Researcher Talloran’.

01:10:23.890 --> 01:10:27.890
This being was dressed in clothing similar
to Foundation researchers, and appeared nervous

01:10:27.890 --> 01:10:32.320
and confused when approached by personnel,
only to be executed when asking to be told

01:10:32.320 --> 01:10:36.050
the location of SCP-3999.

01:10:36.050 --> 01:10:41.700
Later during SCP-3999’s three-million-year
torture session, James Talloran was interviewed

01:10:41.700 --> 01:10:45.270
with another doctor working for the Foundation.

01:10:45.270 --> 01:10:51.310
The interviewing doctor had no recollection
of Talloran’s assignment to SCP-3999, and

01:10:51.310 --> 01:10:57.430
both James’ involvement with the Foundation
and the very existence of SCP-3999 were expunged

01:10:57.430 --> 01:10:59.910
from the Foundation’s database.

01:10:59.910 --> 01:11:04.670
As Talloran desperately tried to rationalize
what was happening to him, he told the interviewer:

01:11:04.670 --> 01:11:07.020
“I have trouble focusing on things now.

01:11:07.020 --> 01:11:09.200
I just feel a lot of unease.

01:11:09.200 --> 01:11:13.870
It's like reality has started to feel less…real…if
that makes sense.”

01:11:13.870 --> 01:11:18.620
Shortly after, he attempted to ask the doctor
for his name, and for him to identify which

01:11:18.620 --> 01:11:21.040
of the Foundation’s sites they were at.

01:11:21.040 --> 01:11:25.530
In response, the doctor emitted a strange
noise from his mouth, confusing Talloran.

01:11:25.530 --> 01:11:30.000
“That's not a name, you just made a noise
with your mouth,” the researcher replied.

01:11:30.000 --> 01:11:32.410
“Why am I thinking of redactions?

01:11:32.410 --> 01:11:36.290
How can a word be redacted like that in normal
conversation?”

01:11:36.290 --> 01:11:41.850
Following this, the floor beneath James Talloran’s
feet disappeared and he was swallowed by darkness,

01:11:41.850 --> 01:11:47.740
as SCP-3999 continued bending reality to make
him suffer.

01:11:47.740 --> 01:11:51.870
What followed were multiple failed attempts
from Talloran to either escape from the entity

01:11:51.870 --> 01:11:54.770
or nullify its abilities to warp reality.

01:11:54.770 --> 01:12:01.180
SCP-3999 had, at a later point, trapped Talloran
in a stage play version of its cell.

01:12:01.180 --> 01:12:05.710
As James frantically tried to escape out of
a nearby door, where he hoped to find reality,

01:12:05.710 --> 01:12:07.450
he was met with a solid wall.

01:12:07.450 --> 01:12:11.910
“I'm trapped in whatever this place is with
this thing, and there's no outside reality

01:12:11.910 --> 01:12:14.540
anymore,” he realized.

01:12:14.540 --> 01:12:20.560
Researcher Talloran tried to understand the
entity, but SCP-3999 defied description, it

01:12:20.560 --> 01:12:22.620
was simply chaos.

01:12:22.620 --> 01:12:27.060
In his desperation, Talloran was able to dig
a tiny hole in the floor, through which he

01:12:27.060 --> 01:12:28.290
could see a light.

01:12:28.290 --> 01:12:32.100
Filled with thoughts of his beloved family,
his colleagues and the rest of the world that

01:12:32.100 --> 01:12:35.100
had once existed, he opened the hole wider.

01:12:35.100 --> 01:12:39.140
It is unclear what happened after this, but
it is apparent that James Talloran was still

01:12:39.140 --> 01:12:42.190
unable to escape SCP-3999.

01:12:42.190 --> 01:12:48.580
However, as the situations he found himself
in grew increasingly strange, James Talloran

01:12:48.580 --> 01:12:51.330
was able to take greater control of his predicament.

01:12:51.330 --> 01:12:55.520
This is evidenced in the Foundation’s document
regarding SCP-3999:

01:12:55.520 --> 01:12:59.680
“Researcher Talloran cannot be contained
by this.

01:12:59.680 --> 01:13:02.340
Researcher Talloran will fight his way back.

01:13:02.340 --> 01:13:08.230
Researcher Talloran will recontain SCP-3999.”

01:13:08.230 --> 01:13:12.620
This torture had gone on long enough, and
Researcher James Talloran was determined to

01:13:12.620 --> 01:13:16.700
be free of the creature that had taken such
sick pleasure in his pain.

01:13:16.700 --> 01:13:23.450
Talloran at one point was able to subdue SCP-3999,
although it is initially unclear how he accomplished

01:13:23.450 --> 01:13:24.570
this.

01:13:24.570 --> 01:13:28.930
There were several attempts made to contain
the entity, using a recursive joke, a melon,

01:13:28.930 --> 01:13:33.880
the graves of American crime novelist Robert
B. Parker or an O5 Council member, and a roach

01:13:33.880 --> 01:13:35.120
motel.

01:13:35.120 --> 01:13:38.900
It is believed that every single one of these
attempts failed.

01:13:38.900 --> 01:13:43.230
Attempting to beat the creature at its own
game, Talloran tried to turn SCP-3999’s

01:13:43.230 --> 01:13:49.580
reality-altering powers against it by imagining
the entity as something easier to subdue.

01:13:49.580 --> 01:13:53.900
These included several mouldy blankets, a
murderous penguin, and a pillow.

01:13:53.900 --> 01:14:00.290
Eventually, Talloran was able to contain SCP-3999
with the aid of forty-eight trained containment

01:14:00.290 --> 01:14:03.680
personnel, all of whom were also Researcher
Talloran.

01:14:03.680 --> 01:14:08.740
During this time, a conversation between the
two was partially recorded, although any dialogue

01:14:08.740 --> 01:14:12.930
from SCP-3999 suffered from data corruption.

01:14:12.930 --> 01:14:18.730
Claiming to finally be in charge and in control
of the situation, Talloran interrogated SCP-3999,

01:14:18.730 --> 01:14:23.280
berating the creature for keeping him prisoner:
“You can't frighten me anymore.

01:14:23.280 --> 01:14:28.180
For the first million years of nonsensical
containment procedures and tortures and dream

01:14:28.180 --> 01:14:32.270
logic, it was the worst pain I had ever felt,
but I survived.

01:14:32.270 --> 01:14:38.480
For the second million years…it was still
the hardest thing I had ever done, but I survived.

01:14:38.480 --> 01:14:43.410
By the third million years, I was growing
numb.”

01:14:43.410 --> 01:14:50.060
The researcher tried to ascertain the exact
nature and origin of SCP-3999, but to no avail.

01:14:50.060 --> 01:14:53.350
Vowing to fight the entity, Talloran told
it:

01:14:53.350 --> 01:14:57.890
“Here's the thing about horror and weirdness:
the more you reveal of it, the less effect

01:14:57.890 --> 01:14:59.050
it has.

01:14:59.050 --> 01:15:01.580
I am sick of your horror.

01:15:01.580 --> 01:15:04.680
I am sick of you.”

01:15:04.680 --> 01:15:08.350
Afterwards, he was melted into goo for five
years.

01:15:08.350 --> 01:15:13.470
It appears that, shortly after this, Researcher
Talloran managed to understand how to destroy

01:15:13.470 --> 01:15:14.470
SCP-3999.

01:15:14.470 --> 01:15:21.370
While the entity was immortal and virtually
unstoppable, he realized its weakness: that

01:15:21.370 --> 01:15:25.080
it had to bond to another being in order to
survive.

01:15:25.080 --> 01:15:31.970
Upon discovering this, Talloran took his own
life, causing all that SCP-3999 had done to

01:15:31.970 --> 01:15:32.970
disappear.

01:15:32.970 --> 01:15:37.060
After Foundation Researcher James Talloran
committed suicide, reality reverted to its

01:15:37.060 --> 01:15:44.830
original state before SCP-3999 had heavily
altered it, his death freeing reality from

01:15:44.830 --> 01:15:47.850
the evil entity once and for all.

01:15:47.850 --> 01:15:54.430
The Foundation remains unaware of what SCP-3999
was, or indeed is, and one can only guess

01:15:54.430 --> 01:15:58.980
as to why the entity subjugated Talloran to
such torture and horror.

01:15:58.980 --> 01:16:04.500
What made this powerful reality-bender hold
such a perverse fixation on one man?

01:16:04.500 --> 01:16:08.330
Some have likened the creature to a horror
writer, continually subjecting a character

01:16:08.330 --> 01:16:13.640
to all manner of nightmares before deciding
to redraft and try something new.

01:16:13.640 --> 01:16:20.770
Whatever it was, SCP-3999 carries with it
a warning that reality isn’t always as it

01:16:20.770 --> 01:16:27.470
seems.
We were a team…despite our differences,

01:16:27.470 --> 01:16:30.500
in spite of the terrible things they’d done,

01:16:30.500 --> 01:16:31.910
we were still a team.

01:16:31.910 --> 01:16:36.240
That’s not how the higher-ups saw it, though..

01:16:36.240 --> 01:16:41.230
No, the guys upstairs with their perfectly
pressed shirts… for them, we were judged

01:16:41.230 --> 01:16:47.010
by our level of expendability and they knew
that our next mission... was a death sentence.

01:16:47.010 --> 01:16:52.660
One by one that… that thing, took out my
team, my friends.

01:16:52.660 --> 01:16:56.750
Snapping their necks so quickly and with such
ease that no sooner did I hear the scream...

01:16:56.750 --> 01:16:58.830
they were dead.

01:16:58.830 --> 01:17:02.980
We had been used…I’d been used.

01:17:02.980 --> 01:17:08.140
Delivered as prey to the predator, a plot
that was sanctioned by the bosses and approved

01:17:08.140 --> 01:17:09.930
with a blood-red stamp.

01:17:09.930 --> 01:17:12.160
Why did they do it?

01:17:12.160 --> 01:17:14.080
I’m still trying to figure that out.

01:17:14.080 --> 01:17:18.770
Maybe that’s something you can tell me after
you hear how these so-called scientific men

01:17:18.770 --> 01:17:24.440
left us in the cell, and in the hands, of
SCP-173.

01:17:24.440 --> 01:17:30.520
For me it had been the best of times before
it became the worst of times.

01:17:30.520 --> 01:17:35.170
The best, because I’d quickly risen through
the ranks at the facility…the worst because,

01:17:35.170 --> 01:17:37.120
well, I’ll get to that.

01:17:37.120 --> 01:17:39.650
I was never the best student.

01:17:39.650 --> 01:17:44.350
I’d finished high-school by the skin of
my teeth, and my job prospects looked bleak.

01:17:44.350 --> 01:17:46.210
But I was lucky I guess…

01:17:46.210 --> 01:17:49.160
Or at least I thought so at the time.

01:17:49.160 --> 01:17:52.320
You see I have an Uncle Siegfried who did
some work for the government.

01:17:52.320 --> 01:17:55.870
I never actually knew what he did, just that
it was secretive work.

01:17:55.870 --> 01:18:00.800
I used to imagine he was some sort of super
spy so you can imagine how excited I was when

01:18:00.800 --> 01:18:03.750
he found out I needed a job and he offered
to help me out.

01:18:03.750 --> 01:18:07.830
I couldn’t believe it, I always thought
he hated me.

01:18:07.830 --> 01:18:11.730
I’d overheard him telling my parents that
I was a no good deadbeat but now he’d had

01:18:11.730 --> 01:18:14.270
a change of heart and was willing to take
me under his wing.

01:18:14.270 --> 01:18:16.390
What would I get to do?

01:18:16.390 --> 01:18:18.100
Undercover intelligence gathering?

01:18:18.100 --> 01:18:19.430
International assassinations?

01:18:19.430 --> 01:18:22.760
“Just you wait,” he said.

01:18:22.760 --> 01:18:28.680
And that’s how I found myself walking into
a sprawling, futuristic-looking facility where

01:18:28.680 --> 01:18:33.160
they handed me a level one security clearance
card with big bold letters that read…

01:18:33.160 --> 01:18:34.160
Janitor.

01:18:34.160 --> 01:18:38.930
But I was happy, just the words “security
clearance” made me feel important and it

01:18:38.930 --> 01:18:40.170
beat flipping burgers.

01:18:40.170 --> 01:18:47.500
I pushed mops, turned off lights, fired-up
generators, clocked in and clocked out, but

01:18:47.500 --> 01:18:52.880
all that time they must have been watching
me, grooming me, waiting for the day they

01:18:52.880 --> 01:18:54.260
could throw me to the wolves.

01:18:54.260 --> 01:18:59.250
I should have known; I’ve always been an
expendable kind of guy.

01:18:59.250 --> 01:19:04.720
After a few years I was called to an office,
and there was a man in a plaid shirt and kind

01:19:04.720 --> 01:19:06.880
of tweed jacket professors wear.

01:19:06.880 --> 01:19:11.040
He asked me, “Do you have any idea about
what we actually do here?”

01:19:11.040 --> 01:19:15.200
And to be honest, I didn’t.

01:19:15.200 --> 01:19:18.750
I knew that there were many parts of the facility
I couldn’t enter.

01:19:18.750 --> 01:19:23.480
I imagined that down the maze of corridors
were weapons being built, or prisoners being

01:19:23.480 --> 01:19:26.460
interrogated, but I had no idea about the
anomalies.

01:19:26.460 --> 01:19:28.120
How could I?

01:19:28.120 --> 01:19:32.040
Before I was told anything I had to sign a
bunch of forms…there were so many I thought

01:19:32.040 --> 01:19:33.880
I’d get to find out who really killed JFK.

01:19:33.880 --> 01:19:39.320
And while they didn’t come out and say it,
what I inferred was that if I ever talk about

01:19:39.320 --> 01:19:44.130
what happens at the facility to someone outside
the facility, well let’s just say it’s

01:19:44.130 --> 01:19:48.130
not the kind of thing they’d spell out on
a piece of paper but it involves padded cells

01:19:48.130 --> 01:19:50.750
and rusty tools.

01:19:50.750 --> 01:19:52.450
I wasn’t scared though.

01:19:52.450 --> 01:19:57.170
I was a part of something big, something secret,
and I loved it.

01:19:57.170 --> 01:20:00.360
So I signed my life away with no hesitation.

01:20:00.360 --> 01:20:05.500
Soon after I was introduced to my first anomaly,
the “Safe” class of course.

01:20:05.500 --> 01:20:09.490
They took me to an observation room and from
that room I could see into another room with

01:20:09.490 --> 01:20:12.140
a sign on the wall that read, “SCP-067.”

01:20:12.140 --> 01:20:17.050
I just stood there, waiting for something
to happen, when in walked another guy in a

01:20:17.050 --> 01:20:18.050
white lab coat.

01:20:18.050 --> 01:20:19.690
“Welcome to your first anomaly,” he said.

01:20:19.690 --> 01:20:22.270
“Is it okay if I hook you up to this heart
monitor?

01:20:22.270 --> 01:20:24.540
We want to gauge your reaction to what you
see.”

01:20:24.540 --> 01:20:28.480
“All I can see,” I told him, “Is an
empty room with a table and what looks like

01:20:28.480 --> 01:20:30.500
a pen on top of some papers.”

01:20:30.500 --> 01:20:36.720
“Correct,” he said, half-smiling as if
I was some kind of idiot, “That’s SCP-067.”

01:20:36.720 --> 01:20:40.860
I thought about telling him that if I needed
years of training before I could see a pen

01:20:40.860 --> 01:20:42.860
then I probably should have taken that fast
food job.

01:20:42.860 --> 01:20:45.100
I could have been shift manager by now.

01:20:45.100 --> 01:20:48.720
They then brought a young chimpanzee into
the room, small enough to be harmless.

01:20:48.720 --> 01:20:53.250
One of the guys forced a pen into the scared
chimp’s hand and something strange happened…

01:20:53.250 --> 01:20:54.820
it started scribbling.

01:20:54.820 --> 01:20:59.590
Nonsense at first, but suddenly it was sketching
and drawing, faster and faster, I could catch

01:20:59.590 --> 01:21:01.350
glimpses of words and images.

01:21:01.350 --> 01:21:04.600
By the time they dragged it out it was flailing
around like it was possessed.

01:21:04.600 --> 01:21:08.850
“That pen has power” said the man in the
lab coat, “a power whose source or origin

01:21:08.850 --> 01:21:10.450
we don’t fully understand.

01:21:10.450 --> 01:21:12.660
That’s why we’re here.

01:21:12.660 --> 01:21:14.250
That’s why you are here.”

01:21:14.250 --> 01:21:17.480
One of the guys in the other room held the
chimp’s drawing up to the window.

01:21:17.480 --> 01:21:22.420
It was a perfect sketch of the Tower of London,
intricate and brilliant.

01:21:22.420 --> 01:21:27.070
Above the sketch was the title, “Tower of
London: Tudor Period, circa 1541, the year

01:21:27.070 --> 01:21:31.730
Margaret Pole, the Countess of Salisbury,
lost her head on the chopping block.”

01:21:31.730 --> 01:21:37.810
Underneath that the chimp had written, “Pity...she
was no traitor, take it from me, I was there.”

01:21:37.810 --> 01:21:43.270
They didn’t need to look at the heart rate
monitor to see that I was shocked.

01:21:43.270 --> 01:21:47.340
That was far from the only anomaly I’d come
into contact with, and I must have been doing

01:21:47.340 --> 01:21:53.270
something right because in time I went from
level 1, to 2, to level 3 security clearance,

01:21:53.270 --> 01:21:55.430
and that’s when they made me a “Containment
Specialist.”

01:21:55.430 --> 01:22:00.130
I won’t bore you with all the details, but
as you can guess, I dealt with the containment

01:22:00.130 --> 01:22:01.250
of anomalies.

01:22:01.250 --> 01:22:05.760
A lot of my time was spent looking through
small windows in cell doors, making sure that

01:22:05.760 --> 01:22:09.830
whatever was inside was still inside and still
in one piece.

01:22:09.830 --> 01:22:14.110
Other times I worked with field agents when
anomalies were brought in, a transition period

01:22:14.110 --> 01:22:17.240
that the arrested freaks didn’t much like.

01:22:17.240 --> 01:22:21.340
There was one certain anomaly though, that
I was tasked to oversee on many occasions.

01:22:21.340 --> 01:22:29.600
I liked to think of it as my pet, but in hindsight,
I was its pet.

01:22:29.600 --> 01:22:34.850
This was SCP-173, something that was in what
we call the Euclid class, a classification

01:22:34.850 --> 01:22:40.070
meaning that we don’t fully understand it,
but know is very dangerous.

01:22:40.070 --> 01:22:45.600
We know it’s intelligent, we know it’s
unpredictable... and we know it will kill.

01:22:45.600 --> 01:22:49.450
And for that reason, there’s people tasked
with containing it and keeping an eye on it

01:22:49.450 --> 01:22:51.840
at all times.

01:22:51.840 --> 01:22:55.240
At first glance, you wouldn’t guess just
how dangerous 173 is.

01:22:55.240 --> 01:23:00.040
You wouldn’t think it’s incredibly intelligent…
in fact you’d probably think the opposite.

01:23:00.040 --> 01:23:04.710
That’s because it’s more or less a walking
slab of concrete and rebar with stunted limbs

01:23:04.710 --> 01:23:08.370
and traces of spray paint that give the impression
of a dopey face.

01:23:08.370 --> 01:23:11.290
We have to enter its cell twice a week for
cleaning duties.

01:23:11.290 --> 01:23:16.250
It leaves a disgusting, foul smelling liquid
on the floor, a reddish brown substance that

01:23:16.250 --> 01:23:19.330
I can only describe as a mix of blood and
waste products.

01:23:19.330 --> 01:23:25.470
Where that stuff comes from has remained a
mystery since we first contained it in 1993.

01:23:25.470 --> 01:23:29.660
Going into the cell was always a three man
job because, and this is maybe the weirdest

01:23:29.660 --> 01:23:33.700
part about 173, it can’t move if human eyes
are watching it.

01:23:33.700 --> 01:23:36.380
That’s why you need at least two people
watching it at all times.

01:23:36.380 --> 01:23:41.600
If you were in the room watching 173 by yourself
and blinked you’d be dead before your eyes

01:23:41.600 --> 01:23:42.600
opened.

01:23:42.600 --> 01:23:46.370
We don’t know how it moves that fast but
in that fraction of a second of a second your

01:23:46.370 --> 01:23:48.970
neck is snapped so hard it’s almost like
being decapitated.

01:23:48.970 --> 01:23:51.390
I’ve seen the videos to prove it.

01:23:51.390 --> 01:23:53.430
All it took was a sneeze.

01:23:53.430 --> 01:23:57.540
He wasn’t even finished getting the rest
of the ACHOO out when there was a flash and

01:23:57.540 --> 01:24:02.320
his partner was left lying on the ground,
his head twisted around the wrong direction.

01:24:02.320 --> 01:24:07.970
So, you can understand why we now require
three men for any time we must enter 173’s

01:24:07.970 --> 01:24:09.020
cell.

01:24:09.020 --> 01:24:14.370
Then a few months ago I was told that a long
process would begin to train and reeducate

01:24:14.370 --> 01:24:16.050
some future Class Ds.

01:24:16.050 --> 01:24:20.620
Class Ds are mostly prisoners with life-long
sentences or those we’ve taken from death

01:24:20.620 --> 01:24:22.430
row and given a new lease on life.

01:24:22.430 --> 01:24:27.040
We were apparently understaffed, so why not
employ men whose lives had pretty much ended

01:24:27.040 --> 01:24:28.040
anyway?

01:24:28.040 --> 01:24:30.870
That was the rationale, or at least that’s
what they told me.

01:24:30.870 --> 01:24:35.930
I was to train them on their new job... mopping
up 173’s mess, so that me and the rest of

01:24:35.930 --> 01:24:39.190
the containment specialists could focus on
more important tasks.

01:24:39.190 --> 01:24:44.170
They hadn’t been through the training I
had, seen what I had seen, but after showing

01:24:44.170 --> 01:24:48.930
them the video of 173 nearly taking off a
man’s head they were more than willing to

01:24:48.930 --> 01:24:50.441
follow the rules.

01:24:50.441 --> 01:24:55.200
They understood not to blink, or turn away,
or sneeze, and that any lapse in focus could

01:24:55.200 --> 01:24:57.180
lead to a violent death.

01:24:57.180 --> 01:25:01.390
So I started to show them the ropes, how we
move as a team into the cell and always keep

01:25:01.390 --> 01:25:03.900
the others informed on what we’re doing.

01:25:03.900 --> 01:25:09.220
173 was always sitting in the corner of the
cell, no expression on that crude face, but

01:25:09.220 --> 01:25:13.240
when we walked in its cell I got the feeling
it knew something had changed.

01:25:13.240 --> 01:25:17.890
I felt almost as if it was communicating with
me, but I couldn’t tell what it was trying

01:25:17.890 --> 01:25:19.070
to say.

01:25:19.070 --> 01:25:21.950
And then it happened.

01:25:21.950 --> 01:25:26.260
It was a Tuesday afternoon, three days from
the last time we’d cleaned.

01:25:26.260 --> 01:25:29.630
As usual, 173 had covered the floor with that
horrible liquid.

01:25:29.630 --> 01:25:34.450
We headed in to clean, my new team alert as
always, and some of them cleaned while others

01:25:34.450 --> 01:25:37.420
kept their eyes focused on the thing in the
corner.

01:25:37.420 --> 01:25:40.280
Things were going smoothly when we heard a
noise I knew very well.

01:25:40.280 --> 01:25:44.490
It was the sound of the cell door locking.

01:25:44.490 --> 01:25:45.670
Someone must have screwed up.

01:25:45.670 --> 01:25:49.010
“Hey guys, we’re locked in here,” I
shouted through the intercom.

01:25:49.010 --> 01:25:50.010
Nothing.

01:25:50.010 --> 01:25:52.120
“Guys, the damn door is locked.”

01:25:52.120 --> 01:25:53.120
Nothing.

01:25:53.120 --> 01:25:55.740
I lost it a bit.

01:25:55.740 --> 01:25:58.000
“Open the door, will you!”

01:25:58.000 --> 01:25:59.170
Nothing.

01:25:59.170 --> 01:26:04.850
My team looked at me, the ones not on eye
contact duty that is, as if I should know

01:26:04.850 --> 01:26:08.780
what to do…hoping that this had happened
before and that there was some kind of standard

01:26:08.780 --> 01:26:10.500
plan to deal with it.

01:26:10.500 --> 01:26:11.500
There wasn’t.

01:26:11.500 --> 01:26:15.760
We were always observed when in the room and
I knew that a technician couldn’t accidentally

01:26:15.760 --> 01:26:16.790
lock the door.

01:26:16.790 --> 01:26:18.330
It was impossible.

01:26:18.330 --> 01:26:20.040
There were protocols.

01:26:20.040 --> 01:26:21.040
Someone had done this on purpose.

01:26:21.040 --> 01:26:26.580
The four of us sat in the corner of the room
as far from 173 as possible, our eyes locked

01:26:26.580 --> 01:26:27.580
on it.

01:26:27.580 --> 01:26:33.150
It didn’t move an inch as usual, just stood,
staring at the wall as it always did.

01:26:33.150 --> 01:26:37.660
We stayed awake through the night, talking
a little, holding on to the slim hope that

01:26:37.660 --> 01:26:39.770
something had gone wrong.

01:26:39.770 --> 01:26:43.710
But as night turned to day again we all began
to lose hope.

01:26:43.710 --> 01:26:46.850
We weren’t sent here to clean.

01:26:46.850 --> 01:26:49.650
We were a test… totally expendable… lab
rats.

01:26:49.650 --> 01:26:51.960
But I wouldn’t go down without a fight.

01:26:51.960 --> 01:26:54.860
We couldn’t just stay up forever, that was
a death sentence.

01:26:54.860 --> 01:26:58.940
I suggested that two of us stand, one sit
and rest, and one get some sleep.

01:26:58.940 --> 01:27:00.120
We’d take shifts.

01:27:00.120 --> 01:27:04.840
A couple hours on, a couple hours off, maybe
if we could show that we wouldn’t give up

01:27:04.840 --> 01:27:08.930
they’d have time to realize what they were
doing was insane, call off the test, and come

01:27:08.930 --> 01:27:10.000
free us.

01:27:10.000 --> 01:27:13.280
We made it through a couple of shifts like
this and it seemed like we’d actually be

01:27:13.280 --> 01:27:17.030
able to make it another day or two when everything
went wrong.

01:27:17.030 --> 01:27:23.270
It was my turn to sit and rest when I heard
the worst possible noise… snoring.

01:27:23.270 --> 01:27:26.920
The con next to me was sleeping quietly so
it must be one of the standers.

01:27:26.920 --> 01:27:31.460
I glanced over for just a split second and
saw both of them, leaning against the cell

01:27:31.460 --> 01:27:32.460
wall, dozing.

01:27:32.460 --> 01:27:35.380
At the same time I saw the flash.

01:27:35.380 --> 01:27:37.940
Crack, snap, pop.

01:27:37.940 --> 01:27:40.780
One after another their necks were snapped.

01:27:40.780 --> 01:27:46.840
I’m not sure how it happened but I was standing
again, staring at 173 who was now in the corner,

01:27:46.840 --> 01:27:50.440
dead bodies with their heads twisted around
piled up in front of it.

01:27:50.440 --> 01:27:55.070
I couldn’t take it anymore, I couldn’t
stare at this thing forever, I felt myself

01:27:55.070 --> 01:27:56.070
giving up.

01:27:56.070 --> 01:28:02.530
I lowered my head to the ground and then finally
broke my gaze, ready to die.

01:28:02.530 --> 01:28:04.570
And then… nothing happened.

01:28:04.570 --> 01:28:10.010
I slowly raised my head back up and -
There it was, it’s hideous face inches away

01:28:10.010 --> 01:28:11.010
from mine.

01:28:11.010 --> 01:28:16.280
It was then that I understood what we’d
been containing, what we’d underestimated.

01:28:16.280 --> 01:28:18.820
I felt again like it was telling me something.

01:28:18.820 --> 01:28:23.560
It was telling me to close my eyes, to sleep.

01:28:23.560 --> 01:28:25.610
So I did.

01:28:25.610 --> 01:28:32.330
But as my eyes closed I didn’t see darkness,
I saw 173, or something like it.

01:28:32.330 --> 01:28:38.950
But not in the cell, I saw it outside, in
the world, standing over children sleeping

01:28:38.950 --> 01:28:41.520
in their beds, watching.

01:28:41.520 --> 01:28:45.980
I saw them hiding in the shadows, staring
out at passers by.

01:28:45.980 --> 01:28:51.210
Then I realized they weren’t watching, waiting
to pounce.

01:28:51.210 --> 01:28:53.470
No, they were hiding.

01:28:53.470 --> 01:28:57.610
My eyes popped open as the door opened and
in rushed six security personnel.

01:28:57.610 --> 01:29:03.800
They took me outside, jabbed my leg with a
syringe injecting me with something as the

01:29:03.800 --> 01:29:07.650
world faded away…

01:29:07.650 --> 01:29:08.650
Incident Report.

01:29:08.650 --> 01:29:10.030
Time and date redacted.

01:29:10.030 --> 01:29:15.271
Following the experimental forced interaction
with Euclid Class anomaly SCP-173, subject

01:29:15.271 --> 01:29:19.030
has ceased responding to external stimuli
and appears to have taken on the traits and

01:29:19.030 --> 01:29:20.840
behaviors of the anomaly.

01:29:20.840 --> 01:29:24.920
Subject now spends entire day sitting in corner
of cell staring at wall.

01:29:24.920 --> 01:29:28.400
Staff are advised to proceed with caution
when dealing with subject as the only behavior

01:29:28.400 --> 01:29:32.090
they engage in is an attempt to strangle anyone
who enters the cell.

01:29:32.090 --> 01:29:36.840
No treatments have shown any effectiveness
and subject will unfortunately require incarceration,

01:29:36.840 --> 01:29:43.420
likely forever.
Mobile Task Force Edna-84, also known by the

01:29:43.420 --> 01:29:45.530
code name “And Thus Upon His Crucible”,

01:29:45.530 --> 01:29:49.020
is on one of the strangest missions they’ve
ever been sent on.

01:29:49.020 --> 01:29:53.990
They sit in a darkened room, beads of sweat
trickling down their brows.

01:29:53.990 --> 01:29:57.270
The lights of computer monitors shine up into
their faces.

01:29:57.270 --> 01:30:03.610
They’re tackling a monster that, if it escapes,
will literally devour all of creation.

01:30:03.610 --> 01:30:10.250
It has no fear, it has no remorse, and if
it isn’t kept contained by Edna-84, it may

01:30:10.250 --> 01:30:13.220
escape and wreak havoc across the world.

01:30:13.220 --> 01:30:17.170
Oh, and did we mention it’s trapped inside
a Minecraft server?

01:30:17.170 --> 01:30:25.050
You heard us right: SCP-4335 is a cognitohazardous,
extradimensional being of pure terror, trapped

01:30:25.050 --> 01:30:30.250
inside a procedurally generated world in the
immensely popular game Minecraft.

01:30:30.250 --> 01:30:34.870
You hear about a lot of horrifying monsters
being described as Lovecraftian, but only

01:30:34.870 --> 01:30:37.040
this monster is Minecraftian.

01:30:37.040 --> 01:30:42.590
There’s a lot that needs explaining here,
and we intend to get to all of it, but let’s

01:30:42.590 --> 01:30:46.640
start with how the Foundation keeps this truly
exceptional beast contained.

01:30:46.640 --> 01:30:53.600
The Foundation first discovered SCP-4335 back
in 2010, shortly after the Alpha Version of

01:30:53.600 --> 01:30:55.690
Minecraft was released.

01:30:55.690 --> 01:30:59.920
Because of the online nature of the game,
it’s proven to be impossible to contain

01:30:59.920 --> 01:31:05.630
4335 externally, seeing as it isn’t confined
to any one physical location.

01:31:05.630 --> 01:31:11.590
Shutting off the server 4335 is found in is
not an effective containment method either,

01:31:11.590 --> 01:31:15.630
as this just causes the entity to simply hop
to a different server.

01:31:15.630 --> 01:31:21.220
The Foundation’s greatest fear is that 4335
will one day make the leap out of Minecraft

01:31:21.220 --> 01:31:23.040
and cause chaos in our world.

01:31:23.040 --> 01:31:30.080
That’s why it’s imperative to draft Containment
Procedures for SCP-4335 within the game.

01:31:30.080 --> 01:31:35.890
This led to the creation of a containment
site unlike any other: Site-M1, the first

01:31:35.890 --> 01:31:40.440
official Foundation Site made entirely out
of Minecraft blocks.

01:31:40.440 --> 01:31:45.270
Everyone who was originally involved in the
server has been removed and amnesthetized.

01:31:45.270 --> 01:31:50.120
Since then, elite SCP Foundation Containment
Specialists and Gamers have been constructing

01:31:50.120 --> 01:31:53.270
the perfect prison for SCP-4335.

01:31:53.270 --> 01:31:58.630
Site-M1 is a large, stone complex built into
the side of a mountain.

01:31:58.630 --> 01:32:03.320
It has a number of key features - including
a supply area filled with materials vital

01:32:03.320 --> 01:32:06.410
for 4335’s continued containment.

01:32:06.410 --> 01:32:11.770
Chests filled with books that contain SCP-4335's
containment procedures.

01:32:11.770 --> 01:32:16.520
A few animal farms, for the purpose of breeding
and killing livestock for their meat.

01:32:16.520 --> 01:32:18.360
The entrance to a mine.

01:32:18.360 --> 01:32:23.520
Several chests containing books specifically
designed for civilians to learn about 4335,

01:32:23.520 --> 01:32:26.650
if they ever breach containment and enter
the server.

01:32:26.650 --> 01:32:34.000
And of course, the actual containment chamber
for SCP-4335 itself, which is a little more

01:32:34.000 --> 01:32:35.210
complicated.

01:32:35.210 --> 01:32:41.610
4335’s containment chamber is built out
of several layers, all made from iron blocks.

01:32:41.610 --> 01:32:50.650
The outermost cube is 75 x 75 x 75 blocks,
the inner cube is 55 x 55 x 55 blocks, and

01:32:50.650 --> 01:32:57.750
the innermost cube is 25 x 25 x 25 blocks,
creating several levels of defense.

01:32:57.750 --> 01:33:02.670
Finally one layer within all these others,
which contains the anomaly proper, is made

01:33:02.670 --> 01:33:04.980
from obsidian blocks.

01:33:04.980 --> 01:33:10.460
SCP-4335 is bound into the center of the containment
chamber with a complex mechanism.

01:33:10.460 --> 01:33:15.230
The outermost cube is completely filled with
water, and several dispensers capable of rapidly

01:33:15.230 --> 01:33:19.420
dispensing large amounts of items in a short
amount of time line the cube.

01:33:19.420 --> 01:33:24.400
The cube also contains several mob farms,
which are devices that constantly spawn enemies

01:33:24.400 --> 01:33:27.420
into the chamber that drop loot when they
die.

01:33:27.420 --> 01:33:33.120
The chamber is essentially designed to funnel
a constant flow of items inwards to SCP-4335.

01:33:33.120 --> 01:33:40.840
There are even contingency measures for if
SCP-4335 manages to breach containment.

01:33:40.840 --> 01:33:46.670
If escape is attempted, blocks of TNT detonate
above the ceiling, causing lava to pour into

01:33:46.670 --> 01:33:47.670
the chamber.

01:33:47.670 --> 01:33:53.610
At that point, MTF Edna-84 are dispatched
into the server to lure 4335 back into its

01:33:53.610 --> 01:33:54.840
containment chamber.

01:33:54.840 --> 01:34:00.630
To do this, they use a mix of fire-resistance
potions and Ender Pearls - which have teleportation

01:34:00.630 --> 01:34:01.630
capabilities.

01:34:01.630 --> 01:34:06.900
Interestingly, there’s one more basic method
of luring SCP-4335 back into the obsidian

01:34:06.900 --> 01:34:10.040
cube: Taunting and insulting.

01:34:10.040 --> 01:34:16.880
One of SCP-4335’s many anomalous abilities
is being able to hear people through the screen,

01:34:16.880 --> 01:34:23.730
and - being a proud creature - it often responds
to insults by charging in to directly engage

01:34:23.730 --> 01:34:24.730
the insulter.

01:34:24.730 --> 01:34:30.251
If SCP-4335 still manages to breach the containment
that has been set up for it, then they expect

01:34:30.251 --> 01:34:32.930
that it will likely soon hop into another
server.

01:34:32.930 --> 01:34:37.730
At that point, the goal will shift to finding
the monster and recontaining it.

01:34:37.730 --> 01:34:42.500
With Containment Procedures this complex and
extensive, it shouldn’t come as a surprise

01:34:42.500 --> 01:34:47.130
that SCP-4335 rests firmly in the Keter Class.

01:34:47.130 --> 01:34:50.090
What exactly is this anomalous entity?

01:34:50.090 --> 01:34:53.980
Why does its containment hinge on constantly
providing it with items?

01:34:53.980 --> 01:35:00.120
And how did it end up in Mojang’s popular
building and survival game in the first place?

01:35:00.120 --> 01:35:06.190
SCP-4335, in terms of physical dimensions,
appears almost identical to the player character,

01:35:06.190 --> 01:35:07.940
with an all-black skin.

01:35:07.940 --> 01:35:13.360
It also appears to be constantly shrouded
in a cloud of smoke particles, and has long,

01:35:13.360 --> 01:35:15.860
black tendrils protruding from its back.

01:35:15.860 --> 01:35:22.260
In some respects, 4335 has been compared to
two popular creepypasta figures: Herobrine

01:35:22.260 --> 01:35:23.730
and The Slenderman.

01:35:23.730 --> 01:35:26.630
But 4335 is far stranger.

01:35:26.630 --> 01:35:32.630
Its physical body behaves similarly to most
assets native to the game, with a few peculiar

01:35:32.630 --> 01:35:35.920
anomalous abilities we’ll discuss soon.

01:35:35.920 --> 01:35:40.250
Handling SCP-4335 is an extremely delicate
process.

01:35:40.250 --> 01:35:44.780
If command blocks, creative mode, or server
commands are ever enabled in a server with

01:35:44.780 --> 01:35:54.130
SCP-4335, the server will instantly shut down,
and SCP-4335 will move to a different server.

01:35:54.130 --> 01:36:00.050
SCP-4335 also uses its tendrils to destroy
surrounding blocks before consuming them.

01:36:00.050 --> 01:36:07.250
With each successful consumption, SCP-4335
grows, and when it reaches sufficient size,

01:36:07.250 --> 01:36:10.880
it hops to a different server.

01:36:10.880 --> 01:36:15.250
As you can see, keeping SCP-4335 contained
is an uphill battle, but luckily there are

01:36:15.250 --> 01:36:18.130
two factors on the Foundation’s side here.

01:36:18.130 --> 01:36:24.420
The first is that SCP-4335 is immobilized
while consuming items and blocks, limiting

01:36:24.420 --> 01:36:27.880
its ability to actively escape Foundation
forces.

01:36:27.880 --> 01:36:33.480
The second factor is that SCP-4335 needs a
rest period between consuming blocks in order

01:36:33.480 --> 01:36:40.120
to grow, meaning, if its consumption is constant
and continuous it isn’t able to grow.

01:36:40.120 --> 01:36:44.190
These two factors have informed the entirety
of the Foundation’s Containment Procedures

01:36:44.190 --> 01:36:51.730
around SCP-4335: It’s locked into its chamber
and fed items and blocks constantly, effectively

01:36:51.730 --> 01:36:53.460
rooting it in place.

01:36:53.460 --> 01:36:59.420
When SCP-4335 begins to grow, the Foundation
also found that the application of Ender Pearls

01:36:59.420 --> 01:37:02.820
helps reduce it back to its normal size.

01:37:02.820 --> 01:37:08.780
However, SCP-4335 does have a method of striking
back against its captors.

01:37:08.780 --> 01:37:13.730
4335 is Vurgent-class multi-sensory cognitohazard.

01:37:13.730 --> 01:37:17.840
Anyone viewing it without proper training
and protection may experience distressing

01:37:17.840 --> 01:37:19.010
hallucinations.

01:37:19.010 --> 01:37:26.410
SCP-4335 is also capable of telepathic speech
with people playing on its server, and - as

01:37:26.410 --> 01:37:31.300
we alluded to earlier - it can also hear any
noises you make while playing.

01:37:31.300 --> 01:37:36.160
“Weird” is a term thrown around a lot
when it comes to SCPs, considering it’s

01:37:36.160 --> 01:37:39.730
pretty much a requirement for the Foundation
to take interest in you.

01:37:39.730 --> 01:37:43.770
But an all-devouring Minecraft demon that
can hear you talking through your screen is

01:37:43.770 --> 01:37:47.570
strange even by Foundation standards.

01:37:47.570 --> 01:37:54.900
MTF Edna-84 first discovered SCP-4335 in the
single-player server of Minecraft user Leakingheart.

01:37:54.900 --> 01:38:00.410
Three team members - Jason Yelsan, Richard
Duchamp, and Sheila Freemason - covertly entered

01:38:00.410 --> 01:38:03.910
the game to investigate and potentially apprehend
the creature.

01:38:03.910 --> 01:38:08.470
When Leakingheart first discovered their presence
on what he thought was a private server, he

01:38:08.470 --> 01:38:11.890
quickly left, a little creeped out by the
sudden intrusion.

01:38:11.890 --> 01:38:15.590
Thankfully, the trio was still able to locate
SCP-4335.

01:38:15.590 --> 01:38:21.490
They discovered the creature hiding inside
a giant crater, as though it had impacted

01:38:21.490 --> 01:38:24.040
the earth at considerable speed.

01:38:24.040 --> 01:38:28.230
Richard Duchamp, who was the leader of the
team at the time, made the mistake of looking

01:38:28.230 --> 01:38:29.860
directly at the entity.

01:38:29.860 --> 01:38:35.920
In that moment, he experienced the full force
of SCP-4335’s cognitohazardous effects.

01:38:35.920 --> 01:38:41.210
He hallucinated, believing that his keyboard
was melting before his eyes.

01:38:41.210 --> 01:38:46.320
In the aftermath of this incident, Duchamp
was taken off the case, and Jason Yelsan was

01:38:46.320 --> 01:38:49.080
promoted to head of the project.

01:38:49.080 --> 01:38:52.250
Things were still going to get stranger.

01:38:52.250 --> 01:38:56.070
Yelsan was able to open a dialogue with the
creature after containing it in a chamber

01:38:56.070 --> 01:38:57.350
filled with lava.

01:38:57.350 --> 01:39:02.680
It asked him whether it had landed in the
right location - meaning, our world - and

01:39:02.680 --> 01:39:07.380
Yelsan informed it that it’d somehow fallen
into the world of Minecraft instead.

01:39:07.380 --> 01:39:12.920
The creature was at first confused, and then
angry and resentful about its situation.

01:39:12.920 --> 01:39:18.540
It vowed to find its way into our world somehow,
and obtain more “sustenance.”

01:39:18.540 --> 01:39:23.170
A few months after this, the entity managed
to breach containment and hop into another

01:39:23.170 --> 01:39:24.170
server.

01:39:24.170 --> 01:39:28.880
Yelsan and two others once again managed to
track it down and recontain it, but this time,

01:39:28.880 --> 01:39:34.300
two civilians also inhabiting the server were
exposed to the anomalous effects of SCP-4335.

01:39:34.300 --> 01:39:41.520
They weren’t hurt, but they did appear strange
and incoherent after experiencing 4335’s

01:39:41.520 --> 01:39:42.520
cognitohazards.

01:39:42.520 --> 01:39:48.670
The Foundation tracked them down in the aftermath
and gave them amnestic treatment.

01:39:48.670 --> 01:39:52.000
4335 was contained shortly thereafter.

01:39:52.000 --> 01:39:55.250
Once again, Yelsan came face to face with
his new foe.

01:39:55.250 --> 01:40:02.010
Eight months after being captured, 4335 granted
Yelsan another interview from containment.

01:40:02.010 --> 01:40:07.550
4335 admitted that it almost respected Yelsan
and the rest of the Foundation for figuring

01:40:07.550 --> 01:40:10.570
out how to capture and contain it so quickly.

01:40:10.570 --> 01:40:15.080
In exchange, it would give the Foundation
something extremely valuable: Information.

01:40:15.080 --> 01:40:21.660
First, it asked one question of Yelsan: How
does he define “creation?”

01:40:21.660 --> 01:40:26.650
Yelsan replied, “Uh… something that is
built and brought to this universe by a sapient

01:40:26.650 --> 01:40:29.560
being, using other things from this universe?”

01:40:29.560 --> 01:40:31.180
4335 agreed.

01:40:31.180 --> 01:40:36.150
It went on to explain that it came from a
universe devoid of creation.

01:40:36.150 --> 01:40:41.910
A dark and unknowable place, filled with nothing
but violent, random chaos.

01:40:41.910 --> 01:40:47.610
Its dimension existed directly above ours,
and it often looked down at us through a dimensional

01:40:47.610 --> 01:40:51.240
window, fascinated by all the creation below.

01:40:51.240 --> 01:40:57.050
It plotted and dreamed to one day infiltrate
our reality, and Yelsan finally had the opportunity

01:40:57.050 --> 01:41:00.520
to ask the magic question, “Why?”

01:41:00.520 --> 01:41:05.430
Though he wasn’t quite ready for the brutal
honesty of SCP-4335’s reply.

01:41:05.430 --> 01:41:11.800
“I do not like to lie, so I will tell you
now.

01:41:11.800 --> 01:41:17.780
I wish to suck it dry of the toys of whatever
force controls your universe.

01:41:17.780 --> 01:41:23.890
Destroy the light, destroy the Earth, and
destroy humanity.

01:41:23.890 --> 01:41:26.640
It reminded me… of me.

01:41:26.640 --> 01:41:30.950
A blubbering mass of intelligence and order.

01:41:30.950 --> 01:41:34.400
It sickens me in ways I cannot comprehend.

01:41:34.400 --> 01:41:38.480
I hope you understand.”

01:41:38.480 --> 01:41:44.380
SCP-4335 was a connoisseur of creation, and
it sought to devour all of it.

01:41:44.380 --> 01:41:49.980
In this moment, Yelsan realized what an incredibly
dangerous entity he was dealing with.

01:41:49.980 --> 01:41:55.140
The only mystery was why this creature had
somehow landed in Minecraft instead of our

01:41:55.140 --> 01:41:59.240
world, which appeared to be the only thing
that saved us.

01:41:59.240 --> 01:42:05.860
But Yelsan didn’t have time to think: 4335
was about to stage another daring escape attempt.

01:42:05.860 --> 01:42:09.480
One of its tendrils reached out and attacked
Yelsan’s player character.

01:42:09.480 --> 01:42:13.750
In that moment, the real life Yelsan began
to hallucinate and panic.

01:42:13.750 --> 01:42:19.680
Suddenly, back in the game, a series of abnormally
tall, slender black figures appeared and began

01:42:19.680 --> 01:42:24.850
deconstructing the containment chamber around
SCP-4335.

01:42:24.850 --> 01:42:29.980
It had somehow summoned new minions into the
game to assist in this containment breach.

01:42:29.980 --> 01:42:35.160
Jason Yelsan entered cognitohazard quarantine
following this incident, and was removed from

01:42:35.160 --> 01:42:36.160
the project.

01:42:36.160 --> 01:42:39.730
And he wasn’t the only one affected by this
incident.

01:42:39.730 --> 01:42:43.940
Following the first appearance of these long,
dark figures, players across the globe began

01:42:43.940 --> 01:42:47.630
to experience them appearing in their own
games.

01:42:47.630 --> 01:42:52.360
The Foundation managed to find a solution:
They contacted Mojang and had the creatures

01:42:52.360 --> 01:42:57.930
patched into the game during the next update,
as a new non-anomalous entity, which seemed

01:42:57.930 --> 01:43:02.190
to stop 4335 from being able to use them as
its own tools.

01:43:02.190 --> 01:43:07.440
They’re now known as the Endermen, and are
beloved among fans for being one of the creepier

01:43:07.440 --> 01:43:08.830
enemies.

01:43:08.830 --> 01:43:13.000
To this day, containment efforts continue
for SCP-4335.

01:43:13.000 --> 01:43:19.360
There’s only one question left: Why did
the entity fall into Minecraft rather than

01:43:19.360 --> 01:43:20.360
our world?

01:43:20.360 --> 01:43:23.280
The file posits the most likely answer.

01:43:23.280 --> 01:43:29.030
Because 4335 defines creation as elements
made by sapient beings.

01:43:29.030 --> 01:43:34.940
In Minecraft, the most popular game in the
world at the height of 4335’s power, everything

01:43:34.940 --> 01:43:39.400
that exists is the product of code made by
humans.

01:43:39.400 --> 01:43:41.460
Creation is truly abundant there.

01:43:41.460 --> 01:43:49.940
As for our world, in SCP-4335’s extra-dimensional
eyes, there is no creation, no intelligent

01:43:49.940 --> 01:43:53.050
design, no soothing piano soundtrack.

01:43:53.050 --> 01:43:58.130
Just frightening, chaotic, randomness, which
is too unlike its own dimension.

01:43:58.130 --> 01:44:04.250
So even if SCP-4335 ever did arrive in our
reality, it would likely be disappointed by

01:44:04.250 --> 01:44:07.680
how little there is to eat.

01:44:07.680 --> 01:44:14.300
It’s the late 90's, and an Air Canada flight
experiences severe malfunctions while traveling

01:44:14.300 --> 01:44:16.110
from London to Vancouver.

01:44:16.110 --> 01:44:21.110
The pilots are unable to do anything and the
plane crashes into the woods of Northern Alberta.

01:44:21.110 --> 01:44:26.720
The crash was devastating - only 10 of the
nearly 300 people on board are alive.

01:44:26.720 --> 01:44:31.440
And even though they survived the initial
disaster, their battle for life has only just

01:44:31.440 --> 01:44:32.440
begun.

01:44:32.440 --> 01:44:36.290
It’s late autumn in Northern Canada and
there’s no telling when help will arrive,

01:44:36.290 --> 01:44:37.470
if at all.

01:44:37.470 --> 01:44:42.840
If the survivors want to make it through the
night they need to find shelter, and fast.

01:44:42.840 --> 01:44:46.460
As they trudge through the freezing woods,
the group finds a path that looks like it

01:44:46.460 --> 01:44:48.900
might lead them to civilization.

01:44:48.900 --> 01:44:53.130
After all, if there was a path in the woods,
that meant they were probably in a national

01:44:53.130 --> 01:44:57.070
park, and if they were in a national park,
there had to be a ranger's station around

01:44:57.070 --> 01:44:59.690
somewhere where they could warm up and call
for help.

01:44:59.690 --> 01:45:03.630
They didn’t have many other options, so
they followed the path which opened up to

01:45:03.630 --> 01:45:08.331
a clearing, but instead of finding a ranger's
station or campground, they found something

01:45:08.331 --> 01:45:10.100
none of them could have expected.

01:45:10.100 --> 01:45:13.650
It was a pond, but there was something off
about it.

01:45:13.650 --> 01:45:19.460
As they got closer they saw that this strange
pond wasn’t filled with water, but blood.

01:45:19.460 --> 01:45:21.130
The survivors were horrified.

01:45:21.130 --> 01:45:23.380
That couldn't really be blood, could it?

01:45:23.380 --> 01:45:26.110
It must be a weird algae or a chemical reaction.

01:45:26.110 --> 01:45:30.480
But one member of the group, a man named Thomas
Dean, who had been on his way back to his

01:45:30.480 --> 01:45:35.390
hometown of Prince George, British Columbia,
thought there was something strangely familiar

01:45:35.390 --> 01:45:36.390
about this.

01:45:36.390 --> 01:45:41.190
He remembered being a boy and going to visit
family in Alberta, and hearing an urban legend

01:45:41.190 --> 01:45:42.910
from the older local kids.

01:45:42.910 --> 01:45:47.640
According to the stories, somewhere out in
the wilderness, in the northern part of the

01:45:47.640 --> 01:45:53.111
province, there was a pond full of human blood,
and what made it even worse, was that some

01:45:53.111 --> 01:45:56.420
said the pond was a gateway to hell.

01:45:56.420 --> 01:46:00.580
The SCP Foundation was also aware of this
legend, and had been trying to pinpoint the

01:46:00.580 --> 01:46:04.580
exact source of it for decades prior to the
Air Canada crash.

01:46:04.580 --> 01:46:09.160
They would finally receive definite confirmation
of the blood pond when Foundation personnel

01:46:09.160 --> 01:46:14.360
intercepted a radio transmission from a ranger's
station located within the Wood Buffalo National

01:46:14.360 --> 01:46:15.360
Park.

01:46:15.360 --> 01:46:19.500
It was the survivors of the crash, who had
managed to make it through the night, and

01:46:19.500 --> 01:46:22.500
they were about to be escorted out of the
park by rangers.

01:46:22.500 --> 01:46:26.580
The Foundation mobilized quickly to cordon
off the pond, as at this time they were unsure

01:46:26.580 --> 01:46:29.570
of what potentially harmful properties the
pond might have had.

01:46:29.570 --> 01:46:34.870
They set up Watch Station Epsilon-38 and put
staff on guard to deter travellers from the

01:46:34.870 --> 01:46:35.870
area.

01:46:35.870 --> 01:46:40.880
The pond was given the designation of SCP-354
and classed as Euclid.

01:46:40.880 --> 01:46:45.730
Foundation scientists made a number of interesting
discoveries about SCP-354 when they collected

01:46:45.730 --> 01:46:47.250
samples for testing.

01:46:47.250 --> 01:46:52.860
First, the pond was not in fact full of blood,
merely an inorganic liquid that closely resembles

01:46:52.860 --> 01:46:55.200
blood in color and consistently.

01:46:55.200 --> 01:47:00.191
Second, and even stranger than the red liquid,
is that the pond doesn't seem to have definite

01:47:00.191 --> 01:47:01.590
banks or a bottom.

01:47:01.590 --> 01:47:06.270
Instead, the liquid in the pond increases
in density as the radius away from the center

01:47:06.270 --> 01:47:11.650
increases - the liquid congeals at the edges,
becoming more solid and blending into the

01:47:11.650 --> 01:47:12.660
surrounding soil.

01:47:12.660 --> 01:47:17.570
It also becomes thicker as one descends deeper
into the pool, and a bottom of the pond has

01:47:17.570 --> 01:47:20.730
not yet been reached, if it even exists.

01:47:20.730 --> 01:47:24.000
Initially, the foundation found no signs of
life within the blood pond.

01:47:24.000 --> 01:47:29.710
But that would all change at 2:03 PM on the
day following the opening of Watch Station

01:47:29.710 --> 01:47:34.470
Epsilon-38, when the science team noticed
an unusual level of activity on the pond's

01:47:34.470 --> 01:47:35.470
surface.

01:47:35.470 --> 01:47:39.710
Security footage feeds showed a shape rising
up out of the pond, followed by a deafening

01:47:39.710 --> 01:47:40.710
shriek.

01:47:40.710 --> 01:47:44.230
After that, the feed was cut and Foundation
lost all communication with Watch Station

01:47:44.230 --> 01:47:45.230
Epsilon-38.

01:47:45.230 --> 01:47:49.820
Fearing the worst, a Mobile Task Force was
dispatched to the location.

01:47:49.820 --> 01:47:53.590
When they got there, all personnel at the
watch station had been killed by what could

01:47:53.590 --> 01:47:56.380
only be described as a gigantic bat.

01:47:56.380 --> 01:48:00.980
The task force was able to neutralize the
entity, and as soon as they could, the Foundation

01:48:00.980 --> 01:48:07.070
moved in to increase security around the SCP,
creating Area - 354 and installing a permanent

01:48:07.070 --> 01:48:08.540
security detail.

01:48:08.540 --> 01:48:13.630
After this point, the pond started to regularly
spit out a variety of monstrous entities,

01:48:13.630 --> 01:48:18.130
almost as if it was reacting to the SCP Foundation's
increased security measures.

01:48:18.130 --> 01:48:23.200
After SCP-354-1, the giant bat came SCP-354-2.

01:48:23.200 --> 01:48:28.960
354-2 was an echidna-like monster the size
of a bear, that was virtually bulletproof

01:48:28.960 --> 01:48:31.750
but unable to escape Area 354.

01:48:31.750 --> 01:48:35.770
The foundation neutralized this anomaly with
napalm.

01:48:35.770 --> 01:48:41.150
SCP-354-3 was a floating black sphere capable
of firing deadly beams of concentrated energy.

01:48:41.150 --> 01:48:45.140
The area's head scientist was able to hit
it with a sledgehammer, causing the sphere

01:48:45.140 --> 01:48:49.050
to malfunction and self-destruct before it
was able to escape the area.

01:48:49.050 --> 01:48:53.570
The Foundation wasn't as lucky with SCP-354-4.

01:48:53.570 --> 01:48:58.320
This creature was a reptilian humanoid that
stood roughly 15 feet tall and was unable

01:48:58.320 --> 01:49:00.480
to be put down with gunfire.

01:49:00.480 --> 01:49:04.650
This was the first creature from the pond
to successfully escape containment, and was

01:49:04.650 --> 01:49:09.840
only able to be neutralized when the Foundation
sent in Mobile Task Force Omega 7, also known

01:49:09.840 --> 01:49:11.910
as “Pandora's Box.”

01:49:11.910 --> 01:49:17.350
The data on pond incursions is partially corrupted,
so a complete list of creatures is not available,

01:49:17.350 --> 01:49:21.400
but some of the other monsters that came out
of the blood pond included a killer robot,

01:49:21.400 --> 01:49:26.050
a set of gigantic tentacles that dragged several
D-class personnel into the pond, a pair of

01:49:26.050 --> 01:49:30.530
panther-like creatures - one made of ice and
the other of magma - that ignored foundation

01:49:30.530 --> 01:49:35.940
staff and instead fought each other, and one
seemingly normal human man who was executed

01:49:35.940 --> 01:49:38.570
as soon as he emerged from the pond.

01:49:38.570 --> 01:49:43.050
Tests on his body revealed that he was, in
fact, totally normal and would have posed

01:49:43.050 --> 01:49:44.230
no threat.

01:49:44.230 --> 01:49:48.570
These anomalies came out of the pond at fairly
regular intervals for several months, before

01:49:48.570 --> 01:49:52.570
the pond went silent for an unprecedented
22 months.

01:49:52.570 --> 01:49:56.670
The head scientist at the time noted, “I
suspect this means one of two things.

01:49:56.670 --> 01:50:00.600
Either the Red Pool has ‘died’ or ‘powered
down’ or whatever the correct term for it

01:50:00.600 --> 01:50:04.240
is; or it is ‘charging up’ for something
big to come through.

01:50:04.240 --> 01:50:09.020
O5 believes the former is the most likely
explanation, and has recalled 30% of our total

01:50:09.020 --> 01:50:11.500
personnel and cut 25% of our funding.

01:50:11.500 --> 01:50:16.810
While I can only hope that they are correct,
if the latter situation is true, we're soon

01:50:16.810 --> 01:50:21.460
to face some terrible monstrosity and we won't
have anywhere near the force necessary to

01:50:21.460 --> 01:50:22.460
deal with it.

01:50:22.460 --> 01:50:24.610
I worry for all of our safety.”

01:50:24.610 --> 01:50:30.690
His words would prove eerily prophetic following
the events of Exploratory Mission 354 Alpha.

01:50:30.690 --> 01:50:34.410
The Foundation's research and development
team built a specialized craft to explore

01:50:34.410 --> 01:50:35.410
the pond.

01:50:35.410 --> 01:50:39.360
Because of the strange properties of the pond's
density, the craft was essentially made to

01:50:39.360 --> 01:50:43.970
be both a submarine, for parts of the pond
where the contents were liquid, and a drill,

01:50:43.970 --> 01:50:47.550
for when the liquid congealed into a semi-solid
towards the bottom.

01:50:47.550 --> 01:50:53.830
The exploration team consisted of Agent Swanson,
Agent Turquoise, Agent 86, Dr. Jay Macarthur,

01:50:53.830 --> 01:50:57.780
Chris Simmons, Leroy Tucker, and a pilot named
Marty.

01:50:57.780 --> 01:51:01.190
With the team assembled, the ship was sent
down into the pond.

01:51:01.190 --> 01:51:05.610
Nothing eventful happened for the first two
days of the mission, but at 4:30 AM on the

01:51:05.610 --> 01:51:09.550
third day, gravity suddenly reversed for the
crew of the ship.

01:51:09.550 --> 01:51:13.150
This seemed to indicate that they were approaching
the halfway point, though what would be on

01:51:13.150 --> 01:51:15.750
the other side, nobody could say.

01:51:15.750 --> 01:51:20.080
On the fourth day, the ship surfaced, proving
definitely that the pond was in fact some

01:51:20.080 --> 01:51:21.230
sort of portal.

01:51:21.230 --> 01:51:24.650
The crew looked out of the portholes to see
the darkness of night above them.

01:51:24.650 --> 01:51:28.840
While sensors outside the ship detected nothing
harmful in the atmosphere around them, the

01:51:28.840 --> 01:51:31.170
crew were wary of exiting the craft.

01:51:31.170 --> 01:51:34.750
The other side of the pond was nothing like
the world the crew knew.

01:51:34.750 --> 01:51:40.360
For one thing, the night lasted for 28 hours
before dawn came, and when the sun finally

01:51:40.360 --> 01:51:44.520
rose, it was much larger and redder than the
earth's sun.

01:51:44.520 --> 01:51:48.820
Under the light of that strange, red star,
the crew could see that the pond on this side

01:51:48.820 --> 01:51:53.690
was massive compared to where they'd come
in - more like a large lake.

01:51:53.690 --> 01:51:57.460
Surrounding the lake was sand and rocks that
were covered in a kind of moss that disappeared

01:51:57.460 --> 01:52:00.340
under sunlight and regrew during the night.

01:52:00.340 --> 01:52:03.170
The team left the ship and started to explore.

01:52:03.170 --> 01:52:07.460
During their time in this strange world, they
found that the day lasted just a few hours

01:52:07.460 --> 01:52:12.260
shorter than the night, meaning that whatever
planet they were on had roughly a 43-hour-long

01:52:12.260 --> 01:52:15.420
rotation as opposed to our own planet's 24.

01:52:15.420 --> 01:52:19.480
The team found a number of anomalous elements
on their expedition, including razor-sharp

01:52:19.480 --> 01:52:23.460
grass that can puncture skin and streams of
liquid carbon dioxide.

01:52:23.460 --> 01:52:28.050
They heard some loud roars in the distance
once or twice, but other than that the planet

01:52:28.050 --> 01:52:32.211
was eerily silent, with seemingly no animal
life and not even wind.

01:52:32.211 --> 01:52:37.670
When it rained, the soil remained dry, and
based on that, the scientists theorized plants

01:52:37.670 --> 01:52:40.460
in this world were more efficient at absorbing
moisture.

01:52:40.460 --> 01:52:45.730
On the 25th day, the team ran into a huge
metal wall that appeared to be artificially

01:52:45.730 --> 01:52:46.730
constructed.

01:52:46.730 --> 01:52:51.480
Luckily, Leroy Tucker, a quick-thinking researcher,
was able to rig a blowtorch from camping supplies

01:52:51.480 --> 01:52:53.320
and melt a hole through the metal.

01:52:53.320 --> 01:52:57.920
On the other side, there was finally wind,
and odd black grass.

01:52:57.920 --> 01:53:01.590
That's the extent of what is known about the
other side of the wall, though, because the

01:53:01.590 --> 01:53:05.710
expedition logs are heavily corrupted after
that point, but we know that whatever was

01:53:05.710 --> 01:53:09.370
in there wasn't good, because the team never
returned.

01:53:09.370 --> 01:53:13.320
Strangely, there's no record of any of the
names mentioned in the ship's log - almost

01:53:13.320 --> 01:53:17.300
as if being killed on the other side completely
erased them from history.

01:53:17.300 --> 01:53:20.530
No other expeditions into the pond were launched
after that.

01:53:20.530 --> 01:53:24.730
On an undisclosed date, a year following the
discovery of the blood pond and construction

01:53:24.730 --> 01:53:30.730
of Area 354, the site was completely evacuated,
and the power was cut to the area.

01:53:30.730 --> 01:53:34.910
Mobile Task Force Theta-12 was dispatched
to investigate the cause of the evacuation,

01:53:34.910 --> 01:53:40.360
but before contact could be established, the
area's on-site nuclear warhead was detonated,

01:53:40.360 --> 01:53:41.940
completely destroying the site.

01:53:41.940 --> 01:53:46.730
MTF Theta 12 was then attacked by a convoy
made up of D-class and other low-ranking staff

01:53:46.730 --> 01:53:48.800
who had evacuated Area 354.

01:53:48.800 --> 01:53:53.300
It was apparent that there had been some kind
of mutiny within the site, and that a dissolution

01:53:53.300 --> 01:53:57.170
of the chain of command had led to its evacuation
and destruction.

01:53:57.170 --> 01:54:02.530
The convoy totally annihilated MTF Theta 12,
and no further contact with the former personnel

01:54:02.530 --> 01:54:05.960
of Area-354 has been made since.

01:54:05.960 --> 01:54:10.400
Following the site's detonation, a new site
was constructed, called simply the Red Pool

01:54:10.400 --> 01:54:11.880
Containment Site.

01:54:11.880 --> 01:54:16.170
Unlike the previous facility, which focused
on research and neutralization, the new site

01:54:16.170 --> 01:54:18.780
is entirely concerned with containment.

01:54:18.780 --> 01:54:23.020
The shift in directive came as a response
to the pond's apparent reactive nature - each

01:54:23.020 --> 01:54:27.770
creature that emerged from the pond seemed
to be in retaliation to the Foundation's actions,

01:54:27.770 --> 01:54:32.280
and it was theorized by some that the mutiny
at Area 354 was triggered by some kind of

01:54:32.280 --> 01:54:34.650
psychic attack from the pond itself.

01:54:34.650 --> 01:54:39.960
An interview in the SCP file on 354 reveals
that there was one more disastrous attempt

01:54:39.960 --> 01:54:42.960
to control and understand the blood pond.

01:54:42.960 --> 01:54:46.840
According to an interview with a Foundation
agent, the head doctor proposed a scheme to

01:54:46.840 --> 01:54:50.400
drain the blood pond using a system of pumps
and hoses.

01:54:50.400 --> 01:54:55.090
All non-essential personnel were evacuated
in case of emergency, leaving only the pump

01:54:55.090 --> 01:54:58.840
technicians, D-class personnel, and a few
agents for security.

01:54:58.840 --> 01:55:03.790
However, as soon as the pump was scheduled
to be turned on, everyone at the site experienced

01:55:03.790 --> 01:55:06.030
a mass dissociative episode.

01:55:06.030 --> 01:55:10.920
The agent described the feeling they all experienced
as like being in a dream and suddenly realizing

01:55:10.920 --> 01:55:11.920
that you're asleep.

01:55:11.920 --> 01:55:14.250
He said “Everything stopped being real.

01:55:14.250 --> 01:55:16.650
It was like we had to escape right now.”

01:55:16.650 --> 01:55:21.030
When asked what happened when the pump was
turned on, he simply explained that it “wouldn't

01:55:21.030 --> 01:55:22.030
let them”.

01:55:22.030 --> 01:55:25.800
This interview confirmed the theory that the
pond is not only capable of releasing monsters

01:55:25.800 --> 01:55:31.710
out into our world, but also that it's capable
of powerful but much more subtle, psychological

01:55:31.710 --> 01:55:32.710
attacks.

01:55:32.710 --> 01:55:37.900
This suggests a chilling possibility - that
the pond isn't just blindly reacting to being

01:55:37.900 --> 01:55:43.250
attacked, but it's fully sentient, and the
actions of the SCP foundation have only served

01:55:43.250 --> 01:55:44.490
to annoy it.

01:55:44.490 --> 01:55:49.000
And worse, studies of the pond's banks have
provided evidence that the area of congealed

01:55:49.000 --> 01:55:53.360
liquid around the perimeter of the pond has
been steadily expanding.

01:55:53.360 --> 01:55:56.440
That's right - the pond is getting bigger.

01:55:56.440 --> 01:56:00.401
The last thing the Foundation agent stationed
at the site said before being dragged out

01:56:00.401 --> 01:56:05.650
of the interview and sedated was “It gets
bigger and stronger every day, and now we've

01:56:05.650 --> 01:56:10.190
made it angry!”

01:56:10.190 --> 01:56:16.650
One of the primary aims of the SCP foundation
is to contain the anomalies that they discover.

01:56:16.650 --> 01:56:20.430
Secure, Contain, Protect is their mission
statement, after all.

01:56:20.430 --> 01:56:25.940
However, there are some forces so unknowable
and malevolent that there is no way to truly

01:56:25.940 --> 01:56:28.800
contain them.

01:56:28.800 --> 01:56:32.650
SCP-4205 is one such force.

01:56:32.650 --> 01:56:38.900
Very little is known about SCP-4205, even
compared to other SCPs, many of which are

01:56:38.900 --> 01:56:41.010
mysterious by their very nature.

01:56:41.010 --> 01:56:46.550
We do not know what it is, we do not know
what it wants, and we do not have any meaningful

01:56:46.550 --> 01:56:48.280
way to stop it.

01:56:48.280 --> 01:56:53.210
The best that can be done to protect yourself
from it is to implement the few known preventative

01:56:53.210 --> 01:56:58.690
measures available, and pray that it doesn’t
set its sights on you.

01:56:58.690 --> 01:57:04.840
The little information that exists on SCP-4205
was recorded by Wade Dalitz, a former junior

01:57:04.840 --> 01:57:06.670
researcher at the Foundation.

01:57:06.670 --> 01:57:12.482
He wrote the initial report on SCP-4205 on
a computer, Terminal 4, which was linked to

01:57:12.482 --> 01:57:13.482
his brain and consciousness as he wrote.

01:57:13.482 --> 01:57:16.310
Though his work was highly valuable, and provided
many previously unknown insights about the

01:57:16.310 --> 01:57:20.520
nature of the SCP, sadly his knowledge could
not save him.

01:57:20.520 --> 01:57:29.160
The official entry on SCP-4205 was his final
act before his death on December 11, 1992.

01:57:29.160 --> 01:57:33.430
Before we get deeper into that entry, and
Wade’s final day on earth, it’s important

01:57:33.430 --> 01:57:36.430
to understand what SCP-4205 is.

01:57:36.430 --> 01:57:40.400
Or, at least, understand the little we know
about what it is.

01:57:40.400 --> 01:57:47.250
4205 is a Keter class SCP, meaning that it’s
incredibly difficult or complicated to contain.

01:57:47.250 --> 01:57:50.840
It also shows extreme hostility toward all
life.

01:57:50.840 --> 01:57:55.380
It is described as an amber-colored pair of
human-like eyes, with the ability to appear

01:57:55.380 --> 01:58:00.940
spontaneously in windows, mirrors, or any
other glass or glass-like surface.

01:58:00.940 --> 01:58:05.530
One of the reasons so little is known about
these eyes, is that anyone who sees them dies

01:58:05.530 --> 01:58:06.530
within seconds.

01:58:06.530 --> 01:58:07.530
The effect is nearly instantaneous.

01:58:07.530 --> 01:58:14.400
Much like the mythical basilisk, the gaze
of SCP-4205 is deadly and inescapable.

01:58:14.400 --> 01:58:20.300
The eyes only seem to appear to people who
are alone in a room, and never in places where

01:58:20.300 --> 01:58:22.930
there’s more than one person.

01:58:22.930 --> 01:58:28.030
But aside from that, there is no way to predict
where the eyes will appear.

01:58:28.030 --> 01:58:32.360
Since it is unknown if the eyes are part of
a corporeal body, or even present on this

01:58:32.360 --> 01:58:37.610
plane of reality, there is no way to contain
them or stop them from killing.

01:58:37.610 --> 01:58:42.290
This brings us back to that fateful day, December
11, 1992.

01:58:42.290 --> 01:58:47.220
The day that the SCP Foundation would finally
be given some tangible information on the

01:58:47.220 --> 01:58:52.880
mysterious amber eyes, at the cost of one
of their researcher’s lives.

01:58:52.880 --> 01:58:57.380
Wade Dalitz, a young man fresh out of his
university studies, was brought onboard as

01:58:57.380 --> 01:59:01.510
a Junior Researcher by Dr. Mark Forsyth, a
Senior Researcher.

01:59:01.510 --> 01:59:06.300
Dr. Forsyth recruited Wade after giving a
guest lecture at his university and being

01:59:06.300 --> 01:59:11.260
impressed by Wade’s keen observational skills,
thirst for knowledge of the unexplained, and

01:59:11.260 --> 01:59:15.970
determination to understand that which seemed
to defy explanation.

01:59:15.970 --> 01:59:20.280
After Wade graduated, he was recruited by
Dr. Forsyth, and given the position of Junior

01:59:20.280 --> 01:59:22.250
Researcher at the SCP Foundation.

01:59:22.250 --> 01:59:27.720
where he assisted Dr. Forsyth, now a site
supervisor, in his research.

01:59:27.720 --> 01:59:32.300
After several months of working under Dr.
Forsyth with minimal responsibilities, Wade

01:59:32.300 --> 01:59:40.030
was finally given the opportunity to write
his first SCP report... on SCP-4205.

01:59:40.030 --> 01:59:45.330
Following some initial documented appearances
of 4205, Dr. Forsyth approved further research

01:59:45.330 --> 01:59:49.650
into the anomaly, with Wade appointed as the
lead researcher on the subject.

01:59:49.650 --> 01:59:55.530
According to Wade’s entry, he was responsible
for the discovery of SCP-4205 when he spotted

01:59:55.530 --> 01:59:57.060
the anomaly in a window.

01:59:57.060 --> 02:00:01.580
Though later reviews of security footage determined
that he was not the first person to observe

02:00:01.580 --> 02:00:07.110
the eyes, he did claim to be the first to
observe them and live to tell the tale.

02:00:07.110 --> 02:00:11.110
In his writing on the subject, Wade is puzzled
by the fact that he survived his encounter

02:00:11.110 --> 02:00:16.520
with SCP-4205, and wonders what could have
set him apart from the others that fell victim

02:00:16.520 --> 02:00:17.980
to its gaze.

02:00:17.980 --> 02:00:24.540
He found himself frustrated as he documented
SCP-4205, his rough drafts of reports dissolving

02:00:24.540 --> 02:00:27.350
into angry rants about his own incompetence.

02:00:27.350 --> 02:00:33.110
Wade had difficulty adjusting to his new responsibilities,
especially with the added element of his assigned

02:00:33.110 --> 02:00:35.720
subject’s mysterious and volatile nature.

02:00:35.720 --> 02:00:41.870
In between his reports on SCP-4205, its previous
appearances, and its effects on its victims,

02:00:41.870 --> 02:00:46.130
he wrote letters to his father, desperate
for reassurance that he was not messing everything

02:00:46.130 --> 02:00:47.130
up.

02:00:47.130 --> 02:00:49.680
Letters that would sadly go unsent.

02:00:49.680 --> 02:00:55.680
In his coverage of SCP-4205, Wade made note
of a troubling recurring element in each victim’s

02:00:55.680 --> 02:00:56.680
death.

02:00:56.680 --> 02:00:59.970
When medical professionals attempted to revive
the victims, it was always noted that their

02:00:59.970 --> 02:01:05.050
brain activity stopped much more quickly than
in cases of death by natural causes.

02:01:05.050 --> 02:01:09.260
When examined, all aspects of the body aside
from the brain were completely unharmed.

02:01:09.260 --> 02:01:14.820
The brain, however, showed massive nerve damage
in the amygdala, hippocampus, medial temporal

02:01:14.820 --> 02:01:16.300
lobe, and occipital lobe.

02:01:16.300 --> 02:01:20.220
The appearance of this brain trauma has been
compared to the effects of electrical shock

02:01:20.220 --> 02:01:22.200
or a severe head injury.

02:01:22.200 --> 02:01:28.130
In addition to Wade, six other people on record
encountered SCP-4205.

02:01:28.130 --> 02:01:33.050
Every encounter ended the same way, with sudden
brain death and severe nerve damage immediately

02:01:33.050 --> 02:01:36.130
after viewing the eyes in a reflective surface.

02:01:36.130 --> 02:01:40.910
The first encounter occurred on January 5,
1990, when Deputy Liaison Gena viewed the

02:01:40.910 --> 02:01:43.910
eyes in a reflective glass one-way window.

02:01:43.910 --> 02:01:47.450
Security tape reveals that the eyes appeared
in the window eight minutes before the Deputy

02:01:47.450 --> 02:01:49.670
Liaison spotted them.

02:01:49.670 --> 02:01:53.280
As soon as he did, he fell from his chair
and immediately died.

02:01:53.280 --> 02:01:58.030
Though the specifics varied from case to case,
the end result of every encounter was the

02:01:58.030 --> 02:02:02.040
same, with none surviving long enough to relate
what they experienced.

02:02:02.040 --> 02:02:04.440
Every encounter except Wade’s.

02:02:04.440 --> 02:02:12.130
As he continued his documentation of SCP-4205,
Wade’s mental state quickly began to deteriorate.

02:02:12.130 --> 02:02:17.170
The process of his mind coming apart, and
his thoughts giving way to confusion, fear,

02:02:17.170 --> 02:02:21.650
and anger at his own survival, is documented
in his reports on the SCP.

02:02:21.650 --> 02:02:28.340
He agonized over the question of why he was
able to survive gazing into the eyes of SCP-4205

02:02:28.340 --> 02:02:30.400
when so many others had not?

02:02:30.400 --> 02:02:35.980
The question consumed him until he was unable
to eat, sleep, or do anything but obsess over

02:02:35.980 --> 02:02:39.140
the SCP he was tasked with researching.

02:02:39.140 --> 02:02:43.780
As he became more agitated, he began to write
about the eyes appearing to him again.

02:02:43.780 --> 02:02:47.960
On one occurrence, he attempted to touch them,
and described the sensation like touching

02:02:47.960 --> 02:02:52.300
a balloon to your arm after it has been charged
with static electricity.

02:02:52.300 --> 02:02:54.630
He continued to report seeing the eyes.

02:02:54.630 --> 02:02:59.830
The worst was when trying to sleep, during
which he was overtaken by violent sleep paralysis

02:02:59.830 --> 02:03:02.860
and plagued with visions of the amber eyes.

02:03:02.860 --> 02:03:06.400
The only comfort Wade seemed to find was in
memories of his loved ones.

02:03:06.400 --> 02:03:11.110
He wrote about his mother and his father,
even as their memories grew foggier to him.

02:03:11.110 --> 02:03:15.790
He also mentioned a man by the name of Theodore
Quale, who he claimed was a researcher at

02:03:15.790 --> 02:03:17.030
the Foundation.

02:03:17.030 --> 02:03:20.830
Later fact checking determined that this was
not the case, and that Quale must have been

02:03:20.830 --> 02:03:22.140
someone from Wade’s past.

02:03:22.140 --> 02:03:27.540
He wrote about Quale wistfully, mournfully,
with words of love and loss, tormented by

02:03:27.540 --> 02:03:32.760
the sense that he was losing his grip on reality
and everything he once held dear.

02:03:32.760 --> 02:03:37.760
As Wade descended deeper into a hell inside
his own mind, he continued to mull over the

02:03:37.760 --> 02:03:40.840
questions of SCP-4205.

02:03:40.840 --> 02:03:42.550
Was there more to it than what was visible?

02:03:42.550 --> 02:03:44.550
Did it have a body?

02:03:44.550 --> 02:03:46.900
Why had he survived his encounter with it?

02:03:46.900 --> 02:03:48.280
And what did it want from him?

02:03:48.280 --> 02:03:52.100
He decided that those who had perished immediately
were the lucky ones.

02:03:52.100 --> 02:03:55.680
They had escaped the torture that had overtaken
his life.

02:03:55.680 --> 02:04:00.010
The eyes were everywhere now, watching him
even as he attempted to unlock their secrets

02:04:00.010 --> 02:04:01.420
on a Foundation computer.

02:04:01.420 --> 02:04:06.060
He spoke to the eyes at one point, but refused
to write down what they had said to him.

02:04:06.060 --> 02:04:11.820
His last entry, his last thought, was a plea
for Theodore Quale’s embrace.

02:04:11.820 --> 02:04:17.410
There is no more written about the research,
or about the eyes, only a simple, desperate

02:04:17.410 --> 02:04:20.160
need for a comfort that is long lost.

02:04:20.160 --> 02:04:23.860
Now is the part of our story where terror
meets tragedy.

02:04:23.860 --> 02:04:28.620
Contrary to what Wade thought, and what was
recorded in his final computer entries, he

02:04:28.620 --> 02:04:32.890
did not survive his brief brush with SCP-4205.

02:04:32.890 --> 02:04:36.890
The truth is that he spotted the amber eyes
in the glass above his terminal monitor while

02:04:36.890 --> 02:04:39.900
recording the entry, and died seconds later.

02:04:39.900 --> 02:04:45.750
Though his death was swift, it felt agonizingly
slow, as revealed by his descent into panic

02:04:45.750 --> 02:04:48.390
and paranoia recorded in his writings.

02:04:48.390 --> 02:04:53.100
The entries recorded as a result of the connection
Wade and the computer, his physical contact

02:04:53.100 --> 02:04:59.400
with it at the moment of exposure to SCP-4205’s
anomalous effects allowing the terminal to

02:04:59.400 --> 02:05:04.990
archive his final thoughts before he succumbed
to the deadly nature of the eyes.

02:05:04.990 --> 02:05:10.680
The entries were never physically typed, but
rather jumped from his dying mind into the

02:05:10.680 --> 02:05:11.680
mainframe.

02:05:11.680 --> 02:05:17.530
His entries, the echoes of a dying mind, offer
a glimpse into the way that SCP-4205 kills

02:05:17.530 --> 02:05:18.720
its victims.

02:05:18.720 --> 02:05:23.430
In the seconds leading up to death, the amber
eyes fast-forward a person’s mind, feeding

02:05:23.430 --> 02:05:27.990
on the electrical impulses that it gives off
as it speeds through what seem to be days

02:05:27.990 --> 02:05:30.590
of fear and of a loosening grip on reality.

02:05:30.590 --> 02:05:35.670
The death may be swift, but the suffering
is long.

02:05:35.670 --> 02:05:40.860
Wade left the foundation with a digital fingerprint,
an echo of the person he was at his core in

02:05:40.860 --> 02:05:45.950
addition to his experiences with SCP-4205
itself.

02:05:45.950 --> 02:05:51.270
Wade spent his final moments terrified for
his life, dreaming of a father that he missed

02:05:51.270 --> 02:05:53.810
and a long-lost love from his college days.

02:05:53.810 --> 02:05:57.430
His sacrifice must not be forgotten.

02:05:57.430 --> 02:06:03.360
Because no one ever survives an encounter
with SCP-4205, his documented final moments

02:06:03.360 --> 02:06:06.400
are the only first-hand account in existence.

02:06:06.400 --> 02:06:09.920
Junior Researcher Wade Dalitz gave us a gift
in death.

02:06:09.920 --> 02:06:16.590
He allowed the SCP Foundation a glimpse into
the true horror of the amber eyes, and a reminder

02:06:16.590 --> 02:06:20.030
of what they are capable of stealing from
us.

02:06:20.030 --> 02:06:26.250
So be careful of windows, mirrors, screens,
and all reflective surfaces.

02:06:26.250 --> 02:06:34.790
When you look into them, you never know what
you might see looking back.

02:06:34.790 --> 02:06:41.610
The Young Girl stood silently in a room full
of corpses, staring off into the distance.

02:06:41.610 --> 02:06:46.860
She was in a trance, seemingly unaware of
the carnage that’d just unfolded around

02:06:46.860 --> 02:06:52.400
her. Some of the men were shot or stabbed
or beaten, others had no apparent cause of

02:06:52.400 --> 02:06:58.730
death whatsoever. But the Young Girl was unscathed.
She was wearing a yellow summer dress and

02:06:58.730 --> 02:07:04.050
looked like she couldn’t have been more
than three years old. She awoke from her trance,

02:07:04.050 --> 02:07:08.500
still paying no heed to the violence in the
room, and left.

02:07:08.500 --> 02:07:16.910
This is the story of SCP - 053, the Young
Girl. Her strange and terrifying life before

02:07:16.910 --> 02:07:22.030
containment, her thrilling capture, and the
part she played in one of the most dangerous

02:07:22.030 --> 02:07:26.760
SCP Foundation cross-tests of all time.

02:07:26.760 --> 02:07:31.870
She had no name. The Young Girl could never
spend enough time around someone to be given

02:07:31.870 --> 02:07:38.260
one. It was her terrible curse that she could
never stay in one place for too long, or something

02:07:38.260 --> 02:07:44.120
truly awful would happen to everyone around
her. She’d been drifting for years, but

02:07:44.120 --> 02:07:49.640
never grew to look older than a child. Her
memories were long but formless - divided

02:07:49.640 --> 02:07:55.880
mostly into ten-minute increments. The Young
Girl kept on moving. It was all she could

02:07:55.880 --> 02:07:56.880
do.

02:07:56.880 --> 02:08:02.440
In 1993, when the Young Girl had already been
alive for a very long time, she found herself

02:08:02.440 --> 02:08:07.250
in Illinois after days of trekking on the
roadside. She would scavenge what little food

02:08:07.250 --> 02:08:12.690
she needed from the trash, and drink from
lakes, rivers, or even puddles when she needed

02:08:12.690 --> 02:08:19.020
to. Deep down, the Young Girl knew that even
if she grew hungry or thirsty, she would not

02:08:19.020 --> 02:08:20.020
die.

02:08:20.020 --> 02:08:25.310
In her travels, the Young Girl had managed
to scrounge together a few dollars, and though

02:08:25.310 --> 02:08:29.920
she knew her contact with people had to be
limited, there was one thing she wanted that

02:08:29.920 --> 02:08:36.580
felt worth the risk: A candy bar. It’d been
a long time since she tasted chocolate, and

02:08:36.580 --> 02:08:42.110
she found the idea of tasting it again too
enticing to resist. When she reached Chicago,

02:08:42.110 --> 02:08:46.980
she entered a small corner store with her
handful of dollars, selected a candy bar from

02:08:46.980 --> 02:08:52.530
the rack, and approached the counter. For
reasons even she didn’t fully understand,

02:08:52.530 --> 02:08:55.470
she kept her eyes down as she placed the money
on the counter.

02:08:55.470 --> 02:09:02.270
You see, SCP - 053 was always a special child.
As we briefly mentioned earlier, you might

02:09:02.270 --> 02:09:07.160
even say she was cursed. Whenever she was
around other people for too long, terrible

02:09:07.160 --> 02:09:14.460
things tended to happen. Unspeakable things.
And as a component of this strange, unexplainable

02:09:14.460 --> 02:09:19.280
curse - or perhaps as a means of keeping her
safe from it - the Young Girl was doomed to

02:09:19.280 --> 02:09:24.990
never understand the nature of her predicament.
It was like there was a mental wall between

02:09:24.990 --> 02:09:28.990
her and the horrors her presence could cause.

02:09:28.990 --> 02:09:33.810
All she knew was that it was best to avoid
eye contact, stay away from large groups of

02:09:33.810 --> 02:09:41.330
people at all costs, and never spend any longer
than ten minutes around anyone. Ever. That’s

02:09:41.330 --> 02:09:46.430
why, as she waited for her chocolate bar at
the cash register, she kept her head down

02:09:46.430 --> 02:09:47.620
and waited to go.

02:09:47.620 --> 02:09:51.970
The cashier looked down upon this strange
girl with apparent concern. It’d been a

02:09:51.970 --> 02:09:57.450
slow day, all in all. A few listless patrons
milled around the store, window shopping.

02:09:57.450 --> 02:10:02.890
His brain was practically on autopilot, until
he saw this child. She looked filthy, in a

02:10:02.890 --> 02:10:09.640
dishevelled dress and was totally alone. What
on earth had happened to this child? The more

02:10:09.640 --> 02:10:15.900
he looked at her, the more he sensed a creepy
vibe exuding from her. Something about this

02:10:15.900 --> 02:10:17.940
girl was wrong.

02:10:17.940 --> 02:10:23.310
He spoke softly to her, introducing himself
as Miles, and asking her where her mommy and

02:10:23.310 --> 02:10:29.130
daddy were. The Young Girl hesitated for just
a few seconds too long for his liking, before

02:10:29.130 --> 02:10:33.310
simply saying that they were at home. Any
parent who’d let their three-year-old kid

02:10:33.310 --> 02:10:40.240
just wander around the South Side of Chicago
alone was either crazy, neglectful, or both.

02:10:40.240 --> 02:10:44.890
He asked her politely to remain in the store,
while he gave the police a quick call. He’d

02:10:44.890 --> 02:10:48.330
even let her have the candy bar for free while
she waited.

02:10:48.330 --> 02:10:53.090
Immediately, the little girl became uncomfortable.
She’d already been in here for a few minutes

02:10:53.090 --> 02:10:59.170
now, and while Miles called the police he
expected her to wait around even longer. Again,

02:10:59.170 --> 02:11:03.320
she couldn’t even tell you why she knew
waiting around like this would be so dangerous,

02:11:03.320 --> 02:11:09.670
but she had a nagging, instinctual awareness
that she needed to get out. Around the store,

02:11:09.670 --> 02:11:15.440
the few patrons still inside started to get
a strange feeling, like someone was watching

02:11:15.440 --> 02:11:16.440
them.

02:11:16.440 --> 02:11:23.820
Soon, what seemed like mild paranoia was quickly
becoming white-hot anger. Pure rage. They

02:11:23.820 --> 02:11:31.200
felt almost like they had to kill someone.
But not just anyone. Someone particular. They

02:11:31.200 --> 02:11:33.060
had to kill the little girl.

02:11:33.060 --> 02:11:38.210
It wasn’t long until the police arrived.
Two officers, Holiday and DeWitt, entered

02:11:38.210 --> 02:11:42.860
to collect the apparently neglected little
girl. It was a story they’d sadly heard

02:11:42.860 --> 02:11:48.050
all too often - a tragically common occurrence
in the big city. When the two of them approached

02:11:48.050 --> 02:11:52.400
the Young Girl, she was looking down at the
ground. They tried to speak to her but she

02:11:52.400 --> 02:11:58.710
was unresponsive. Miles looked on, concerned,
but deep down, he felt the same bizarre progression

02:11:58.710 --> 02:12:07.380
all the other store-goers were feeling: Confusion,
irritability, and finally... violent rage.

02:12:07.380 --> 02:12:12.630
The corner store was a powder keg, all it
needed now was a spark.

02:12:12.630 --> 02:12:17.900
And that spark came when Officer Holiday reached
down, and took the Young Girl by the arm.

02:12:17.900 --> 02:12:23.880
His bare skin touched her’s, and it was
all over. The cop twitched, his mind washed

02:12:23.880 --> 02:12:29.120
out in murderous insanity, as he reached for
his side-arm. Officer DeWitt opened his mouth

02:12:29.120 --> 02:12:34.140
to protest, but he didn’t manage to get
a syllable out before Holiday turned and shot

02:12:34.140 --> 02:12:39.770
him. In a fraction of a second, the quiet
corner store exploded into a violent bloodbath,

02:12:39.770 --> 02:12:42.510
as the patrons and cops slaughtered each other.

02:12:42.510 --> 02:12:46.640
All the while, the Young Girl just stood there
in a trance.

02:12:46.640 --> 02:12:51.850
Everyone was dead within minutes, except Miles
and the Young Girl. He shakily rose to his

02:12:51.850 --> 02:12:57.210
feet, his mind carved out by madness, and
reached for Officer Holiday’s discarded

02:12:57.210 --> 02:12:59.190
pistol.

02:12:59.190 --> 02:13:04.630
Outside of the store, The Young Girl appeared,
walking out as if nothing had happened. Inside,

02:13:04.630 --> 02:13:09.881
Miles’s body was sprawled over the counter,
clutching his chest as if his heart had just

02:13:09.881 --> 02:13:11.250
exploded.

02:13:11.250 --> 02:13:16.880
This was the true nature of the Young Girl’s
curse. Anyone who meets her eye, touches her

02:13:16.880 --> 02:13:21.700
skin, or spends over ten minutes around her
is destined to slip into a state of murderous

02:13:21.700 --> 02:13:28.940
insanity. They’ll go into a kill-crazy frenzy,
and attack anyone around them, until all that’s

02:13:28.940 --> 02:13:33.970
left is them and the Young Girl. At which
point, they’ll try to kill her, too - but

02:13:33.970 --> 02:13:39.560
the mere act of attempting to kill her is
also fatal, killing its victims with a massive

02:13:39.560 --> 02:13:45.200
heart attack or a seizure. The second part
of the girl’s curse is that she herself

02:13:45.200 --> 02:13:46.520
can never die.

02:13:46.520 --> 02:13:53.280
She keeps coming back, unknowingly causing
more misery and death along the way. Until,

02:13:53.280 --> 02:13:58.320
of course, a certain secret organization took
notice.

02:13:58.320 --> 02:14:03.390
That night, the Young Girl sat in the cavernous
interior of a local abandoned warehouse, quietly

02:14:03.390 --> 02:14:09.240
eating her hard-earned candy bar. Suddenly,
she heard a quiet “crack” in the distance.

02:14:09.240 --> 02:14:13.760
She tried to move her left arm, but found
it was numb. She could barely move it. The

02:14:13.760 --> 02:14:18.750
Girl turned her head, and saw a tranquilizer
dart sticking out of her skin. Instinctively,

02:14:18.750 --> 02:14:23.880
she got up and began to run, limping as her
left leg started to lose all feeling too.

02:14:23.880 --> 02:14:28.660
Suddenly, she was surrounded by footsteps,
as masked men in SWAT uniforms emerged out

02:14:28.660 --> 02:14:34.450
of the darkness on all sides. The last thing
she remembered before slipping out of consciousness

02:14:34.450 --> 02:14:40.240
was a blindfold being pulled over her eyes,
and tiny metal handcuffs clasping over her

02:14:40.240 --> 02:14:41.240
wrists.

02:14:41.240 --> 02:14:46.440
When she woke up, she was in the nicest room
she’d slept in in years. It was sixteen

02:14:46.440 --> 02:14:52.860
feet by sixteen feet, with toys, books, and
games. Standing across the room from her was

02:14:52.860 --> 02:14:58.090
a stranger in a full hazmat suit, connected
to a long, steel cable leading out of the

02:14:58.090 --> 02:15:04.110
room. He spoke calmly and politely. A timer
on his wrist counted down from ten minutes.

02:15:04.110 --> 02:15:08.280
The stranger explained to the Young Girl that
they would be taking care of her now, and

02:15:08.280 --> 02:15:14.400
would provide her with all the toys and games
and treats she wanted, provided she cooperated

02:15:14.400 --> 02:15:17.640
and submitted willingly to some tests.

02:15:17.640 --> 02:15:22.410
Compared to living out on the streets with
no food or comfort, this seemed like an excellent

02:15:22.410 --> 02:15:28.090
compromise. She would live in what seemed
like luxury, and every so often, she’d be

02:15:28.090 --> 02:15:33.960
taken to a testing room with a stranger in
an orange jumpsuit. After a few minutes of

02:15:33.960 --> 02:15:38.150
waiting, the stranger would inevitably go
feral, and attempt to kill her - and then

02:15:38.150 --> 02:15:42.940
always die in the process. The strangers in
the hazmat suits told her that all of these

02:15:42.940 --> 02:15:47.740
tests were helping them learn extremely valuable
things about her, and that soon, they’d

02:15:47.740 --> 02:15:53.420
have a very special job for her. It would
be a wonderful surprise.

02:15:53.420 --> 02:16:00.310
It was in the spring of 1994 that the Young
Girl, now officially designated SCP - 053,

02:16:00.310 --> 02:16:06.280
finally received her special mission. Several
strangers in full hazmat suits, wielding rifles,

02:16:06.280 --> 02:16:11.080
escorted her out of her usual bedroom. She
asked where she was being taken, and was told

02:16:11.080 --> 02:16:16.810
she was going to meet a new friend. She was
taken to one of the recreational testing rooms,

02:16:16.810 --> 02:16:24.010
and pushed inside. Out in front of her, she
saw something terrifying: A giant, reptilian

02:16:24.010 --> 02:16:25.490
creature.

02:16:25.490 --> 02:16:32.730
She retreated from it instinctually, hiding
behind a chair in the containment area. The

02:16:32.730 --> 02:16:35.490
giant reptile didn’t move. It just sat there.
Eventually, she plucked up the courage to

02:16:35.490 --> 02:16:40.890
go and touch the creature, before retreating
again. No reaction. Little by little, the

02:16:40.890 --> 02:16:45.889
Young Girl gained confidence, and continued
to approach and pat the creature. It exhaled

02:16:45.889 --> 02:16:50.460
through its huge nostrils, and the Young Girl
literally jumped for joy in her amusement.

02:16:50.460 --> 02:16:56.300
She began to play with the docile monster,
hugging it, even drawing on it with a crayon.

02:16:56.300 --> 02:17:00.420
The observers were truly baffled. What the
Young Girl didn’t know was that she was

02:17:00.420 --> 02:17:07.360
playing with SCP - 682, one of the most hateful
and aggressive anomalies under the Foundation’s

02:17:07.360 --> 02:17:14.070
watchful eye. And yet, in her presence, the
creature was like an overgrown puppy dog.

02:17:14.070 --> 02:17:19.090
After the test phase, the Young Girl was removed
from the Hard-To-Kill Reptile’s presence,

02:17:19.090 --> 02:17:23.760
immediately causing the creature to become
violent and murderous once again. After being

02:17:23.760 --> 02:17:29.990
separated from her strange new friend, the
Young Girl was seen crying for several minutes.

02:17:29.990 --> 02:17:34.170
According to all accounts, the Young Girl
is eager to see her friend again some day,

02:17:34.170 --> 02:17:38.881
though considering the Reptile killed several
guards after their separation, the Foundation

02:17:38.881 --> 02:17:42.230
isn’t eager to grant her that wish.

02:17:42.230 --> 02:17:48.470
SCP-053 was classified as Euclid and continues
to be held in containment, with her toys,

02:17:48.470 --> 02:17:54.670
books, and games rotated out on a regular
basis. Seeing as she shows no sign of aging,

02:17:54.670 --> 02:18:00.360
this ongoing containment is likely to be the
sad, strange fate of the Young Girl. An anomaly

02:18:00.360 --> 02:18:05.620
that never wanted to hurt anyone - but an
important and cruel reminder that what we

02:18:05.620 --> 02:18:10.900
want doesn’t always influence the world
in the way we want.

02:18:10.900 --> 02:18:15.280
Cast your mind back to the height of the Cold
War.

02:18:15.280 --> 02:18:20.710
The ever-accelerating nuclear arms race brought
us closer than ever before to making the nightmare

02:18:20.710 --> 02:18:24.349
of nuclear armageddon a terrifying reality.

02:18:24.349 --> 02:18:29.370
The two main parties in this conflict are
the United States and Soviet Union, each amassing

02:18:29.370 --> 02:18:34.660
huge amounts of nuclear warheads and jumping
on any available advantage they can over their

02:18:34.660 --> 02:18:35.660
enemies.

02:18:35.660 --> 02:18:39.849
What you may not know is that, in order to
detect an impending attack from the United

02:18:39.849 --> 02:18:45.190
States, the Soviets developed what would become
known to the West as the “Dead Hand.”

02:18:45.190 --> 02:18:52.429
But this experimental piece of technology
also has another name: SCP-1984.

02:18:52.429 --> 02:18:57.540
It is no secret that the Soviet Union feared
the threat of US nuclear strikes and invasion

02:18:57.540 --> 02:19:03.719
just as much as the US feared an attack from
them, so SCP-1984 was created to act as a

02:19:03.719 --> 02:19:08.889
deterrence mechanism, specifically against
secure second strikes.

02:19:08.889 --> 02:19:13.990
Secure second strikes were both a deterrent
and overwhelming concern during the Cold War,

02:19:13.990 --> 02:19:18.530
referring to a country’s ability, after
suffering an initial nuclear attack, to still

02:19:18.530 --> 02:19:24.540
retaliate, firing on their enemy and causing
untold damage with their own arsenal of nuclear

02:19:24.540 --> 02:19:25.540
weapons.

02:19:25.540 --> 02:19:29.610
The Soviet Union knew that any action they
made that could be perceived as an attack

02:19:29.610 --> 02:19:34.190
on United States soil would be threatened
with nuclear retaliation, and that if they

02:19:34.190 --> 02:19:40.389
fired first, America would return fire and
potentially annihilate them with a second

02:19:40.389 --> 02:19:41.389
strike.

02:19:41.389 --> 02:19:45.710
SCP-1984 was to be an automated system that
would activate in response to the destruction

02:19:45.710 --> 02:19:49.130
of the Soviet’s main command and control
structure.

02:19:49.130 --> 02:19:53.440
Given that the Dead Hand was created during
the eighties, you might be forgiven for expecting

02:19:53.440 --> 02:19:58.820
a nuclear detection system built at this point
in history to consist of various sensors connected

02:19:58.820 --> 02:20:00.060
to a computer network.

02:20:00.060 --> 02:20:05.740
But you have also probably already realized
that, if that was the case, then the Foundation

02:20:05.740 --> 02:20:08.900
wouldn’t be so interested in it.

02:20:08.900 --> 02:20:14.030
In actuality, SCP-1984 is a fully autonomous
entity rather than a network of sensors for

02:20:14.030 --> 02:20:20.511
detecting incoming nuclear attacks or a computer
capable of initiating a second strike.

02:20:20.511 --> 02:20:25.720
Perhaps what makes the Foundation more interested
in the Dead Hand is that it is both self-aware,

02:20:25.720 --> 02:20:31.011
and linked simultaneously to every single
one of Russia’s nuclear launch sites.

02:20:31.011 --> 02:20:37.890
Not only this, but SCP-1984 also has direct
access to every single one of the atomic weapons

02:20:37.890 --> 02:20:43.730
stockpiled by the Soviet Union during the
Cold War, and is capable of launching Intercontinental

02:20:43.730 --> 02:20:46.590
Ballistic Missiles at a moment’s notice.

02:20:46.590 --> 02:20:55.320
In short, SCP-1984 could very easily and horrifyingly
quickly, trigger an all-out nuclear war, resulting

02:20:55.320 --> 02:20:58.690
in the extinction of all life on Earth.

02:20:58.690 --> 02:21:03.160
No wonder the Foundation considers the Dead
Hand such a massive threat.

02:21:03.160 --> 02:21:08.551
SCP-1984 itself actually consists of the preserved
remains of a Russian soldier by the name of

02:21:08.551 --> 02:21:14.420
Sergeant Marat Chernikov, who was killed during
the Soviet-Afghan War in 1982.

02:21:14.420 --> 02:21:18.650
Most of the official documentation of Chernikov’s
existence has been expunged by the Russian

02:21:18.650 --> 02:21:23.521
Federation, and he is only referenced in fragmented
documents recovered by the Foundation that

02:21:23.521 --> 02:21:26.080
refer to a ”Project December.”

02:21:26.080 --> 02:21:31.070
These remains serve as the location of a semi-sentient
consciousness that has been classified as

02:21:31.070 --> 02:21:34.200
SCP-1984-01.

02:21:34.200 --> 02:21:40.521
When it remains dormant and is not interfered
with, SCP-1984-01 has the ability to receive

02:21:40.521 --> 02:21:45.960
and process any signal broadcast to it, and
is able to decipher information contained

02:21:45.960 --> 02:21:47.860
in any signal it picks up.

02:21:47.860 --> 02:21:52.721
However, when global military tensions start
to climb, especially when those tensions affect

02:21:52.721 --> 02:21:57.480
the Russian Federation - or what were once
the satellite states of the former Soviet

02:21:57.480 --> 02:22:03.961
Union - the entity begins to manifest itself
in the physical world, and usually in various

02:22:03.961 --> 02:22:07.400
strange and differing ways.

02:22:07.400 --> 02:22:13.500
SCP-1984-01 has been known to appear in the
real world in forms such as a humanoid outline,

02:22:13.500 --> 02:22:18.561
or a bright-red specter, taking the shape
of a child with its legs cut off.

02:22:18.561 --> 02:22:22.800
Regardless of which shape it appears in, it
is when the Dead Hand manifests in a more

02:22:22.800 --> 02:22:27.301
physical form that the wider scope of its
abilities become clear.

02:22:27.301 --> 02:22:32.210
As mentioned previously, it can influence
and even launch nuclear weapons, overriding

02:22:32.210 --> 02:22:35.990
their command systems and bypassing launch
sequences.

02:22:35.990 --> 02:22:43.690
After appearing fully, SCP-1984-01 will travel
at speeds of up to 140 kilometers an hour,

02:22:43.690 --> 02:22:48.200
directly to the nearest military installation
capable of launching Intercontinental Ballistic

02:22:48.200 --> 02:22:49.311
Missiles.

02:22:49.311 --> 02:22:55.880
Once it reaches its destination, SCP-1984-01
will immediately attempt to override the necessary

02:22:55.880 --> 02:22:58.390
systems to initiate a launch.

02:22:58.390 --> 02:23:04.521
After firing missiles at their pre-determined
targets, SCP-1984-01 will hastily travel to

02:23:04.521 --> 02:23:09.600
another facility housing nuclear ordnance,
repeating the process until it has successfully

02:23:09.600 --> 02:23:12.240
launched all of Russia’s atomic weapons.

02:23:12.240 --> 02:23:18.771
When engaged, the physical manifestation of
SCP-1984 is highly aggressive, and will lethally

02:23:18.771 --> 02:23:23.500
defend itself against anyone that it observes
trying to interfere with it or stop it from

02:23:23.500 --> 02:23:25.780
causing a nuclear launch.

02:23:25.780 --> 02:23:31.090
The entity has displayed the ability to disrupt
the nervous system, causing excruciating pain

02:23:31.090 --> 02:23:33.580
and debilitating damage to human beings.

02:23:33.580 --> 02:23:39.420
Its only known weakness, if any, is a susceptibility
to microwave radiation.

02:23:39.420 --> 02:23:45.760
Although, exposure to this doesn’t seem
to cause any lasting damage to SCP-1984, instead

02:23:45.760 --> 02:23:49.620
only temporarily disorientating its physical
form.

02:23:49.620 --> 02:23:55.800
As for the origins of SCP-1984, the Soviet
Union’s official liaison with the SCP Foundation

02:23:55.800 --> 02:24:00.710
offered some clarity on this, ironically,
during early 1984.

02:24:00.710 --> 02:24:05.880
The liaison, a doctor named Sergei, described
information regarding the Dead Hand as being

02:24:05.880 --> 02:24:10.230
of “grave importance to the continued survival
of the human race”.

02:24:10.230 --> 02:24:14.370
Seeing as he was referring to an entity that
could single-handedly launch the Soviet Union’s

02:24:14.370 --> 02:24:17.530
entire nuclear arsenal, he certainly had a
point.

02:24:17.530 --> 02:24:22.420
A top-secret conference was held in Sarajevo
between the Foundation’s O5 Council and

02:24:22.420 --> 02:24:29.340
officials from both the USSR and United States,
using the 1984 Winter Olympics as cover, avoiding

02:24:29.340 --> 02:24:33.320
too many questions about several nations’
high-ranking state officials being in the

02:24:33.320 --> 02:24:34.870
same place at once.

02:24:34.870 --> 02:24:39.670
The O5 Council was then given full, in-depth
information about the Dead Hand, something

02:24:39.670 --> 02:24:44.780
they had previously assumed was a more conventional
form of nuclear deterrence.

02:24:44.780 --> 02:24:51.300
SCP-1984 was far more, and worse, far out
of the control of the Russian government.

02:24:51.300 --> 02:24:56.360
It was revealed that the entity had been designed
outside the original specifications given

02:24:56.360 --> 02:24:58.240
to those who developed it.

02:24:58.240 --> 02:25:03.630
Initially, the Soviets had intended for the
Dead Hand to be solely used as a secure second-strike

02:25:03.630 --> 02:25:04.730
response.

02:25:04.730 --> 02:25:10.771
If the leaders of the Soviet Union were killed,
SCP-1984 would react in-kind to Russia’s

02:25:10.771 --> 02:25:15.960
enemies, launching back all the USSR’s nuclear
missiles in retaliation.

02:25:15.960 --> 02:25:20.340
This is most likely where the “Dead Hand”
nickname comes from, hearkening to the idea

02:25:20.340 --> 02:25:22.510
of a “dead man’s switch.”

02:25:22.510 --> 02:25:26.280
Picture that you had been mortally wounded
by your worst enemy - shot in the stomach

02:25:26.280 --> 02:25:29.400
and laying on the ground, rapidly bleeding
out.

02:25:29.400 --> 02:25:33.380
You know that there is no chance you will
survive, but you cannot afford to let your

02:25:33.380 --> 02:25:34.650
enemy get away.

02:25:34.650 --> 02:25:40.300
What they don’t know is that you rigged
the entire building around you both with explosives.

02:25:40.300 --> 02:25:46.220
With your dying breath, you activate the detonator,
assuring your own and your enemy’s mutual

02:25:46.220 --> 02:25:47.220
destruction.

02:25:47.220 --> 02:25:49.900
Kaboom, then curtain for both of you.

02:25:49.900 --> 02:25:56.060
The pressing issue, and reason this secret
summit between the USSR, USA and SCP Foundation

02:25:56.060 --> 02:26:02.240
was held, was that SCP-1984 was no longer
interested in just waiting for Russia to be

02:26:02.240 --> 02:26:05.021
attacked and only reacting after the fact.

02:26:05.021 --> 02:26:09.730
Instead, the entity’s physical form was
trying to preemptively strike at the enemies

02:26:09.730 --> 02:26:14.500
of the Motherland, attempting to activate
nuclear launches and send atomic weapons to

02:26:14.500 --> 02:26:19.820
destroy the United States, France, West Germany,
and the People’s Republic of China.

02:26:19.820 --> 02:26:25.670
In other words, the Dead Hand was eager to
get a head start at causing total nuclear

02:26:25.670 --> 02:26:30.450
annihilation, not to mention potentially killing
millions and reducing target countries to

02:26:30.450 --> 02:26:36.110
little more than irradiated craters awash
with deadly nuclear fallout.

02:26:36.110 --> 02:26:41.120
Both the Soviet Union and United States begged
the SCP Foundation to intervene and contain

02:26:41.120 --> 02:26:47.710
SCP-1984, and under the direction of the O5
Council, they stepped in to take direct control

02:26:47.710 --> 02:26:53.960
of the situation, establishing new containment
procedures in the hopes of keeping SCP-1984

02:26:53.960 --> 02:26:56.880
from hitting as many launch buttons as it
could find.

02:26:56.880 --> 02:27:00.960
The embalmed remains that seemed to create
the manifestations of the Dead Hand entity

02:27:00.960 --> 02:27:06.440
were held securely in an Armed Containment
Complex, near Verkhoyansk, part of the Sakha

02:27:06.440 --> 02:27:08.920
Republic within the Russian Federation.

02:27:08.920 --> 02:27:14.500
SCP-1984 was placed within a standard humanoid
containment cell, which was itself held within

02:27:14.500 --> 02:27:20.470
a Faraday cage - a type of enclosure constructed
with or covered in conductive material, designed

02:27:20.470 --> 02:27:22.970
to block electromagnetic fields.

02:27:22.970 --> 02:27:29.230
This was done to block any external broadcast
signals from reaching SCP-1984, and thus causing

02:27:29.230 --> 02:27:34.070
it to manifest its other form if it learned
of an impending attack on Russia - even a

02:27:34.070 --> 02:27:38.021
fictional one, perhaps as part of a television
broadcast.

02:27:38.021 --> 02:27:42.010
As part of the Dead Hand’s containment,
all signals broadcast near its cage are to

02:27:42.010 --> 02:27:47.011
be monitored, and only broadcasts featuring
doctored information would reach the entity.

02:27:47.011 --> 02:27:53.200
Thanks to the work of the SCP Foundation’s
Information Control Team, SCP-1984 is drip-fed

02:27:53.200 --> 02:27:56.190
a stream of carefully-fabricated information.

02:27:56.190 --> 02:28:01.620
Using on-site equipment and facilities, a
team of military historians, economists, actors,

02:28:01.620 --> 02:28:06.890
and Soviet media specialists, the Information
Control Team have created an ongoing narrative

02:28:06.890 --> 02:28:09.280
wherein the Cold War never ended.

02:28:09.280 --> 02:28:15.550
Through falsified radio and television broadcasts
made to look like era-appropriate news organizations,

02:28:15.550 --> 02:28:21.450
they manage to keep SCP-1984 convinced that
the nuclear stalemate between the Soviet Union

02:28:21.450 --> 02:28:24.780
and United States is still ongoing.

02:28:24.780 --> 02:28:29.200
Unfortunately, sometimes information from
the real world is able to bleed through.

02:28:29.200 --> 02:28:35.850
An incident involving SCP-1984 occurred on
August 8th, 1984, and almost brought forth

02:28:35.850 --> 02:28:37.840
a full-scale nuclear war.

02:28:37.840 --> 02:28:42.370
While preparing for a campaign speech, the
fortieth President of the United States Ronald

02:28:42.370 --> 02:28:46.971
Reagan uttered the following joke: “I'm
pleased to tell you today that I've signed

02:28:46.971 --> 02:28:52.560
legislation that will outlaw Russia forever,
we begin bombing in five minutes.”

02:28:52.560 --> 02:28:57.660
Naturally, thousands of media outlets reported
on this, but signals of those broadcasts,

02:28:57.660 --> 02:29:03.180
including recordings of the President’s
remark, made their way to SCP-1984.

02:29:03.180 --> 02:29:06.820
Foundation researchers were both unsure of
how these transmissions breached the Faraday

02:29:06.820 --> 02:29:12.301
cage surrounding the Dead Hand, and were unable
to prevent what happened next.

02:29:12.301 --> 02:29:16.970
Hearing what it could only assume was a genuine
declaration of war, the manifestation of the

02:29:16.970 --> 02:29:24.680
SCP-1984-01 entity happened immediately after
Reagan’s words reached its containment cell.

02:29:24.680 --> 02:29:30.440
This time, the entity appeared as a semi-transparent
woman, withered and blue, wearing traditional

02:29:30.440 --> 02:29:32.230
Pashtun dress.

02:29:32.230 --> 02:29:38.190
Armed personnel guarding SCP-1984’s cage
engaged the entity, causing it to retaliate,

02:29:38.190 --> 02:29:41.431
lashing out and attacking any that stood in
its way.

02:29:41.431 --> 02:29:46.210
One captain and two privates that suffered
the brunt of SCP-1984’s offensive capabilities

02:29:46.210 --> 02:29:50.130
began bleeding heavily from their ears, seizing
violently.

02:29:50.130 --> 02:29:54.920
Most gruesomely of all, a liquid leaked from
their eyes and nose, believed to have been

02:29:54.920 --> 02:29:56.350
cerebrospinal fluid.

02:29:56.350 --> 02:30:01.240
While armed personnel attempted to utilize
microwave emitting weaponry to slow the entity

02:30:01.240 --> 02:30:06.590
down, the Information Control Team was frantically
trying to record a new falsified broadcast

02:30:06.590 --> 02:30:12.230
in the hopes that SCP-1984 would cease its
hostilities and the entity would dissipate

02:30:12.230 --> 02:30:16.910
before it was able to travel at high speed
to any nearby Soviet nuclear facilities and

02:30:16.910 --> 02:30:19.780
begin bombarding the United States.

02:30:19.780 --> 02:30:25.280
Their first attempt to record a new broadcast
that would result in a cessation of SCP-1984’s

02:30:25.280 --> 02:30:29.510
slaughter of its guards was interrupted when
a nearby wall collapsed.

02:30:29.510 --> 02:30:33.930
Desperate to re-contain the creature, Information
Control Team tried to film the broadcast a

02:30:33.930 --> 02:30:38.521
second time, and succeeded, although one of
the actors portraying a newsreader suffered

02:30:38.521 --> 02:30:44.230
a stroke and had to be edited out, to convince
the Dead Hand of its supposed authenticity.

02:30:44.230 --> 02:30:48.660
What followed was a short news clip, clarifying
that Russia’s Politburo was in on President

02:30:48.660 --> 02:30:53.421
Reagan’s joke, including old footage of
the USSR’s General Secretary Konstantin

02:30:53.421 --> 02:30:59.181
Chernenko confirming that the Soviet Union’s
nuclear forces were not on high alert.

02:30:59.181 --> 02:31:05.660
Miraculously, after fifteen more minutes of
sustained fighting with guards, SCP-1984’s

02:31:05.660 --> 02:31:10.630
physical manifestation began to disappear,
gradually dematerializing while it seemed

02:31:10.630 --> 02:31:13.350
to attack with far-less intensity.

02:31:13.350 --> 02:31:17.650
Eventually, containment was re-established,
at the cost of the deaths of seventeen members

02:31:17.650 --> 02:31:21.280
of Foundation staff, both guards and researchers.

02:31:21.280 --> 02:31:25.970
A further eight, however, suffered traumatic
brain injuries that left them all permanently

02:31:25.970 --> 02:31:27.060
disabled.

02:31:27.060 --> 02:31:32.940
The one upside is that this incident remains
the only time SCP-1984 has ever breached its

02:31:32.940 --> 02:31:37.580
containment and caused harm to others, and
considering that it could very well have launched

02:31:37.580 --> 02:31:42.110
Russia’s nuclear arsenal, the casualties
could easily have been much higher.

02:31:42.110 --> 02:31:47.840
So remember, folks, don’t joke about nuclear
weapons, especially if you’re the president.

02:31:47.840 --> 02:31:53.250
You never know what anomalies lurking in the
bodies of dead Russian soldiers might be listening,

02:31:53.250 --> 02:31:57.550
and if they are, you better hope they have
a sense of humor.

02:31:57.550 --> 02:32:03.110
It’s 3:00 AM, and the facility is quiet.

02:32:03.110 --> 02:32:05.630
Office workers and administrators roam the
halls.

02:32:05.630 --> 02:32:10.460
Security Officers stand at their posts, clad
in advanced tactical armour and carrying standard-issue

02:32:10.460 --> 02:32:11.610
M4 Carbines.

02:32:11.610 --> 02:32:15.500
Three Foundation employees sit at flickering
monitors, watching a live feed of footage

02:32:15.500 --> 02:32:21.010
from the containment cell of the infamous
SCP-106, or as it’s referred to by staff,

02:32:21.010 --> 02:32:22.040
The Old Man.

02:32:22.040 --> 02:32:26.610
No Foundation personnel are permitted to travel
within sixty feet of the cell for security

02:32:26.610 --> 02:32:30.540
reasons, and nobody is permitted to physically
interact with the anomaly without the approval

02:32:30.540 --> 02:32:32.870
of two-thirds of O5-Command.

02:32:32.870 --> 02:32:37.350
The observer’s eyes itch and sting from
the hours of unending blue-light exposure,

02:32:37.350 --> 02:32:38.840
but they can’t look away.

02:32:38.840 --> 02:32:43.230
The Old Man is crafty – he may have the
insatiable bloodlust of a hungry great white

02:32:43.230 --> 02:32:44.430
shark, but he’s not mindless.

02:32:44.430 --> 02:32:49.300
He’s a calculating predator, more sadistic
than the worst human serial killer, and he’s

02:32:49.300 --> 02:32:51.340
always searching for the next opportunity.

02:32:51.340 --> 02:32:55.141
According to Foundation records, he’s been
active since at least World War II, and it

02:32:55.141 --> 02:32:58.810
is estimated that he has hundreds if not thousands
of victims to his name.

02:32:58.810 --> 02:33:03.660
And many of those made the simple, but extremely
foolish mistake of underestimating him.

02:33:03.660 --> 02:33:08.380
After all, it only takes a few seconds of
inattentiveness, the briefest moment of distraction,

02:33:08.380 --> 02:33:09.990
to give him the window he needs.

02:33:09.990 --> 02:33:11.510
To do what, you ask?

02:33:11.510 --> 02:33:14.650
Oh, don’t worry, you’ll find out – just
like they did.

02:33:14.650 --> 02:33:19.740
The Old Man has his nickname for a reason
– most of the time, he really does look

02:33:19.740 --> 02:33:21.320
like exactly that - an old man.

02:33:21.320 --> 02:33:26.500
Or more specifically, an Old Man’s decaying
corpse, his body covered in rotten, dark greyish-black

02:33:26.500 --> 02:33:28.850
flesh that looks like putrid meat.

02:33:28.850 --> 02:33:32.850
Though the creature has been observed being
able to change shape, the rot seems to run

02:33:32.850 --> 02:33:35.530
too deep for the Old Man to ever hide it.

02:33:35.530 --> 02:33:40.080
Foundation employees that have observed SCP-106
for extended periods of time have reported

02:33:40.080 --> 02:33:44.930
seeing it assume the form of grinning, decayed
children, and women whose rotted flesh barely

02:33:44.930 --> 02:33:46.910
hangs on to their creaking bones.

02:33:46.910 --> 02:33:51.200
Just seeing the images through a video feed
is enough to cause a lifetime of insomnia

02:33:51.200 --> 02:33:52.630
and other sleeping issues.

02:33:52.630 --> 02:33:56.210
Still, they have a job to do, and the cameras
remain fixed on the Old Man.

02:33:56.210 --> 02:34:00.341
He’s been completely motionless for three
months, just sitting there, like a Buddhist

02:34:00.341 --> 02:34:01.820
monk in deep meditation.

02:34:01.820 --> 02:34:05.801
A novice might see this period of inactivity
as a cause for celebration, but those with

02:34:05.801 --> 02:34:09.560
experience know that this is merely the calm
before the storm.

02:34:09.560 --> 02:34:13.470
SCP–106 can remain in a dormant state for
months at a time.

02:34:13.470 --> 02:34:17.360
Described by Foundation scientists as a “lulling
state,” it is believed that The Old Man

02:34:17.360 --> 02:34:21.840
is simply waiting for its captors to get soft,
make a mistake, or simply have a momentary

02:34:21.840 --> 02:34:25.160
lapse in concentration, which is all it needs
to make its move.

02:34:25.160 --> 02:34:29.101
It had happened so many times before, and
it was about to happen again.

02:34:29.101 --> 02:34:33.750
One of the observers must have felt an overwhelming
wave of anxiety when he saw the creature ever

02:34:33.750 --> 02:34:35.921
so slightly twitch.

02:34:35.921 --> 02:34:38.060
Just a tiny quiver in the shoulder muscles.

02:34:38.060 --> 02:34:42.170
But that was enough to tell the observer that
their day had just taken a terrifying turn.

02:34:42.170 --> 02:34:46.420
He grabbed the emergency phone fixed to his
desk and practically screamed into the receiver

02:34:46.420 --> 02:34:50.100
that 106 is moving, that they needed a tactical
team stat.

02:34:50.100 --> 02:34:51.101
But it was already too late.

02:34:51.101 --> 02:34:55.480
He and the two other observers stared into
the monitors with their mouths agape, as a

02:34:55.480 --> 02:34:59.540
gooey, rust-like substance began to pool around
the creature on the floor of its cell.

02:34:59.540 --> 02:35:02.090
Slowly, the creature craned its withered neck
around.

02:35:02.090 --> 02:35:05.851
Its face was fixed into a broad, yellow-toothed,
lipless grin.

02:35:05.851 --> 02:35:09.450
Its eyes had the kind of dull, flat malice
of an underwater predator.

02:35:09.450 --> 02:35:11.820
It looked directly into the camera.

02:35:11.820 --> 02:35:12.820
Directly at them.

02:35:12.820 --> 02:35:13.820
And smiled.

02:35:13.820 --> 02:35:15.660
The observers know this was bad.

02:35:15.660 --> 02:35:17.300
Really, really bad.

02:35:17.300 --> 02:35:22.120
With what they could have sworn was a little
nod, the Old Man began sinking into the rusty

02:35:22.120 --> 02:35:26.480
puddle it’d made on the ground beneath it,
until it had disappeared entirely.

02:35:26.480 --> 02:35:30.801
SCP–106 is capable of phasing through any
solid surface with ease, making it one of

02:35:30.801 --> 02:35:35.470
the hardest entities to reliably contain,
and earning it a spot on the dreaded “Keter”

02:35:35.470 --> 02:35:39.771
class – reserved for the anomalies that
are a complete nightmare to keep locked up.

02:35:39.771 --> 02:35:43.250
Through years of costly research and deadly
trial and error, the Foundation would later

02:35:43.250 --> 02:35:45.670
devise ways of at least slowing the creature
down.

02:35:45.670 --> 02:35:50.340
It’s shown to have an aversion to lead,
highly complex or random physical structures,

02:35:50.340 --> 02:35:52.100
and intense bright light.

02:35:52.100 --> 02:35:56.240
None of these cause harm to the creature – as
far as we know, literally nothing can – but

02:35:56.240 --> 02:36:01.801
they’ll at least buy you some precious extra
seconds with which to at least try and escape,

02:36:01.801 --> 02:36:04.070
seconds the three observers didn’t have.

02:36:04.070 --> 02:36:07.800
One of them grabbed the emergency line again
and barked into it that they had lost visual

02:36:07.800 --> 02:36:08.970
on the anomaly.

02:36:08.970 --> 02:36:13.720
Just then the observers heard a faint crackling
sound behind them, and the hissing of a chemical

02:36:13.720 --> 02:36:14.720
burn.

02:36:14.720 --> 02:36:18.720
They turned in horror to see a huge, rusty
burnmark expanding across the wall, right

02:36:18.720 --> 02:36:21.480
next to the door – which was their only
escape route.

02:36:21.480 --> 02:36:26.160
They backed as far away from the door as they
could as a rotten hand began reaching out

02:36:26.160 --> 02:36:31.990
of the mass of corrosive, black sludge, followed
by the grinning face of SCP–106, ready to

02:36:31.990 --> 02:36:33.190
have some fun.

02:36:33.190 --> 02:36:37.340
Meanwhile, two heavily-armed Security Officers
– Agents Goodwin and Resnick – came charging

02:36:37.340 --> 02:36:39.420
down the corridor towards the observation
rooms.

02:36:39.420 --> 02:36:44.170
It’d become a bleak slogan during SCP–106
escape attempts that all you need to do is

02:36:44.170 --> 02:36:45.170
“follow the screams.”

02:36:45.170 --> 02:36:48.800
And that motto was proven true that night,
because awful things were happening to the

02:36:48.800 --> 02:36:52.050
observation personnel, they were certainly
screaming about it.

02:36:52.050 --> 02:36:55.970
Of course, even with top-of-the-line firearms,
there was little they could do to harm the

02:36:55.970 --> 02:36:57.490
rampaging Old Man.

02:36:57.490 --> 02:37:00.021
He seemed immune to all forms of physical
damage.

02:37:00.021 --> 02:37:03.920
All they could hope to do was keep the thing
distracted until the scientists and containment

02:37:03.920 --> 02:37:08.420
specialists finished the preparations to lure
him back into his containment cell.

02:37:08.420 --> 02:37:11.260
Goodwin surged forward while Resnick covered
his six.

02:37:11.260 --> 02:37:16.160
Vigilance was key, as – unlike a standard
human combatant – SCP–106 could attack

02:37:16.160 --> 02:37:19.490
from literally any angle including above or
below.

02:37:19.490 --> 02:37:23.091
Physical obstacles were irrelevant to him
and no cover was safe.

02:37:23.091 --> 02:37:26.880
The hardened security officers could see the
burnmark on the wall of the observation room

02:37:26.880 --> 02:37:28.330
as they approached.

02:37:28.330 --> 02:37:33.470
SCP–106 was perpetually coated in a thick,
black mucus with powerful corrosive properties

02:37:33.470 --> 02:37:36.660
that left any surfaces it touched permanently
marred.

02:37:36.660 --> 02:37:40.740
Foundation Scientists speculated that this
mucus served as a kind of pre-digestive substance

02:37:40.740 --> 02:37:45.670
that tenderizes meat and bone alike, but to
what purpose this serves is a mystery as the

02:37:45.670 --> 02:37:47.620
Old Man has never been observed eating.

02:37:47.620 --> 02:37:50.940
It’s postulated that the only purpose is
causing additional pain.

02:37:50.940 --> 02:37:55.680
Goodwin and Resnick knew to treat this hissing
sludge as a potential threat, as the corrosive

02:37:55.680 --> 02:37:59.900
properties would remain active for as much
as six hours before finally fizzling out.

02:37:59.900 --> 02:38:04.540
The two officers shared a quiet nod, before
Goodwin breached the observation room door

02:38:04.540 --> 02:38:05.720
with a hard kick.

02:38:05.720 --> 02:38:08.181
The two of them surged inside, guns at the
ready.

02:38:08.181 --> 02:38:12.580
In their time working at the Foundation, they’d
seen some truly horrific sights.

02:38:12.580 --> 02:38:17.700
From the mutilation of D-Class Personnel – typically
death row prison inmates brought in for use

02:38:17.700 --> 02:38:21.440
as SCP guinea pigs – to the violence and
mayhem of a containment breach.

02:38:21.440 --> 02:38:25.280
But there was nothing in their past that would
ever make the horrifying sight they saw in

02:38:25.280 --> 02:38:28.181
the observation room that night feel “normal.”

02:38:28.181 --> 02:38:30.080
All three observers were dead.

02:38:30.080 --> 02:38:34.580
Almost nothing remained of two of them, and
the third, while still intact, no longer looked

02:38:34.580 --> 02:38:35.580
human.

02:38:35.580 --> 02:38:38.800
He looked like he’d somehow been dead a
hundred years in the brief period that the

02:38:38.800 --> 02:38:39.860
Old Man had been free.

02:38:39.860 --> 02:38:44.910
His skin was grey and completely dried out,
and his mouth was locked into a perpetual

02:38:44.910 --> 02:38:45.910
scream.

02:38:45.910 --> 02:38:48.300
It was a massacre, but there was no sign of
the Old Man.

02:38:48.300 --> 02:38:52.300
Goodwin grabbed his radio, and whispered “This
is Goodwin in observation room six.

02:38:52.300 --> 02:38:53.300
Requesting immediate back up.

02:38:53.300 --> 02:38:57.220
We have no idea where this thing—”
But his sentence was cut off by a sudden scream

02:38:57.220 --> 02:38:58.521
from Agent Resnick.

02:38:58.521 --> 02:39:02.500
SCP Foundation security officers are as tough
as nails – the best of the best, you might

02:39:02.500 --> 02:39:07.490
say, recruited from the top military organizations
in the world – so hearing one of them scream

02:39:07.490 --> 02:39:10.650
in fright is a rare if not impossible occurrence.

02:39:10.650 --> 02:39:14.920
But even they were forced to yell out in fear
when they looked up to see the Old Man standing

02:39:14.920 --> 02:39:17.090
on the ceiling, grinning down at them.

02:39:17.090 --> 02:39:21.250
Resnick raised his M4 and shot a three-round
burst at center mass.

02:39:21.250 --> 02:39:23.000
SCP–106 didn’t care.

02:39:23.000 --> 02:39:26.720
Even under sustained gunfire from the two
security officers, it didn’t even flinch.

02:39:26.720 --> 02:39:30.920
The Old Man simply reached down and snatched
Agent Resnick from the ground, like it was

02:39:30.920 --> 02:39:32.650
picking an apple from a tree.

02:39:32.650 --> 02:39:37.480
The Old Man held Resnick in one hand and pounded
its other rotten fist into the Agent’s body,

02:39:37.480 --> 02:39:39.030
breaking all of his bones.

02:39:39.030 --> 02:39:42.340
Resnick screamed for his partner to help him
but there was no time.

02:39:42.340 --> 02:39:47.260
Before Goodwin could do anything, SCP–106
began receding back into another slimy burnmark

02:39:47.260 --> 02:39:48.311
on the wall.

02:39:48.311 --> 02:39:51.140
Only this time, he was taking his screaming
victim with him.

02:39:51.140 --> 02:39:55.920
Agent Resnick gave one more horrified scream
before he was pulled backwards into the inky

02:39:55.920 --> 02:40:00.420
darkness leaving the room silent except for
the burning hiss of the corrosive goo left

02:40:00.420 --> 02:40:01.420
behind.

02:40:01.420 --> 02:40:03.830
You might think this would be the end of it,
but no.

02:40:03.830 --> 02:40:06.910
For poor Agent Resnick, the worst was yet
to come.

02:40:06.910 --> 02:40:11.880
He was being dragged into what SCP Foundation
scientists refer to as the Old Man’s “Pocket

02:40:11.880 --> 02:40:16.420
Dimension”, a miniature layer of reality
within our own where the malicious SCP is

02:40:16.420 --> 02:40:19.800
essentially a cruel, all-powerful God.

02:40:19.800 --> 02:40:24.290
According to witness reports extracted from
victims who were taken to this little nightmare

02:40:24.290 --> 02:40:28.461
realm, the dimension resembles a series of
twisting, endless corridors where the Old

02:40:28.461 --> 02:40:32.910
Man stalks and tortures his captured victims
to the breaking point, manipulating space

02:40:32.910 --> 02:40:35.350
and time to its own sadistic ends.

02:40:35.350 --> 02:40:39.890
On rare occasions, the SCP will even release
its victims, just for the joy of hunting,

02:40:39.890 --> 02:40:42.990
capturing, and torturing them all over again.

02:40:42.990 --> 02:40:47.100
While Agent Resnick was learning the true
meaning of terror, containment specialists

02:40:47.100 --> 02:40:51.900
were mobilizing in its cell, preparing the
one known tried-and-true method of luring

02:40:51.900 --> 02:40:56.360
the Old Man back: Tempting it with the prospect
of causing even more suffering.

02:40:56.360 --> 02:41:00.830
In order to do this, Foundation personnel
take one of the aforementioned Class D personnel

02:41:00.830 --> 02:41:06.681
and begin inducing extreme pain by breaking
a major bone or slicing a tendon every twenty

02:41:06.681 --> 02:41:07.681
minutes.

02:41:07.681 --> 02:41:11.690
The victim’s agonized screams are then played
over the facility’s intercom, acting as

02:41:11.690 --> 02:41:13.900
bait for the pain-loving Old Man.

02:41:13.900 --> 02:41:18.350
The screams echo through the facility’s
otherwise silent halls, as the mutilated corpse

02:41:18.350 --> 02:41:21.570
of Agent Resnick falls from a new scorch mark
on the ceiling.

02:41:21.570 --> 02:41:25.303
The Old Man can hear the sounds of suffering
ringing out through the air around him, and

02:41:25.303 --> 02:41:29.240
he can barely contain his excitement over
the prospect of a new plaything.

02:41:29.240 --> 02:41:33.910
The snapped femurs, the torn Achilles tendons,
it was all too good to miss.

02:41:33.910 --> 02:41:38.620
Having had its twisted fun with the security
officers and observers, SCP-106 wandered back

02:41:38.620 --> 02:41:42.080
to its containment cell, where a new screaming
victim awaited.

02:41:42.080 --> 02:41:46.830
The other security officers, containment specialists,
and scientists evacuated the area, leaving

02:41:46.830 --> 02:41:48.900
the Old Man alone with his prey.

02:41:48.900 --> 02:41:52.580
While the unfortunate Class D was left to
his fate, the rest of the staff commenced

02:41:52.580 --> 02:41:57.090
clean up procedures, which mainly involved
wiping the brown and black mucus from the

02:41:57.090 --> 02:41:58.090
walls.

02:41:58.090 --> 02:42:01.460
It would probably be at least another month
before anything like this happened again,

02:42:01.460 --> 02:42:05.391
and new personnel would be transferred over
to the facility to replace the fallen.

02:42:05.391 --> 02:42:10.271
All in all, just another night at The SCP
Foundation.

02:42:10.271 --> 02:42:16.690
“Every time a bell rings, an angel gets
its wings.” It’s an iconic line from “It’s

02:42:16.690 --> 02:42:22.341
a Wonderful Life” and even if you don’t
believe in angels, it’s a pleasant image.

02:42:22.341 --> 02:42:27.240
After all, angels are the embodiment of goodness
and light, so an angel getting its wings has

02:42:27.240 --> 02:42:29.040
to be a good thing, right?

02:42:29.040 --> 02:42:34.900
Well, as anyone who’s ever eaten one too
many Christmas cookies can tell you, it’s

02:42:34.900 --> 02:42:40.011
possible to have too much of a good thing.
Even something that sounds like the embodiment

02:42:40.011 --> 02:42:46.550
of all things good, in theory, can become
deadly when taken to the extreme.

02:42:46.550 --> 02:42:53.420
That is where SCP-469, also known as the Many-Winged
Angel, comes in. This angel-like creature

02:42:53.420 --> 02:42:59.530
is anything but innocent and in fact it has
been responsible for multiple deaths of SCP

02:42:59.530 --> 02:43:02.030
Foundation personnel during its captivity.

02:43:02.030 --> 02:43:09.880
SCP-469 may be deadly, but it is also undeniably
beautiful in a mysterious, ethereal way. When

02:43:09.880 --> 02:43:15.370
first seen, it appears to be nothing more
than a massive pile of pristine white feathers,

02:43:15.370 --> 02:43:19.830
like something out of a pillow commercial.
The pile measures 24 feet in diameter and

02:43:19.830 --> 02:43:22.091
weighs at least two tons.

02:43:22.091 --> 02:43:26.980
However, like many of the entities contained
within the walls of the Foundation’s countless

02:43:26.980 --> 02:43:31.940
containment sites, all is not as it seems
with 469.

02:43:31.940 --> 02:43:36.650
What looks at first glance to be a pile of
feathers is in fact a dense, curled mass of

02:43:36.650 --> 02:43:43.370
giant wings. The wings vary in size, from
the tiny wingspan of a sparrow to the staggering

02:43:43.370 --> 02:43:46.990
3.6-meter wingspan of the wandering albatross.

02:43:46.990 --> 02:43:51.810
The one thing they have in common is their
plumage, with each wing being covered with

02:43:51.810 --> 02:43:56.410
the same glossy white feathers.
The Foundation was able to perform a series

02:43:56.410 --> 02:44:01.880
of X-rays on the mass of wings, revealing
a skeletal structure beneath the feathers.

02:44:01.880 --> 02:44:07.340
Like those of a bird, the wings’ bones are
hollow. However, they are unnaturally soft

02:44:07.340 --> 02:44:12.771
and flexible, allowing for a range of motion
that no known birds possess. This accounts

02:44:12.771 --> 02:44:17.610
for the curvature of the wings, and their
ability to coil tightly into each other.

02:44:17.610 --> 02:44:22.940
The X-Ray also provided the first, and only
recorded glimpse at the creature hiding underneath

02:44:22.940 --> 02:44:27.860
all of the feathers. At the center of the
layers upon layers of wings, is a humanoid

02:44:27.860 --> 02:44:33.090
creature, curled into a fetal position. In
defiance of the laws of natural anatomy, every

02:44:33.090 --> 02:44:38.650
single wing appears to be fused to this creature’s
spine. How it would be able to move under

02:44:38.650 --> 02:44:43.500
the weight of these wings, or if it even could,
is as of yet unknown.

02:44:43.500 --> 02:44:51.460
The first Foundation personnel lost to SCP-469
were D-class personnel, D-112 and D-624, who

02:44:51.460 --> 02:44:55.700
were sent in to investigate the nature of
the creature and attempt to contact the humanoid

02:44:55.700 --> 02:44:58.170
entity identified on the X-rays.

02:44:58.170 --> 02:45:04.341
112 and 624 entered the room, equipped with
gloves and protective eye gear, as scientists

02:45:04.341 --> 02:45:09.680
watched the situation unfold on a monitor
on a video feed. No one expected much to come

02:45:09.680 --> 02:45:13.840
of the encounter, the working hypothesis was
that the layers of feathers would simply be

02:45:13.840 --> 02:45:20.190
too thick to get through without seriously
damaging the bones. It would be a fairly uneventful

02:45:20.190 --> 02:45:22.150
experiment, or so they thought.

02:45:22.150 --> 02:45:28.021
112 approached SCP-469 first, and attempted
to part a section of its feathers with his

02:45:28.021 --> 02:45:33.840
hands as 624 stood back and observed. The
moment his fingertips touched the feathers,

02:45:33.840 --> 02:45:39.330
a rustling sound filled the room. The feathers
began to quiver, shaking as if all waking

02:45:39.330 --> 02:45:41.740
up at once from a deep slumber.

02:45:41.740 --> 02:45:46.510
Suddenly, in the blink of an eye, the hundreds
and hundreds of wings unfurled and pulled

02:45:46.510 --> 02:45:52.280
112 into their depths. He was swept into a
swirl of feathers and, within seconds, he

02:45:52.280 --> 02:45:54.020
had disappeared from view completely.

02:45:54.020 --> 02:45:58.990
Though 624 and the scientists watching on
their monitor could no longer see him…they

02:45:58.990 --> 02:46:03.560
could certainly hear him. From the moment
the wings pulled him into the sea of feathers,

02:46:03.560 --> 02:46:09.760
112 had begun to scream in agony. His screams
persisted, growing louder and more desperate

02:46:09.760 --> 02:46:12.370
even as his voice cracked and grew weak.

02:46:12.370 --> 02:46:19.311
624 stood, frozen in place with fear and shock.
624 was ordered to attempt to retrieve 112

02:46:19.311 --> 02:46:24.820
from the feathers, but made no move to do
so. He simply stared at the feathers, eyes

02:46:24.820 --> 02:46:29.820
wide, face pale, as the pained shrieks of
his colleague bounced off the sterile white

02:46:29.820 --> 02:46:31.470
walls of the containment room.

02:46:31.470 --> 02:46:36.170
A guard called over the intercom, threatening
to terminate 624 if he did not attempt to

02:46:36.170 --> 02:46:43.210
remove 112 from SCP-469’s grasp. This warning
seemed to shock 624 back into action, and

02:46:43.210 --> 02:46:48.600
he made a run for the center feathers, arms
up to protect his face. Again, the sound of

02:46:48.600 --> 02:46:54.600
rustling as the feathers began to quake. The
wings unfurled once more, and 624 did not

02:46:54.600 --> 02:46:58.810
even have a chance to turn back before he
was pulled in to meet the same dismal fate

02:46:58.810 --> 02:47:00.810
as the other D Class.

02:47:00.810 --> 02:47:04.770
His screams of pain joined 112’s as the
scientists watching over the monitor could

02:47:04.770 --> 02:47:06.720
do nothing to help them.

02:47:06.720 --> 02:47:11.011
As the two men cried out in pain and horror
from within their winged prison, something

02:47:11.011 --> 02:47:16.190
began to happen that the scientists had never
seen before. The feathers started to shake

02:47:16.190 --> 02:47:21.650
again, faster than before, and at first the
wings appeared to be unfolding again. Upon

02:47:21.650 --> 02:47:26.970
a second look though, it was clear they hadn’t
moved. There were simply more of them now.

02:47:26.970 --> 02:47:31.550
As the men continued to scream, more feathers
appeared, the wings stretching out and elongating,

02:47:31.550 --> 02:47:35.090
and new wings sprouting as if from nowhere.

02:47:35.090 --> 02:47:41.440
SCP-469 appeared to be feeding on the sound
of the screams, and using that energy to expand.

02:47:41.440 --> 02:47:45.950
It was only after several long minutes when
the screaming finally stopped that the growth

02:47:45.950 --> 02:47:52.150
of the wings did too. At this point, the wings
shifted again, expelling the bodies of 112

02:47:52.150 --> 02:47:57.010
and 624 from within its folds, dropping them
limply on the ground.

02:47:57.010 --> 02:48:02.880
Though their deaths were unfortunate, the
loss of 112 and 624 did reveal some new information

02:48:02.880 --> 02:48:09.050
about SCP-469. First, that it could grow,
and apparently needed to feed on sounds in

02:48:09.050 --> 02:48:14.180
order to do so. Second, that it was deadly
to humans, and most likely to other living

02:48:14.180 --> 02:48:20.830
things as well. Autopsies of 112 and 624’s
corpses would later reveal exactly what happened

02:48:20.830 --> 02:48:25.400
to them, and how SCP-469 kills its prey.

02:48:25.400 --> 02:48:30.011
Though 469’s feathers may look as though
they would be soft to the touch, each feather

02:48:30.011 --> 02:48:34.621
is actually made up of sharp barbs that are
capable of piercing clothing and skin. These

02:48:34.621 --> 02:48:39.630
barbs release a neurotoxin into the system
that activates every pain receptor in the

02:48:39.630 --> 02:48:43.730
body of the victim. The neurotoxin present
in these feathers has not been identified

02:48:43.730 --> 02:48:48.670
anywhere else in nature, but is somewhat similar
in structure and function to the neurotoxins

02:48:48.670 --> 02:48:53.590
excreted by the cone snail and in the bite
of the blue-ringed octopus.

02:48:53.590 --> 02:48:58.320
In addition to the pain-inducing neurotoxin,
the feathers also carry several unidentified

02:48:58.320 --> 02:49:00.240
stimulant compounds, which seem to be intended
to keep the victim awake for as long as possible.

02:49:00.240 --> 02:49:04.090
Where the neurotoxin on its own would induce
enough pain for the affected party to pass

02:49:04.090 --> 02:49:08.880
out almost immediately, the stimulants serve
to keep the victim conscious.

02:49:08.880 --> 02:49:15.070
SCP Foundation scientists posit that this
is so that 469 can get as much noise, in this

02:49:15.070 --> 02:49:20.050
case in the form of screams, out of its prey
as possible and achieve maximum growth before

02:49:20.050 --> 02:49:23.360
the captive creature dies or goes into shock.

02:49:23.360 --> 02:49:28.610
Further experiments confirmed this theory,
and showed that SCP 469 will react similarly

02:49:28.610 --> 02:49:34.341
when exposed to any living creature capable
of making a sound and experiencing pain, not

02:49:34.341 --> 02:49:41.280
just humans. Nonliving matter though, including
dead animals, elicit no response from 469.

02:49:41.280 --> 02:49:45.940
Additional experiments were then undertaken
that involved applying various different sounds

02:49:45.940 --> 02:49:52.050
in order to test their effect on SCP 469’s
growth. Though it feeds on any sounds produced

02:49:52.050 --> 02:49:57.970
in its presence, it seems to respond most
strongly to musical sounds, exhibiting a particularly

02:49:57.970 --> 02:50:03.880
strong response to classical music. No sound
elicits a stronger reaction from this creature,

02:50:03.880 --> 02:50:06.890
however, than the sound of ringing bells.

02:50:06.890 --> 02:50:11.320
It is rumored that when a bell was rung in
the presence of the 469, the humanoid at the

02:50:11.320 --> 02:50:16.180
center of the feathers is said to have moved
for the first time on record. Apparently whatever

02:50:16.180 --> 02:50:22.090
is at the center appeared to “wake up”
and unfurl its wings, revealing itself. But

02:50:22.090 --> 02:50:25.950
unfortunately, all security footage of this
incident has been wiped, and the data has

02:50:25.950 --> 02:50:30.300
been expunged from the record leaving the
creature’s true form a mystery.

02:50:30.300 --> 02:50:34.390
You may be remembering that four members of
Foundation personnel on record were lost to

02:50:34.390 --> 02:50:41.040
SCP-469, and that only two have been mentioned
so far. The second pair of casualties resulted

02:50:41.040 --> 02:50:46.770
when the Foundation attempted to terminate
SCP 469. Believing that there was no more

02:50:46.770 --> 02:50:51.350
scientific benefit to keeping it alive, or
at least that the possible benefits did not

02:50:51.350 --> 02:50:57.500
outweigh the risks, it was ordered that SCP-469
be terminated by any means necessary.

02:50:57.500 --> 02:51:03.570
Two skilled personnel, Dr. Jones and Dr. Smith,
were sent into the containment facility in

02:51:03.570 --> 02:51:08.620
hazmat gear, armed with several sharp instruments.
They were to attempt to use these to cut through

02:51:08.620 --> 02:51:13.080
the forest of feathers and soft bones until
they could reach the humanoid at the center.

02:51:13.080 --> 02:51:17.650
It was presumed that once they did and the
creature no longer had the protection of its

02:51:17.650 --> 02:51:21.650
poisonous feathers, that it would be relatively
easy to kill.

02:51:21.650 --> 02:51:24.340
They could not have been more wrong.

02:51:24.340 --> 02:51:28.800
Dr. Jones approached the dense cover of feathers
with a pair of sharpened gardening shears,

02:51:28.800 --> 02:51:34.310
while Dr. Smith opted for a machete. At first,
the strategy appeared to be working. Dr. Jones

02:51:34.310 --> 02:51:39.060
made several quick cuts, with feathers fluttering
to the floor and sticking to the Hazmat suit

02:51:39.060 --> 02:51:44.920
as Dr. Smith slashed into the feathers with
his machete, making similarly promising progress.

02:51:44.920 --> 02:51:50.180
However, the situation quickly took a turn
for the worse when Dr. Jones dropped her shears

02:51:50.180 --> 02:51:54.851
and let out a bloodcurdling scream. The feathers
had taken a little longer to get through the

02:51:54.851 --> 02:52:00.530
suit to her skin, but somehow they had managed
to find a way just the same. Smith grabbed

02:52:00.530 --> 02:52:05.320
Jones and attempted to make a break for it,
but he was too slow, and far too close to

02:52:05.320 --> 02:52:10.510
the feathers already. The wings wrapped around
them and swallowed them both up, leaving the

02:52:10.510 --> 02:52:15.320
researchers outside helpless to do anything
but listen to their screams and wait for them

02:52:15.320 --> 02:52:22.590
to go quiet. After several minutes they finally
did, and the now larger SCP-469 had officially

02:52:22.590 --> 02:52:25.480
claimed another two Foundation personnel.

02:52:25.480 --> 02:52:30.391
Obviously, 469 could not be terminated using
any methods that would place the responsible

02:52:30.391 --> 02:52:36.240
personnel within its grasp. That was simply
too risky. So, the Foundation selected a team

02:52:36.240 --> 02:52:41.340
of D-class personnel to attempt to burn the
feathers with an array of flamethrowers. Though

02:52:41.340 --> 02:52:46.630
the feathers were vulnerable to fire and began
to blacken and disintegrate on contact, the

02:52:46.630 --> 02:52:52.720
sound of the flames being expelled was loud
enough to feed SCP 469. Its growth was so

02:52:52.720 --> 02:52:57.220
quick in response to the noise that the fire
could not keep up with the amount of new feathers

02:52:57.220 --> 02:52:59.370
and wings being produced.

02:52:59.370 --> 02:53:05.260
By the time the flamethrowers ran out of propellant,
469 was the same size as it had been when

02:53:05.260 --> 02:53:08.960
they started. Other termination methods have
been discussed, including the possibility

02:53:08.960 --> 02:53:13.860
of submerging the entire creature in a highly
corrosive acid, but so far this has not yet

02:53:13.860 --> 02:53:19.330
been attempted. Whatever they end up trying,
it is clear that nothing that produces a significant

02:53:19.330 --> 02:53:22.420
amount of noise will be able to kill 469.

02:53:22.420 --> 02:53:28.180
So, where is it now? What’s become of this
perverse angel with the never-ending wings?

02:53:28.180 --> 02:53:32.090
Currently, it is kept isolated in an airtight,
soundproof chamber where nothing can trigger

02:53:32.090 --> 02:53:38.940
the growth of any more wings. So, what is
it? An angel? A demon? A twisted, mutated

02:53:38.940 --> 02:53:44.461
bird of some kind? It is entirely possible
we will never know. But It’s a Wonderful

02:53:44.461 --> 02:53:50.840
Life had something right, even if by accident.
Every time a bell rings, something, twisted

02:53:50.840 --> 02:53:56.920
and deadly though it may be, gets its wings.

02:53:56.920 --> 02:54:02.410
When an anomaly is first detected by an SCP
Foundation Field Agent, it’s up to the Foundation’s

02:54:02.410 --> 02:54:08.511
Mobile Task Forces to tag and bag the impossible
entities before they can do any more harm.

02:54:08.511 --> 02:54:14.160
Sometimes these retrievals are uneventful,
other times... not so much - especially when

02:54:14.160 --> 02:54:22.540
they’re dealing with brutal forces of nature
like SCP - 096, also known as, The Shy Guy.

02:54:22.540 --> 02:54:26.990
A creature that, from its very first interaction
with the Foundation, had a reputation for

02:54:26.990 --> 02:54:30.200
being dangerous and needed to be feared.

02:54:30.200 --> 02:54:34.480
A series of vague sightings and mysterious
disappearances up in the frosty mountains

02:54:34.480 --> 02:54:38.530
of the Yukon first sparked the Foundation’s
interest. When they were certain that they

02:54:38.530 --> 02:54:45.260
had an anomaly on their hands, two retrieval
teams - Zulu 9-A and Zulu 9-B - were dispatched

02:54:45.260 --> 02:54:50.480
to secure and contain the entity. Zulu 9-A
took the lead in a heavy duty chopper, equipped

02:54:50.480 --> 02:54:57.320
with .50 caliber GAU-19 Heavy Machine-Guns
and carrying an AT-4 Anti-Tank launcher. They

02:54:57.320 --> 02:55:02.870
were prepared for anything - or so they thought
- as they established a visual on SCP - 096

02:55:02.870 --> 02:55:05.380
while two clicks away from the target.

02:55:05.380 --> 02:55:10.320
They couldn’t get a clear line of sight
on the creature, but it appeared to be stationary,

02:55:10.320 --> 02:55:15.561
docile, and was making no attempt to flee.
Piece of cake, right? Little did they know

02:55:15.561 --> 02:55:21.320
that SCP - 096 was just looking away from
them. If it was facing towards them, it’d

02:55:21.320 --> 02:55:26.190
be a whole different horror story, as Zulu
9-A were about to find out.

02:55:26.190 --> 02:55:30.160
The team landed their helicopter next to the
creature and were shocked to see that it was

02:55:30.160 --> 02:55:34.790
completely naked, in spite of the subzero
temperatures all around them. The creature

02:55:34.790 --> 02:55:39.601
was unnaturally thin, as though it’d been
starved for weeks, with bone-white skin and

02:55:39.601 --> 02:55:44.150
unnaturally long limbs. The team guessed that
the creature’s arms must have been at least

02:55:44.150 --> 02:55:49.440
1.5 metres long, but its docile nature and
insubstantial body mass gave the impression

02:55:49.440 --> 02:55:54.840
that it wouldn’t prove too difficult to
contain. That is, until they saw its face.

02:55:54.840 --> 02:55:59.420
Zulu 9-A’s Captain was the lone survivor
of the incident, as he was lucky enough to

02:55:59.420 --> 02:56:04.130
be looking away when the creature turned towards
his team. The rest of the squad ended up staring

02:56:04.130 --> 02:56:10.741
eye to eye with SCP - 096, and from that moment
on it wasn’t docile anymore. The creature

02:56:10.741 --> 02:56:17.490
began to whimper, then cry, then sob uncontrollably
in a way that sounded eerily human. This sudden

02:56:17.490 --> 02:56:22.280
change in temperament startled the rest of
Zulu 9-A and they opened fire on the creature.

02:56:22.280 --> 02:56:27.840
Under the hail of gunfire, SCP - 096 entered
a murderous frenzy and began tearing into

02:56:27.840 --> 02:56:32.580
the hapless squad of soldiers. While its flesh
and organs did seem to take damage as a result

02:56:32.580 --> 02:56:37.790
of the barrage of .50 caliber rounds from
the helicopter mounted machine guns, its skeletal

02:56:37.790 --> 02:56:42.970
structure remained intact and it continued
its onslaught, tearing the team limb from

02:56:42.970 --> 02:56:47.620
limb even after they’d blown practically
all the flesh from the creature.

02:56:47.620 --> 02:56:52.040
The AT-4 Anti-Tank launcher proved equally
ineffective at stopping SCP - 096 while it

02:56:52.040 --> 02:56:57.351
was in attack mode, and it was only after
slaughtering the entire team that it returned

02:56:57.351 --> 02:57:02.040
to its docile state. Nobody knows exactly
what the creature did to Zulu 9-A after the

02:57:02.040 --> 02:57:06.130
gunfire started, but no trace of the team
was left behind.

02:57:06.130 --> 02:57:11.190
Zulu 9-B touched down soon after, and with
a grave warning from the Captain not to look

02:57:11.190 --> 02:57:16.720
at the creature’s face, they were finally
able to subdue it. A bag was placed over SCP

02:57:16.720 --> 02:57:21.770
- 096’s face, which seemed to soothe it
enough to move it to a nearby Foundation facility.

02:57:21.770 --> 02:57:26.230
Little did they know, they’d just obtained
one of the deadliest SCPs of all time, and

02:57:26.230 --> 02:57:30.480
while it may have been under lock and key
for now, it seems inevitable that it would

02:57:30.480 --> 02:57:33.700
get out and cause more violence and chaos.

02:57:33.700 --> 02:57:38.040
Research and containment procedures for the
SCP - 096 were put under the command of Dr.

02:57:38.040 --> 02:57:42.620
Dan, a senior researcher at the site. It was
his job to find out exactly what this being

02:57:42.620 --> 02:57:47.570
was capable of, and the more he tested, the
more he realized that they were dealing with

02:57:47.570 --> 02:57:50.070
something truly terrifying.

02:57:50.070 --> 02:57:53.870
Disposable D-Class personnel were used to
figure out exactly what it was that caused

02:57:53.870 --> 02:57:58.950
the creature to enter its attack mode. Just
as it had during the initial retrieval mission,

02:57:58.950 --> 02:58:04.600
SCP - 096 went berserk when any of the attending
personnel saw its face. In this stage, it

02:58:04.600 --> 02:58:10.160
would enter a period of considerable and unstoppable
distress for one to two minutes - covering

02:58:10.160 --> 02:58:15.130
its face and wailing loudly. When the period
of distress ended, the creature would mercilessly

02:58:15.130 --> 02:58:20.670
slaughter every D-Class that had seen its
face, and just like with Zulu 9-A, no trace

02:58:20.670 --> 02:58:23.290
of their bodies would be left behind.

02:58:23.290 --> 02:58:28.190
Dr. Dan was horrified and intrigued by this
phenomenon. The creature killed anyone that

02:58:28.190 --> 02:58:32.900
saw its face directly, but could the same
be said for indirect depictions of the creature’s

02:58:32.900 --> 02:58:38.150
face - such as images and videos? Dr. Dan
was desperate to find out.

02:58:38.150 --> 02:58:42.780
More D-Class personnel were brought in to
test this, to frightening results. Dr. Dan

02:58:42.780 --> 02:58:47.440
found that the creature did indeed still enter
attack mode when people saw pictures and videos

02:58:47.440 --> 02:58:52.620
of SCP - 096’s face. The creature seemed
to have an innate sense of when people were

02:58:52.620 --> 02:58:57.130
viewing these representations even when it
should have had no conceivable way of knowing.

02:58:57.130 --> 02:59:01.200
It didn’t matter how far away or how many
barriers were in place between the viewer

02:59:01.200 --> 02:59:06.660
and the creature, the attack mode would still
activate. And once it did, it seemed as though

02:59:06.660 --> 02:59:11.350
nothing could stop the creature from hunting
down the one who saw its face.

02:59:11.350 --> 02:59:15.300
With all of this new data, special containment
procedures were devised to keep the creature

02:59:15.300 --> 02:59:22.391
safely under lock and key. Its cell was a
5 m x 5 m x 5 m airtight steel cube, fitted

02:59:22.391 --> 02:59:28.570
with advanced pressure sensors and laser detectors
to ensure that SCP - 096 remained in its cell

02:59:28.570 --> 02:59:33.200
without risking anyone having visual contact
with the creature’s face. All cameras and

02:59:33.200 --> 02:59:37.340
video equipment were strictly forbidden, and
weekly checks for any cracks or holes in the

02:59:37.340 --> 02:59:42.160
containment cell were mandatory. Of course,
none of this would stop the creature if anyone

02:59:42.160 --> 02:59:47.740
somehow saw its face. In order to solve that
little problem, Dr. Dan would need to continue

02:59:47.740 --> 02:59:49.730
his research.

02:59:49.730 --> 02:59:54.140
To find a method of subverting the creature’s
deadly glance, they needed to know exactly

02:59:54.140 --> 02:59:59.170
what they were dealing with - but how could
they, when even a glance at a photo or video

02:59:59.170 --> 03:00:04.660
of the being meant certain death? A potential
solution was proposed - creating an artistic

03:00:04.660 --> 03:00:09.080
representation of the creature’s face, something
that hadn’t yet been attempted. But how

03:00:09.080 --> 03:00:14.150
would they achieve such a feat? Simple - they’d
procure a D-Class prisoner with some artistic

03:00:14.150 --> 03:00:18.410
talent, and they found one who had been a
tattoo artist before becoming a Foundation

03:00:18.410 --> 03:00:19.410
guinea pig.

03:00:19.410 --> 03:00:25.011
Dr. Dan formulated an ingenious plan for keeping
this D-Class alive for long enough to accurately

03:00:25.011 --> 03:00:31.080
draw an image of SCP - 096’s face: He would
be placed in a Bathysphere diving bell several

03:00:31.080 --> 03:00:36.200
kilometers underwater, and tens of kilometers
away from the containment cell where the SCP

03:00:36.200 --> 03:00:40.561
was being held. The D-Class was made to look
at a photograph of the creature’s face,

03:00:40.561 --> 03:00:45.860
and then replicate that image in a pencil
sketch. Dr. Dan first confirmed that the creature’s

03:00:45.860 --> 03:00:50.360
attack mode is only activated by the creature’s
face by having the D-Class look at a series

03:00:50.360 --> 03:00:56.270
of photos of the SCP’s body parts one by
one, finally finishing with its face. The

03:00:56.270 --> 03:01:01.311
D-Class began drawing and even remarked on
how creepy the SCP’s facial features were,

03:01:01.311 --> 03:01:03.750
despite not knowing the deadly context.

03:01:03.750 --> 03:01:09.820
Meanwhile, back in its containment cell SCP
- 096 sensed someone viewing its face, and

03:01:09.820 --> 03:01:14.440
entered its inconsolable crying state followed
by its attack mode. It broke out of containment

03:01:14.440 --> 03:01:19.170
easily, and began making a beeline for the
D-Class, traversing the miles between it and

03:01:19.170 --> 03:01:23.910
its prey. The D-Class didn’t know it as
he locked the finished drawing into a separate,

03:01:23.910 --> 03:01:28.271
autonomous submersible, but he was already
dead. As the drawing made its way up to a

03:01:28.271 --> 03:01:33.880
researcher on the surface, SCP - 096 dived
into the water, and started swimming down

03:01:33.880 --> 03:01:40.830
towards the artist. Minutes later, the bathysphere
was breached, and the D-Class was torn to

03:01:40.830 --> 03:01:41.830
shreds.

03:01:41.830 --> 03:01:47.730
SCP - 096 was recaptured without issue by
surface recovery team Foxtrot-303-A, and further

03:01:47.730 --> 03:01:52.430
testing on the drawing showed that artistic
representations of SCP - 096’s face were

03:01:52.430 --> 03:01:57.330
in fact harmless. From this experience, we
now know that the creature has a gaunt face

03:01:57.330 --> 03:02:03.380
with totally white eyes, possibly indicating
blindness, and a grossly extended jaw. Nevertheless,

03:02:03.380 --> 03:02:08.680
Dr. Dan was adamant that SCP - 096 was too
dangerous to be left alive, and requested

03:02:08.680 --> 03:02:12.670
permission from the upper echelons of the
Foundation to terminate the creature by any

03:02:12.670 --> 03:02:18.410
means necessary. However, the doctor’s request
would fall on deaf ears, until...

03:02:18.410 --> 03:02:22.351
It all started with a seemingly innocent image.
(Use this image: http://scp-wiki.wdfiles.com/local--files/incident-096-1-a/G9zmJ.jpg)

03:02:22.351 --> 03:02:26.450
While it’s now been redacted for your safety,
the black spec inside the yellow circle was

03:02:26.450 --> 03:02:33.660
once a minuscule image of SCP - 096, taken
unknowingly in the 1990s by a semi-professional

03:02:33.660 --> 03:02:37.101
mountaineer. One day, they were looking at
old photographs when his eyes passed over

03:02:37.101 --> 03:02:42.580
the tiny spec without even noticing he had
seen anything. But SCP - 096 noticed, and

03:02:42.580 --> 03:02:45.730
began entering its attack mode.

03:02:45.730 --> 03:02:49.540
It tore through its steel containment unit
like tissue paper, causing the release of

03:02:49.540 --> 03:02:53.830
a nerve agent that killed a number of attending
Foundation staff. The monster then fled the

03:02:53.830 --> 03:02:59.410
base and began pursuing its prey, with Mobile
Task Force Tau-1 in hot pursuit. Dr. Oleksei,

03:02:59.410 --> 03:03:04.060
who was helping to manage the site where the
SCP was contained, was in dismay over the

03:03:04.060 --> 03:03:07.780
situation. Dr. Dan was out of the country
at the time, trying to discover more about

03:03:07.780 --> 03:03:12.310
the creature’s origins. However, he did
leave the Mobile Task Force with a new secret

03:03:12.310 --> 03:03:16.530
weapon against the rampaging Shy Guy… Project
SCRAMBLE.

03:03:16.530 --> 03:03:20.760
SCRAMBLE were state of the art goggles featuring
a new technology created by Dr. Dan, which

03:03:20.760 --> 03:03:25.891
- using artistic renditions of 096’s facial
features - could detect and scramble the features

03:03:25.891 --> 03:03:31.830
of SCP - 096 into an unrecognizable form,
preventing the normally deadly effect of gazing

03:03:31.830 --> 03:03:38.370
upon on its face. In theory, this would allow
MTF Tau-1 to engage safely with 096 once its

03:03:38.370 --> 03:03:43.490
prey had been eliminated and bring it back
into containment. But disaster struck on two

03:03:43.490 --> 03:03:48.550
fronts. First, the prey in question was located
in a population center, creating the potential

03:03:48.550 --> 03:03:50.140
for a huge loss of life.

03:03:50.140 --> 03:03:54.691
And the second bigger problem was that the
SCRAMBLE technology didn’t work, as stray

03:03:54.691 --> 03:03:58.490
pixels of the creature’s face would reach
the eyes of the task force before the internal

03:03:58.490 --> 03:04:03.320
microprocessor had time to scramble them.
The mission turned into a death sentence,

03:04:03.320 --> 03:04:08.710
as SCP - 096 slaughtered almost the entire
task force, as well as a number of civilians

03:04:08.710 --> 03:04:14.730
in town - including an infant and its entire
family. It was a monumental disaster, made

03:04:14.730 --> 03:04:19.910
even worse by a final revelation: Dr. Dan
and Dr. Oleksei had themselves facilitated

03:04:19.910 --> 03:04:24.910
the entire containment breach and allowed
the resulting massacre to happen, with Dr.

03:04:24.910 --> 03:04:29.250
Dan hoping it would be enough motivation for
Foundation Command to green-light his research

03:04:29.250 --> 03:04:31.641
into destroying the creature.

03:04:31.641 --> 03:04:35.920
Anything that would give him the opportunity
to kill this thing would be worth the bloodshed.

03:04:35.920 --> 03:04:41.080
His plan worked and the SCP Foundation saw
it his way, approving his request to neutralize

03:04:41.080 --> 03:04:48.230
SCP - 096. However, success comes at a cost
for Dr. Dan. Once he figures out a way to

03:04:48.230 --> 03:04:53.580
finally kill the creature, though done in
the line of duty, he himself will be terminated

03:04:53.580 --> 03:04:56.250
by the Foundation for his crimes against humanity.

03:04:56.250 --> 03:05:01.650
But considering how much damage SCP - 096
is capable of causing if it ever got to a

03:05:01.650 --> 03:05:06.730
major population center, or - even worse - was
ever caught on camera and broadcast to a worldwide

03:05:06.730 --> 03:05:12.350
audience, the doctor himself would likely
deem his own death a justifiable cost. To

03:05:12.350 --> 03:05:16.360
this day, the Foundation is researching ways
to kill the creature, and they’re still

03:05:16.360 --> 03:05:21.470
looking for their silver bullet. And the pressure
is on. They hadn’t known about the seemingly

03:05:21.470 --> 03:05:26.560
innocent picture that sparked the last containment
breach. The one taken decades ago, in which

03:05:26.560 --> 03:05:32.650
the Shy Guy had only occupied four tiny pixels.
Four tiny pixels that resulted in multiple

03:05:32.650 --> 03:05:35.360
innocent lives lost.

03:05:35.360 --> 03:05:39.980
So be careful where you look because who knows
how many other photos of the creature are

03:05:39.980 --> 03:05:44.600
lurking out there, photos with an innocent
dot in the background. Your eyes glance over

03:05:44.600 --> 03:05:50.170
it, not even noticing the little blip, until
you hear a distant wailing that seems to be

03:05:50.170 --> 03:05:57.351
getting closer, and closer, and closer. And
then... it’s already too late.

